window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("james", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"James","chapters":"5","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Faith That Works","paragraphs":["Picture a church that says all the right things. Its members know the creed by heart — God is one, Jesus is Lord, salvation is by faith. They sing, they pray, they gather. But walk out of the meeting with them and watch what happens. A poor man in shabby clothes is shown to the floor while a rich man in gold rings is shown to the best seat. A hungry sister is sent away with a blessing and an empty stomach. Tongues that praised God on Sunday curse people on Monday. Members fight over money and position. Business plans are made without a thought of God. The faith is real on their lips and absent in their lives.","To that church James writes the most practical letter in the New Testament. It has 108 verses and more than fifty commands. It reads less like a theological argument and more like a series of sharp, short sermons — on trials, temptation, hearing and doing, favouritism, faith and works, the tongue, wisdom, quarrels, pride, planning, wealth, patience and prayer. Its style is close to Proverbs and to the Sermon on the Mount, and it echoes Jesus' own teaching on almost every page, though it quotes Him directly only by allusion.","The letter's central tension is simple and searching: does your faith actually work? James is not interested in a faith that only talks. He asks, “What good is it, my brothers, if a man says he has faith, but has no works? Can faith save him?” (James 2:14). And he answers bluntly: “Even so faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself” (James 2:17). Real faith survives trials, resists temptation, obeys the word, loves the poor, controls the tongue, chooses heavenly wisdom, submits to God, waits for the Lord and prays.","The writer calls himself simply “James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ” (James 1:1). The strongest reading, held from the early church onward, is that he is James the brother of Jesus, the leader of the Jerusalem church. He did not believe in Jesus during His ministry — “For even his brothers didn’t believe in him” (John 7:5) — but the risen Christ appeared to him: “Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles” (1 Corinthians 15:7). The unbelieving brother became a pillar of the church. Tradition, preserved by the historian Eusebius, called him ‘James the Just’ and said his knees were hard like a camel's from long hours of prayer. The Jewish historian Josephus records that he was stoned in Jerusalem around AD 62. When a man like that writes about faith that works, he writes as one whose own faith was tested and proved."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"Hebrews","intro":"Hebrews ended by pointing ahead to James and 1 Peter. James stands first among the letters written to the whole scattered church, and the Holy Spirit has placed it right after Hebrews for good reason: Hebrews showed us who Jesus is and called us to hold fast; James shows us what holding fast looks like on an ordinary Tuesday.","points":[{"title":"From ‘You Need Endurance’ to ‘Let Endurance Have Its Perfect Work’","body":"Hebrews told weary believers, “For you need endurance so that, having done the will of God, you may receive the promise” (Hebrews 10:36). James begins exactly there: “knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance” (James 1:3), and “Let endurance have its perfect work, that you may be perfect and complete, lacking in nothing” (James 1:4). The same Greek word, ὑπομονή — remaining under a load — links the two letters. Hebrews told you to run; James tells you what the race will feel like and why it is worth it."},{"title":"From the Heroes of Faith to Faith That Is Seen","body":"Hebrews 11 celebrated faith that acted: “By faith, Abraham, being tested, offered up Isaac” (Hebrews 11:17), and “By faith, Rahab the prostitute, didn’t perish with those who were disobedient, having received the spies in peace” (Hebrews 11:31). James picks the very same two people to make his point: “Wasn’t Abraham our father justified by works, in that he offered up Isaac his son on the altar?” (James 2:21) and “wasn’t Rahab the prostitute also justified by works, in that she received the messengers, and sent them out another way?” (James 2:25). Both letters agree: faith that is real always does something."},{"title":"From ‘Doing Good and Sharing’ to ‘Pure Religion’","body":"Hebrews closed with practical worship: “But don’t forget to be doing good and sharing, for with such sacrifices God is well pleased” (Hebrews 13:16). James turns that into a definition: “Pure religion and undefiled before our God and Father is this: to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep oneself unstained by the world” (James 1:27). Hebrews showed the altar outside the camp; James shows the widow's door down the street."},{"title":"From an Unknown Preacher to the Lord's Brother","body":"Hebrews named no author. James names himself, though only as a servant. He grew up in the same house as Jesus — “Isn’t his mother called Mary, and his brothers, James, Joses, Simon, and Judas?” (Matthew 13:55) — and yet he never calls himself the Lord's brother. Others did: Paul writes, “But of the other apostles I saw no one, except James, the Lord’s brother” (Galatians 1:19). He presided at the Jerusalem council (Acts 15:13) and was counted among those “who were reputed to be pillars” (Galatians 2:9). His humility is itself a lesson: the man with the greatest family claim on Jesus called himself His slave."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"Faith That Works","anchorRef":"James 1:22; 2:17; 2:26","paragraphs":["Every section of James orbits one conviction: true faith is never alone. It shows itself in what you do. “But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves” (James 1:22). “Even so faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself” (James 2:17). “For as the body apart from the spirit is dead, even so faith apart from works is dead” (James 2:26). A body without breath is a corpse, however well dressed. A faith without works is the same.","This is the teaching of Jesus Himself. He ended the Sermon on the Mount with two builders: “Everyone therefore who hears these words of mine, and does them, I will liken him to a wise man, who built his house on a rock” (Matthew 7:24), and “Everyone who hears these words of mine, and doesn’t do them will be like a foolish man, who built his house on the sand” (Matthew 7:26). James is that closing parable expanded into a whole letter. Its echoes of the Sermon are everywhere — joy in trials, the poor who are rich, mercy, judging, treasures that rust, and the plain ‘yes’ and ‘no’ (compare Matthew 5:37 with James 5:12).","Does James contradict Paul? Paul wrote, “We maintain therefore that a man is justified by faith apart from the works of the law” (Romans 3:28). James writes, “You see then that by works, a man is justified, and not only by faith” (James 2:24). Martin Luther famously struggled with James and once called it ‘an epistle of straw’ compared with Paul's letters. But the two apostles are fighting different enemies. Paul opposes people who think they can earn God's acceptance by keeping the law; James opposes people who think a faith that does nothing can save. Paul speaks of how a sinner is declared righteous; James speaks of how that righteousness is shown to be real. Paul himself says the same thing in his own words: what counts is “faith working through love” (Galatians 5:6), for “we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works” (Ephesians 2:10).","The rest of the New Testament says the same. John writes that love must be shown “in deed and truth” (1 John 3:18), and Jesus said, “If you know these things, blessed are you if you do them” (John 13:17). The church in every age has needed James whenever its faith became talk. He does not replace grace; he tests whether grace has actually arrived."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 5 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"Trials Are Not Wasted","body":"James begins with a startling command: “Count it all joy, my brothers, when you fall into various temptations” (James 1:2). Not because pain is good, but because God uses testing to produce endurance, and endurance to make you “perfect and complete, lacking in nothing” (James 1:4). The same trial that can make you approved can, if your own desire takes hold of it, become a temptation to sin. James teaches you to tell the difference and to bring every trial to the God who “gives to all liberally” (James 1:5)."},{"title":"Real Faith Is Visible","body":"James will not let faith hide in the heart or the creed. It must be seen — in how you treat the poor, how you use your tongue, how you handle money, how you speak about others, how you plan your days, and how you pray. “Show me your faith without works, and I by my works will show you my faith” (James 2:18). The question of this letter is not ‘Do you believe?’ but ‘What has your believing done?’"},{"title":"Humility Draws Grace","body":"Quarrels, favouritism, boasting and worldliness all spring from pride. James calls you back to the lowly place: “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble” (James 4:6), and “Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he will exalt you” (James 4:10). The letter that begins with the brother of Jesus calling himself a servant ends with ordinary believers praying, confessing and turning one another back to God."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Where Is the Gap?","body":"Every believer has a gap between what they say they believe and how they actually live. James will put his finger on it. Before you begin, ask the Holy Spirit honestly: Where is the gap in my life — in my words, my money, my attitude to people, my prayer? Write down one area, and read James with that area open before God."},{"title":"What Trial Are You In?","body":"James was written to people under pressure. Name the trial you are facing right now — a loss, a delay, a temptation, a hardship, a difficult person. As you read, look for how God wants to use it to make you complete, rather than how you can simply escape it."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, as I open the letter of James, I come to You not only as a hearer but as one who wants to do Your word. You know the gap between what I say I believe and how I live. Search me, and show me where my faith has become only talk. Teach me to count my trials as joy, because You are using them to make me complete. When I lack wisdom, I ask You, the God who gives to all liberally and without reproach, and I ask in faith, without doubting. Guard my tongue. Free me from favouritism and from the love of money. Humble my pride, and give me the grace You give to the humble. Let my faith be seen in my works — in my care for the poor, my words, my plans and my prayers — so that my life is a living proof that Jesus Christ is Lord. Like James, who once did not believe and then became Your servant, make me faithful to the end. In Jesus' name, Amen.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"James","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Trials, Temptation, and the Word That Must Be Done","themeTitle":"“Be Doers of the Word, and Not Only Hearers”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are in the Dispersion: Greetings.","understanding":"Ἰάκωβος θεοῦ καὶ κυρίου Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ δοῦλος, “James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ” — the brother of Jesus calls himself His δοῦλος, a slave, from the servant family: not a hired helper (μισθωτός) or an attendant (διάκονος) but someone wholly owned. He puts God and the Lord Jesus Christ side by side as the one Master he serves. “to the twelve tribes which are in the Dispersion” — διασπορά, the scattering: Jewish believers living outside the land, and through them the whole church as God's people in exile.","words":[{"strongs":"G2385","hebrew":"Ἰάκωβος","translit":"Iákōbos","english":"James"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribes"},{"strongs":"G1290","hebrew":"διασπορά","translit":"diasporá","english":"Dispersion"},{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"Greetings"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Count it all joy, my brothers, when you fall into various temptations,","understanding":"πᾶσαν χαρὰν ἡγήσασθε, “Count it all joy” — χαρά echoes the greeting χαίρειν (v. 1): ‘Rejoice! … consider it all joy.’ ἡγέομαι means to reckon, to make a considered judgement — not to feel happy about pain, but to decide what trials are worth. πειρασμοῖς περιπέσητε ποικίλοις, “when you fall into various temptations” — πειρασμός, from the test family. Here it means outward trials that come upon you (περιπίπτω, to fall into something on every side), as the next verse shows; in verses 13–14 the same root will mean an inner pull to sin.","words":[{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"Count"},{"strongs":"G4045","hebrew":"περιπίπτω","translit":"peripíptō","english":"fall into"},{"strongs":"G4164","hebrew":"ποικίλος","translit":"poikílos","english":"various"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"temptations"}]},{"v":3,"text":"knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance.","understanding":"“knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance” — δοκίμιον, the second word of the test family: the assayer's proving of metal in fire to show it is genuine. πειρασμός is the trial; δοκίμιον is what the trial does to your faith — it proves it. κατεργάζεται, from the work family, means to work out fully, to produce a finished result. And what it produces is ὑπομονή, endurance — from the endurance family: ‘remaining under’ a load without giving way.","words":[{"strongs":"G1383","hebrew":"δοκίμιον","translit":"dokímion","english":"testing"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2716","hebrew":"κατεργάζομαι","translit":"katergázomai","english":"produces"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"endurance"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Let endurance have its perfect work, that you may be perfect and complete, lacking in nothing.","understanding":"ἡ δὲ ὑπομονὴ ἔργον τέλειον ἐχέτω, “Let endurance have its perfect work” — ὑπομονή, from the endurance family, must be allowed to finish its ἔργον, from the work family: do not cut the process short by escaping the trial too soon. Two words from the perfect family follow: τέλειος, mature, having reached its goal, and ὁλόκληρος, whole in every part, nothing missing. “lacking in nothing” — λείπω, to fall short; verse 5 will pick up the same word.","words":[{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"endurance"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G3648","hebrew":"ὁλόκληρος","translit":"holóklēros","english":"complete"},{"strongs":"G3007","hebrew":"λείπω","translit":"leípō","english":"lacking"}]},{"v":5,"text":"But if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach; and it will be given to him.","understanding":"εἰ δέ τις ὑμῶν λείπεται σοφίας, “But if any of you lacks wisdom” — the same verb as verse 4: endurance makes you lack nothing, but in the middle of the trial you will lack σοφία, from the wisdom family: the skill to see and live it God's way. αἰτείτω, from the pray family: to ask as a child asks a father. God gives ἁπλῶς — simply, generously, with an undivided heart — and μὴ ὀνειδίζοντος, without scolding you for asking.","words":[{"strongs":"G3007","hebrew":"λείπω","translit":"leípō","english":"lacks"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G574","hebrew":"ἁπλῶς","translit":"haplōs","english":"liberally"},{"strongs":"G3679","hebrew":"ὀνειδίζω","translit":"oneidízō","english":"reproach"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gives"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But let him ask in faith, without any doubting, for he who doubts is like a wave of the sea, driven by the wind and tossed.","understanding":"αἰτείτω δὲ ἐν πίστει μηδὲν διακρινόμενος, “But let him ask in faith, without any doubting” — αἰτέω again, from the pray family; διακρίνομαι, to be divided against yourself, arguing both ways. The picture is κλύδων θαλάσσης, the surge of the sea, driven (ἀνεμίζω) and tossed (ῥιπίζω) — never still, going wherever the wind pushes it.","words":[{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"doubting"},{"strongs":"G2830","hebrew":"κλύδων","translit":"klýdōn","english":"wave"},{"strongs":"G416","hebrew":"ἀνεμίζω","translit":"anemízō","english":"driven by the wind"},{"strongs":"G4494","hebrew":"ῥιπίζω","translit":"rhipízō","english":"tossed"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For that man shouldn’t think that he will receive anything from the Lord.","understanding":"“For that man shouldn’t think that he will receive anything from the Lord.” The problem is not that God is unwilling (v. 5), but that the doubter is not really asking God at all — he is asking and not asking at the same time. λαμβάνω, to receive, takes hold of what God gives with an open hand.","words":[{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":8,"text":"He is a double-minded man, unstable in all his ways.","understanding":"ἀνὴρ δίψυχος, “He is a double-minded man” — δίψυχος, literally ‘two-souled’, a word James may have coined; he uses it again in 4:8. One soul leans towards God, the other towards the world. ἀκατάστατος, “unstable” — unsettled, restless, the same root as the ‘confusion’ of 3:16. A divided heart makes a divided life: “in all his ways.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1374","hebrew":"δίψυχος","translit":"dípsychos","english":"double-minded"},{"strongs":"G182","hebrew":"ἀκατάστατος","translit":"akatástatos","english":"unstable"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"ways"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But let the brother in humble circumstances glory in his high position;","understanding":"“But let the brother in humble circumstances glory in his high position” — ταπεινός, from the humble family: low, poor, of small account. καυχάσθω, let him boast — not in himself but in his ὕψος, his height: in Christ he is a child of God and an heir of the kingdom (2:5). Poverty is the first trial James names.","words":[{"strongs":"G5011","hebrew":"ταπεινός","translit":"tapeinós","english":"humble circumstances"},{"strongs":"G2744","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5311","hebrew":"ὕψος","translit":"hýpsos","english":"high position"}]},{"v":10,"text":"and the rich, in that he is made humble, because like the flower in the grass, he will pass away.","understanding":"“and the rich, in that he is made humble” — ταπείνωσις, from the humble family: lowering. The rich believer must boast in what humbles him: before God his wealth counts for nothing, and his life is short. ὡς ἄνθος χόρτου παρελεύσεται, “because like the flower in the grass, he will pass away” — an image from Isaiah 40, which Peter also quotes (1 Peter 1:24).","words":[{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G5014","hebrew":"ταπείνωσις","translit":"tapeínōsis","english":"made humble"},{"strongs":"G438","hebrew":"ἄνθος","translit":"ánthos","english":"flower"},{"strongs":"G5528","hebrew":"χόρτος","translit":"chórtos","english":"grass"},{"strongs":"G3928","hebrew":"παρέρχομαι","translit":"parérchomai","english":"pass away"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For the sun arises with the scorching wind, and withers the grass, and the flower in it falls, and the beauty of its appearance perishes. So also will the rich man fade away in his pursuits.","understanding":"“For the sun arises with the scorching wind, and withers the grass” — καύσων may be the burning heat or the hot east wind of Palestine that can wither fields in a day. εὐπρέπεια, the beauty or grace of its appearance, perishes. “So also will the rich man fade away in his pursuits” — μαραίνω, to wither, in the middle of his πορείαι, his business journeys. Death interrupts the rich in the middle of their plans (compare 4:13–14).","words":[{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G2742","hebrew":"καύσων","translit":"kaúsōn","english":"scorching wind"},{"strongs":"G3583","hebrew":"ξηραίνω","translit":"xēraínō","english":"withers"},{"strongs":"G2143","hebrew":"εὐπρέπεια","translit":"euprépeia","english":"beauty"},{"strongs":"G3133","hebrew":"μαραίνω","translit":"maraínō","english":"fade away"},{"strongs":"G4197","hebrew":"πορεία","translit":"poreía","english":"pursuits"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has been approved, he will receive the crown of life, which the Lord promised to those who love him.","understanding":"μακάριος ἀνὴρ ὃς ὑπομένει πειρασμόν, “Blessed is the man who endures temptation” — ὑπομένω, the verb of the endurance family; πειρασμός, the trial of verse 2. ὅτι δόκιμος γενόμενος, “for when he has been approved” — δόκιμος, the third word of the test family: what is left when the testing is over, proved genuine. The reward is τὸν στέφανον τῆς ζωῆς, “the crown of life” — the victor's wreath, which is life itself, from the life family: ζωή, the life God gives, not βίος, the means of living. It is promised “to those who love him” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family: endurance is love that will not let go. στέφανος is from the crown family — a wreath or garland — the victor’s prize, a mark of honour and reward.","words":[{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endures"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"temptation"},{"strongs":"G1384","hebrew":"δόκιμος","translit":"dókimos","english":"approved"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promised"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Let no man say when he is tempted, “I am tempted by God,” for God can’t be tempted by evil, and he himself tempts no one.","understanding":"Now the test family turns. μηδεὶς πειραζόμενος λεγέτω, “Let no man say when he is tempted” — πειράζω, here in its bad sense: enticed towards sin. “I am tempted by God” is the oldest excuse; Adam blamed ‘the woman whom you gave to be with me’. ὁ γὰρ θεὸς ἀπείραστός ἐστιν κακῶν, “for God can’t be tempted by evil” — ἀπείραστος, untemptable, a member of the test family used of God alone. κακός, from the evil family, is badness in itself. God may test (πειράζω in the good sense, as with Abraham), but “he himself tempts no one.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tempted"},{"strongs":"G551","hebrew":"ἀπείραστος","translit":"apeírastos","english":"can’t be tempted"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":14,"text":"But each one is tempted when he is drawn away by his own lust, and enticed.","understanding":"“But each one is tempted when he is drawn away by his own lust, and enticed.” πειράζω, from the test family, in its bad sense. Two hunting and fishing words: ἐξέλκω, to drag out, as a fish is drawn from its hiding place, and δελεάζω, to lure with bait. The bait works because of ἐπιθυμία, desire — “his own”. The trial comes from outside; the temptation finds its hook inside.","words":[{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tempted"},{"strongs":"G1828","hebrew":"ἐξέλκω","translit":"exélkō","english":"drawn away"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lust"},{"strongs":"G1185","hebrew":"δελεάζω","translit":"deleázō","english":"enticed"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Then the lust, when it has conceived, bears sin; and the sin, when it is full grown, produces death.","understanding":"“Then the lust, when it has conceived, bears sin” — a birth sequence, using two words from the birth family. Desire conceives (συλλαμβάνω), gives birth (τίκτω) to ἁμαρτία — sin, from the sin family: missing the mark. And sin, ἀποτελεσθεῖσα, “when it is full grown” (literally, brought to completion — the dark twin of the τέλειος of verse 4), ἀποκύει θάνατον, “produces death.” ἀποκυέω, to bring forth from the womb, is the second birth-family word; James uses it again in verse 18 for God giving birth to us: two families, two births, two outcomes.","words":[{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lust"},{"strongs":"G4815","hebrew":"συλλαμβάνω","translit":"syllambánō","english":"conceived"},{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"bears"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G658","hebrew":"ἀποτελέω","translit":"apoteléō","english":"full grown"},{"strongs":"G616","hebrew":"ἀποκυέω","translit":"apokyéō","english":"produces"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Don’t be deceived, my beloved brothers.","understanding":"μὴ πλανᾶσθε, “Don’t be deceived” — πλανάω, to wander off, to be led astray. The deception James means is the lie of verse 13: that God is the source of evil. ἀγαπητοί, “my beloved brothers” — from the love family: the people God loves. James corrects them with tenderness.","words":[{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of lights, with whom can be no variation, nor turning shadow.","understanding":"πᾶσα δόσις ἀγαθὴ καὶ πᾶν δώρημα τέλειον, “Every good gift and every perfect gift” — two words from the gift family in one line: δόσις, the act of giving, and δώρημα, the thing given. Both the way God gives and what He gives are good (ἀγαθός, from the good family) and complete (τέλειος, the perfect family again). ἄνωθεν, “from above.” He is “the Father of lights” — Creator of the sun, moon and stars — but unlike them “with whom can be no variation, nor turning shadow”: παραλλαγή, change, and τροπή, turning, with ἀποσκίασμα, the shadow cast by turning. The heavenly lights move and wane; their Maker never does.","words":[{"strongs":"G1394","hebrew":"δόσις","translit":"dósis","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1434","hebrew":"δώρημα","translit":"dṓrēma","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G509","hebrew":"ἄνωθεν","translit":"ánōthen","english":"from above"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"lights"},{"strongs":"G3883","hebrew":"παραλλαγή","translit":"parallagḗ","english":"variation"},{"strongs":"G5157","hebrew":"τροπή","translit":"tropḗ","english":"turning"},{"strongs":"G644","hebrew":"ἀποσκίασμα","translit":"aposkíasma","english":"shadow"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Of his own will he gave birth to us by the word of truth, that we should be a kind of first fruits of his creatures.","understanding":"βουληθεὶς ἀπεκύησεν ἡμᾶς λόγῳ ἀληθείας, “Of his own will he gave birth to us by the word of truth” — ἀποκυέω, from the birth family, the same verb as verse 15. Sin gives birth to death; God, by His own decision, gives birth to children. The means is λόγος, from the word family: the message of the gospel, the whole truth God has spoken, rather than ῥῆμα, a particular saying. ἀπαρχή, “first fruits” — the first sheaf of the harvest given to God, the promise of more to come.","words":[{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G616","hebrew":"ἀποκυέω","translit":"apokyéō","english":"gave birth"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G536","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","english":"first fruits"},{"strongs":"G2938","hebrew":"κτίσμα","translit":"ktísma","english":"creatures"}]},{"v":19,"text":"So, then, my beloved brothers, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to anger;","understanding":"“let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to anger” — ταχύς, quick; βραδύς, slow. James will develop all three: hearing (vv. 21–25), speaking (3:1–12), and anger (4:1–2). ὀργή is a settled, smouldering anger, not a passing flash. ἀγαπητοί, “beloved”, from the love family, frames even this rebuke. ὀργή is from the wrath family — wrath — settled, purposeful anger against evil that leads to judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G5036","hebrew":"ταχύς","translit":"tachýs","english":"swift"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G1021","hebrew":"βραδύς","translit":"bradýs","english":"slow"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"anger"}]},{"v":20,"text":"for the anger of man doesn’t produce the righteousness of God.","understanding":"“for the anger of man doesn’t produce the righteousness of God.” ἐργάζεται, from the work family: human anger cannot work out the right living God desires (δικαιοσύνη). Compare verse 3, where testing produces (κατεργάζεται) endurance: one work family member is fruitful, the other barren. ὀργή is from the wrath family — wrath — settled, purposeful anger against evil that leads to judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"anger"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"produce"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Therefore, putting away all filthiness and overflowing of wickedness, receive with humility the implanted word, which is able to save your souls.","understanding":"“Therefore, putting away all filthiness and overflowing of wickedness” — ἀποθέμενοι, taking off like dirty clothes. ῥυπαρία, filthiness, is moral grime; κακία, from the evil family, is malice, ill-will towards others — the vice of the heart behind evil acts. ἐν πραΰτητι, “with humility” — πραΰτης, from the humble family: meekness, teachableness, rather than ταπεινοφροσύνη, lowliness of mind — δέξασθε τὸν ἔμφυτον λόγον, “receive … the implanted word” — λόγος, from the word family, and ἔμφυτος, planted in, like a seed rooted in the soil. The word is already in you; welcome it. It “is able to save your souls” — σῴζω, from the heal family: to rescue and make whole; ψυχή, from the life family: your whole self.","words":[{"strongs":"G659","hebrew":"ἀποτίθημι","translit":"apotíthēmi","english":"putting away"},{"strongs":"G4507","hebrew":"ῥυπαρία","translit":"rhyparía","english":"filthiness"},{"strongs":"G4050","hebrew":"περισσεία","translit":"perisseía","english":"overflowing"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"wickedness"},{"strongs":"G4240","hebrew":"πραΰτης","translit":"praÿtēs","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G1721","hebrew":"ἔμφυτος","translit":"émphytos","english":"implanted"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"}]},{"v":22,"text":"But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves.","understanding":"γίνεσθε δὲ ποιηταὶ λόγου, “But be doers of the word” — λόγος, from the word family, the message planted in you (v. 21); ποιητής, a doer, a maker; our word ‘poet’ comes from it. καὶ μὴ μόνον ἀκροαταί, “and not only hearers” — ἀκροατής, a mere listener, an audience member. Hearing without doing is not neutral: παραλογιζόμενοι ἑαυτούς, “deluding your own selves” — reasoning falsely, miscalculating your own spiritual condition.","words":[{"strongs":"G4163","hebrew":"ποιητής","translit":"poiētḗs","english":"doers"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G202","hebrew":"ἀκροατής","translit":"akroatḗs","english":"hearers"},{"strongs":"G3884","hebrew":"παραλογίζομαι","translit":"paralogízomai","english":"deluding"}]},{"v":23,"text":"For if anyone is a hearer of the word and not a doer, he is like a man looking at his natural face in a mirror;","understanding":"“he is like a man looking at his natural face in a mirror” — κατανοέω, from the see family: to look carefully, to take note. He does look properly. τὸ πρόσωπον τῆς γενέσεως αὐτοῦ, literally ‘the face of his birth’ — the face he was born with. ἔσοπτρον, a mirror of polished bronze, giving a dim but real picture.","words":[{"strongs":"G202","hebrew":"ἀκροατής","translit":"akroatḗs","english":"hearer"},{"strongs":"G4163","hebrew":"ποιητής","translit":"poiētḗs","english":"doer"},{"strongs":"G2657","hebrew":"κατανοέω","translit":"katanoéō","english":"looking"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G1078","hebrew":"γένεσις","translit":"génesis","english":"natural"},{"strongs":"G2072","hebrew":"ἔσοπτρον","translit":"ésoptron","english":"mirror"}]},{"v":24,"text":"for he sees himself, and goes away, and immediately forgets what kind of man he was.","understanding":"“for he sees himself, and goes away, and immediately forgets what kind of man he was.” κατενόησεν again, from the see family — he saw clearly. The failure is not in the seeing but in the walking away. ἐπιλανθάνομαι, to let slip out of the mind. A sermon heard and not done is a face seen and forgotten.","words":[{"strongs":"G2657","hebrew":"κατανοέω","translit":"katanoéō","english":"sees"},{"strongs":"G1950","hebrew":"ἐπιλανθάνομαι","translit":"epilanthánomai","english":"forgets"}]},{"v":25,"text":"But he who looks into the perfect law of freedom, and continues, not being a hearer who forgets, but a doer of the work, this man will be blessed in what he does.","understanding":"ὁ δὲ παρακύψας εἰς νόμον τέλειον, “But he who looks into the perfect law of freedom” — παρακύπτω, a second member of the see family: to stoop down and peer in, as the disciples bent to look into the empty tomb (John 20:5). The glance of verse 24 becomes the bent-over gaze. The law is τέλειος, from the perfect family: perfect, and ἐλευθερία, freedom — the word of the gospel that sets free and teaches free people how to live. καὶ παραμείνας, “and continues” — stays with it. οὐκ ἀκροατὴς ἐπιλησμονῆς … ἀλλὰ ποιητὴς ἔργου, “not being a hearer who forgets, but a doer of the work” — ἔργον, the work family. He is μακάριος ἐν τῇ ποιήσει αὐτοῦ, blessed in the doing itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G3879","hebrew":"παρακύπτω","translit":"parakýptō","english":"looks"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G1657","hebrew":"ἐλευθερία","translit":"eleuthería","english":"freedom"},{"strongs":"G3887","hebrew":"παραμένω","translit":"paraménō","english":"continues"},{"strongs":"G1953","hebrew":"ἐπιλησμονή","translit":"epilēsmonḗ","english":"forgets"},{"strongs":"G4163","hebrew":"ποιητής","translit":"poiētḗs","english":"doer"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G4162","hebrew":"ποίησις","translit":"poíēsis","english":"does"}]},{"v":26,"text":"If anyone among you thinks himself to be religious while he doesn’t bridle his tongue, but deceives his heart, this man’s religion is worthless.","understanding":"“If anyone among you thinks himself to be religious” — θρησκός, from the godliness family: outwardly devout, careful in worship. That is not εὐσεβής, the inner reverence of the godly; it is the external side of religion. Yet if he μὴ χαλιναγωγῶν γλῶσσαν, does “not bridle his tongue” — χαλιναγωγέω, to lead with a bit, as a horse — and so deceives his own heart (ἀπατάω), “this man’s religion is worthless” — μάταιος, empty, futile. James will take up the tongue fully in chapter 3.","words":[{"strongs":"G2357","hebrew":"θρησκός","translit":"thrēskós","english":"religious"},{"strongs":"G5468","hebrew":"χαλιναγωγέω","translit":"chalinagōgéō","english":"bridle"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G538","hebrew":"ἀπατάω","translit":"apatáō","english":"deceives"},{"strongs":"G2356","hebrew":"θρησκεία","translit":"thrēskeía","english":"religion"},{"strongs":"G3152","hebrew":"μάταιος","translit":"mátaios","english":"worthless"}]},{"v":27,"text":"Pure religion and undefiled before our God and Father is this: to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep oneself unstained by the world.","understanding":"θρησκεία καθαρὰ καὶ ἀμίαντος, “Pure religion and undefiled” — θρησκεία, from the godliness family, the same word as verse 26, now filled with right content. καθαρός, clean; ἀμίαντος, unstained (the word Hebrews uses of Jesus, Hebrews 7:26). Two marks: ἐπισκέπτεσθαι ὀρφανοὺς καὶ χήρας, “to visit the fatherless and widows” — ἐπισκέπτομαι, to look in on and care for, the word used of God visiting His people (Luke 1:68) — and ἄσπιλον ἑαυτὸν τηρεῖν ἀπὸ τοῦ κόσμου, “to keep oneself unstained by the world.” τηρέω, from the hold family: to guard carefully. κόσμος, from the world family: not the earth (γῆ) or the age (αἰών), but human society organised without God.","words":[{"strongs":"G2356","hebrew":"θρησκεία","translit":"thrēskeía","english":"religion"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"Pure"},{"strongs":"G283","hebrew":"ἀμίαντος","translit":"amíantos","english":"undefiled"},{"strongs":"G1980","hebrew":"ἐπισκέπτομαι","translit":"episképtomai","english":"visit"},{"strongs":"G3737","hebrew":"ὀρφανός","translit":"orphanós","english":"fatherless"},{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widows"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"affliction"},{"strongs":"G784","hebrew":"ἄσπιλος","translit":"áspilos","english":"unstained"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Refiner's Fire","verseRef":"v. 2-4, 12","image":"Picture a goldsmith bent over a small clay crucible set in glowing coals. The ore inside melts and bubbles; dark scum rises to the surface, and he skims it off. He does not leave the fire, and he does not take the crucible out too soon. Again and again he looks in, until at last he can see his own face reflected in the molten gold. Then he lifts it out — proven, pure, approved.","meaning":"“knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance” (v. 3). The word for testing is the assayer's word: the fire does not destroy genuine gold; it reveals it. “Let endurance have its perfect work, that you may be perfect and complete, lacking in nothing” (v. 4). And at the end, “when he has been approved, he will receive the crown of life” (v. 12). Your trial is not a sign that God has left the room. The Refiner is watching the crucible."},{"title":"The Wave and the Rock","verseRef":"v. 5-8","image":"Picture the sea on a stormy day. A wave rises, is pushed towards the shore, then sucked back, then driven sideways by a gust, never resting, never arriving. Now picture a rock standing in the surf. The same waves break on it, but it does not move.","meaning":"“But let him ask in faith, without any doubting, for he who doubts is like a wave of the sea, driven by the wind and tossed” (v. 6). The double-minded person is not someone with questions, but someone with two loyalties, asking God while keeping another option open. James calls him “unstable in all his ways” (v. 8). Faith is not the absence of storms; it is the decision about which way you face in them."},{"title":"The Bait and the Hook","verseRef":"v. 13-15","image":"Picture a fish in the shadow of a rock, safe. A fisherman drops a fat, wriggling worm into the water just beyond the shadow. The fish sees it, wants it, edges out, then darts and bites. Too late it feels the hook. It is dragged out of its hiding place, into the light, and onto the bank.","meaning":"“But each one is tempted when he is drawn away by his own lust, and enticed” (v. 14). The words are taken from fishing and hunting. The bait is always attractive; the hook is always hidden. And James traces the full life-cycle: desire conceives, gives birth to sin, and sin grows up and “produces death” (v. 15). The time to deal with temptation is before the bite, in the moment of wanting."},{"title":"The Mirror","verseRef":"v. 22-25","image":"Picture a man getting ready in the morning. He looks in the mirror and sees a smear of dirt across his cheek and his hair standing up. He nods, puts the mirror down, walks out of the door and goes to work exactly as he is. Then picture another man who looks in the same mirror, bends closer, keeps looking, and washes his face before he leaves.","meaning":"“For if anyone is a hearer of the word and not a doer, he is like a man looking at his natural face in a mirror” (v. 23). Scripture shows you yourself. The question is not whether you have looked, but what you did next. “But he who looks into the perfect law of freedom, and continues, not being a hearer who forgets, but a doer of the work, this man will be blessed in what he does” (v. 25)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting (v. 1)","body":"James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, greets the twelve tribes in the Dispersion — believers scattered among the nations."},{"label":"Trials, Wisdom and Faith (v. 2-8)","body":"Count trials as joy because testing produces endurance and endurance makes you complete; if you lack wisdom, ask God, who gives generously; ask in faith, without the divided heart that makes a person like a wave of the sea."},{"label":"Poor and Rich (v. 9-11)","body":"The lowly believer should boast in his exaltation and the rich in his humiliation, because the rich will fade like a flower in the scorching heat."},{"label":"Testing and Temptation (v. 12-18)","body":"The one who endures trial will receive the crown of life; temptation never comes from God but from our own desire, which conceives sin and brings death; every good and perfect gift comes from the unchanging Father of lights, who gave us new birth by the word of truth."},{"label":"Hearing and Doing (v. 19-27)","body":"Be quick to hear, slow to speak and slow to anger; put away filth and receive the implanted word; be doers of the word and not hearers only, like a man who looks in a mirror and forgets; true religion bridles the tongue, cares for orphans and widows, and keeps itself unstained by the world."}],"sections":[{"heading":"One Word, Two Directions: Trial and Temptation","paragraphs":["English Bibles divide what James holds together. The Greek word πειρασμός in verse 2 and πειράζω in verse 13 come from the same root. The WEB keeps the link by translating both with ‘temptation’ — “when you fall into various temptations” (v. 2) and “Let no man say when he is tempted, ‘I am tempted by God’” (v. 13) — while many translations use ‘trials’ in verse 2. The Greek word simply means a test. Whether it becomes a trial that proves your faith or a temptation that leads to sin depends on what you do with it.","James shows both directions. Upward: the trial is met with joy and faith, endurance grows, and the person becomes δόκιμος, approved — “Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has been approved, he will receive the crown of life, which the Lord promised to those who love him” (v. 12). Downward: the same pressure meets “his own lust” (v. 14), desire takes the bait, and a terrible birth follows: “Then the lust, when it has conceived, bears sin; and the sin, when it is full grown, produces death” (v. 15).","So who is responsible? Not God. “God can’t be tempted by evil, and he himself tempts no one” (v. 13). God may allow and even send trials — He tested Abraham (Hebrews 11:17) — but He never entices anyone to sin. Paul gives the same assurance: “God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted above what you are able, but will with the temptation also make the way of escape, that you may be able to endure it” (1 Corinthians 10:13). The source of temptation's power is inside us. The source of everything good is above us: “Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of lights, with whom can be no variation, nor turning shadow” (v. 17)."]},{"heading":"Wisdom for the Middle of the Trial","paragraphs":["Why does James move from trials (vv. 2–4) to wisdom (v. 5)? Because the hardest thing in a trial is not the pain but the confusion. You do not know what God is doing, or how to respond. James's answer is simple: ask. “But if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach; and it will be given to him” (v. 5). Wisdom in James is not cleverness but practical godliness — knowing how to live God's way in a real situation. Chapter 3 will describe it: “pure, then peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits” (James 3:17).","God's giving is described with two beautiful words. He gives ἁπλῶς — generously, with a single, undivided heart — and He does not scold. You will never hear God say, ‘You again? Why didn't you know this already?’ The only condition is on the side of the asker: “But let him ask in faith, without any doubting” (v. 6). The doubter here is not a person wrestling honestly with hard questions. He is δίψυχος, double-souled — asking God for wisdom while keeping his own plan in reserve, wanting God's answer only if it agrees with his. God's single-hearted giving meets a double-hearted asking, and nothing passes between them.","Jesus taught the same: “Ask, and it will be given you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and it will be opened for you” (Matthew 7:7). And He said, “if you have faith, and don’t doubt, you will not only do what was done to the fig tree” (Matthew 21:21). The cure for double-mindedness is not more effort but undivided trust: “Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you. Cleanse your hands, you sinners; and purify your hearts, you double-minded” (James 4:8)."]},{"heading":"The Word That Must Be Done","paragraphs":["The last section of the chapter introduces the theme of the whole letter. God has given us birth “by the word of truth” (v. 18). That word is now ἔμφυτος, “implanted” (v. 21), like a seed planted in good soil. The question is whether it will grow. James calls for a certain kind of listener: “let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to anger” (v. 19). Many church conflicts would end if this verse were obeyed. Anger has a reputation for getting things done, but James says it never does what God wants: “for the anger of man doesn’t produce the righteousness of God” (v. 20).","Then comes the great command: “But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves” (v. 22). Notice the danger. James does not say that hearers without doing are simply incomplete; he says they are deceived. They think that because they know the truth and enjoy hearing it, they are living it. The mirror shows them their face, they agree with what they see, and they walk away unchanged. The one who is blessed is the one who bends close to “the perfect law of freedom” (v. 25), keeps looking, and does what it says. God's word does not set you free from obedience; it sets you free to obey.","Finally, James tests religion. A person can be careful in worship and yet ruin it with his tongue (v. 26). True religion — the kind God the Father accepts — is measured by two things: compassion for the helpless, “to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction,” and purity before the world, “to keep oneself unstained by the world” (v. 27). Love and holiness, outward mercy and inward cleanness. The rest of the letter will apply both."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Joy in Trials, the Crown of Life, and the Doers of the Word","paragraphs":["James's teaching on trials is shared by Paul and Peter. Paul writes, “Not only this, but we also rejoice in our sufferings, knowing that suffering produces perseverance” (Romans 5:3), “and perseverance, proven character; and proven character, hope” (Romans 5:4). Peter uses James's very words: “Wherein you greatly rejoice, though now for a little while, if need be, you have been put to grief in various trials” (1 Peter 1:6), “that the proof of your faith, which is more precious than gold that perishes even though it is tested by fire, may be found to result in praise, glory, and honor at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (1 Peter 1:7). Three apostles, one pattern: trials, then proven faith, then endurance, then glory. All three are rooted in Jesus' beatitude: “Rejoice, and be exceedingly glad, for great is your reward in heaven” (Matthew 5:12).","The fading flower of verses 10–11 comes from Isaiah 40, and Peter quotes the passage: “For, ‘All flesh is like grass, and all of man’s glory like the flower in the grass. The grass withers, and its flower falls’” (1 Peter 1:24), “but the Lord’s word endures forever” (1 Peter 1:25). Peter then speaks of new birth by that word, exactly as James does in verse 18: “having been born again, not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible, through the word of God, which lives and remains forever” (1 Peter 1:23). The crown of life promised in verse 12 is promised again by the risen Christ to a suffering church: “Be faithful to death, and I will give you the crown of life” (Revelation 2:10).","And James's call to be doers of the word comes straight from Jesus. “Everyone therefore who hears these words of mine, and does them, I will liken him to a wise man, who built his house on a rock” (Matthew 7:24). “But he said, ‘On the contrary, blessed are those who hear the word of God, and keep it’” (Luke 11:28). “If you know these things, blessed are you if you do them” (John 13:17). Even Paul, the great preacher of grace, agrees: “For it isn’t the hearers of the law who are righteous before God, but the doers of the law will be justified” (Romans 2:13). James did not invent a religion of works. He was repeating his Brother."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Temptation / trial","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","strongs":"G3986 (verb πειράζω, peirázō, G3985; testing δοκίμιον, dokímion, G1383)","verses":"v. 2, 3, 12, 13, 14","insight":"Strong's: “a putting to proof.” πειρασμός is a test, and context decides its direction. In verses 2 and 12 it is an outward trial that God uses to prove faith; in verses 13–14 the verb πειράζω means an inner enticement to sin, which never comes from God. Its partner δοκίμιον (v. 3) is the assayer's testing of metal, and δόκιμος (v. 12) is the result — approved, genuine. This study adds all of them to a new test family. The lesson of the chapter turns on the difference: the same circumstance can make you approved or lead you into sin, depending on whether you meet it with faith or with desire."},{"english":"Endurance","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","strongs":"G5281 (verb ὑπομένω, hypoménō, G5278)","verses":"v. 3, 4, 12","insight":"Strong's: “cheerful (or hopeful) endurance, constancy.” Literally ‘remaining under’ — staying under a load instead of throwing it off. It is not passive resignation but active, hopeful steadfastness. This study adds it to a new endurance family, alongside μακροθυμία (long-tempered patience with people, James 5:10). Hebrews told believers they needed it (Hebrews 10:36); James tells them how it grows — through testing — and what it produces: maturity, completeness, lacking nothing."},{"english":"Double-minded","hebrew":"δίψυχος","translit":"dípsychos","strongs":"G1374","verses":"v. 8; 4:8","insight":"Strong's: “two-spirited, i.e. vacillating (in opinion or purpose).” Literally ‘two-souled’. The word is not found in Greek before James and may be his own coinage. It describes a person with divided loyalty — one part reaching for God, another part reaching for the world. Jesus said no one can serve two masters. James uses the word twice: here of prayer, and in 4:8 of a heart that needs purifying. The cure is not a stronger will but a single heart."},{"english":"Implanted","hebrew":"ἔμφυτος","translit":"émphytos","strongs":"G1721","verses":"v. 21","insight":"Strong's: “implanted (figuratively).” The word was used of something rooted or innate. The word of truth by which God gave us new birth (v. 18) has been planted in believers like a seed. That is why James says ‘receive’ it — not as something foreign, but as something already growing that must be welcomed, watered and obeyed. Jesus' parable of the sower makes the same point: the good soil hears the word, holds it fast and bears fruit with endurance."},{"english":"Religion / religious","hebrew":"θρησκεία","translit":"thrēskeía","strongs":"G2356 (adjective θρησκός, thrēskós, G2357)","verses":"v. 26, 27","insight":"Strong's: “ceremonial observance.” θρησκεία is the outward side of worship — rituals, practices, visible devotion. It differs from εὐσέβεια, godliness or inner reverence, and this study adds it to the godliness family to show the contrast. James does not despise religion; he redefines it. Religion that God the Father accepts is not measured by ceremony but by a bridled tongue, practical care for orphans and widows, and a life kept clean from the world's stain."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is dealing with how you respond to pressure, and whether the word you hear is actually changing your life.","items":[{"title":"Your Trial Has a Purpose","body":"The Spirit wants you to stop asking only ‘How do I get out of this?’ and start asking ‘What is God producing in me through this?’ “Count it all joy, my brothers, when you fall into various temptations” (v. 2) is not a call to pretend; it is a call to judge your trial by its outcome. What would change if you believed that your current difficulty is the Refiner's fire, and that He is watching the crucible? What is He trying to make complete in you?"},{"title":"Stop Blaming, Start Owning","body":"“Let no man say when he is tempted, ‘I am tempted by God’” (v. 13). We rarely say it so directly. We say, ‘This is just how I am made,’ or ‘My circumstances made me do it,’ or ‘If God had given me a different life…’ The Spirit gently refuses every excuse and points to the hook inside: “his own lust” (v. 14). Where are you blaming God, your past or other people for a sin that began in your own desire? Name it. Owning it is the beginning of freedom."},{"title":"The Mirror Is Waiting","body":"You have heard many sermons. The Spirit asks: which one have you actually done? He brings to mind something He showed you in Scripture recently — a command, a warning, a call — that you agreed with and walked away from. “But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves” (v. 22). What is the one thing you already know you should do? What would it take to do it before the end of this week?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Joy Check","body":"When trouble comes, is your first response complaint, panic or prayer? Think of your last trial. Did you look for what God was producing in you (vv. 2–4), or only for the way out?"},{"title":"The Tongue Check","body":"Are you quick to hear, slow to speak and slow to anger (v. 19)? In your last disagreement, how much did you listen before you spoke? Is your religion being made worthless by an unbridled tongue (v. 26)?"},{"title":"The Widow Check","body":"Who are the ‘fatherless and widows’ in your world — the lonely, the bereaved, the vulnerable, those with no one to stand up for them (v. 27)? When did you last visit one of them? What stains of the world are you tolerating in your own life?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write Your Trial Down","body":"Tonight, take a sheet of paper. At the top write the trial you are facing. Below it write three columns: ‘What I want God to remove’, ‘What God may be producing (endurance, wisdom, humility, faith)’, and ‘What I am asking Him for’. Then pray verse 5 over the third column, asking for wisdom in faith. Keep the paper in your Bible and read it again in a month."},{"title":"Hear, Then Do Within 24 Hours","body":"This week, every time you read Scripture or hear a sermon, write down one specific thing to do in response — and do it within 24 hours: make the phone call, give the gift, ask the forgiveness, visit the person, remove the temptation. At the end of the week, look at your list. That list is the difference between a hearer and a doer."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father of lights, every good gift and every perfect gift in my life has come down from You, and You do not change. I confess that I have often met my trials with complaint instead of joy, and I have blamed You for temptations that began in my own desires. Today I count my trials as joy, because You are using the testing of my faith to produce endurance. Let endurance have its perfect work in me, until I am mature and complete, lacking nothing. I lack wisdom, Lord, and I ask You for it now — You who give to all generously and without scolding. Make my heart single, not double, so that I am not tossed like a wave of the sea. When I am drawn by my own desire, give me grace to refuse the bait before sin is conceived. Thank You that You gave me new birth by the word of truth. Plant that word deep in me. Make me quick to hear, slow to speak and slow to anger. Do not let me be a hearer who forgets. Make me a doer of Your word, who bends close to the perfect law of freedom and continues in it. Bridle my tongue, open my heart to the fatherless and widows, and keep me unstained by the world. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Joseph Refuses the Bait","ref":"Genesis 39:1-23","relatedVerses":"v. 12-15","summary":"Joseph was a slave in Egypt, far from home, when his master's wife began to pursue him. Day after day she pressed him, and one day, when no one else was in the house, she caught hold of his garment. Joseph did not argue with temptation; he ran, leaving his cloak in her hand. His answer revealed where his heart was: ‘How then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?’ The trial cost him his freedom — he was thrown into prison on a false charge — but it proved him. Joseph is the man of James 1:12: he endured the test, and God later lifted him from the prison to the palace."},{"title":"David on the Roof","ref":"2 Samuel 11:1-27","relatedVerses":"v. 14-15","summary":"In the spring, when kings went out to battle, David stayed at home. One evening he walked on the roof of his palace and saw a woman bathing. The sight became desire; desire became a question — ‘Who is she?’ — then an invitation, then adultery. When Bathsheba became pregnant, sin grew up: David tried to hide it, then arranged the death of her husband Uriah in battle. James 1:14–15 reads like a summary of that chapter: drawn away by his own desire and enticed, desire conceived and gave birth to sin, and sin, when it was full grown, brought death — Uriah's, and later the child's. David's story warns that the time to fight is on the roof, at the first look."},{"title":"The Brother Who Did Not Believe","ref":"John 7:1-9; 1 Corinthians 15:7; Acts 15:13-21","relatedVerses":"v. 1, 18","summary":"During Jesus' ministry, His brothers mocked Him, urging Him to go to Jerusalem and show Himself to the world — “For even his brothers didn’t believe in him” (John 7:5). James was one of those brothers. Then came the cross and the empty tomb, and Paul records that the risen Lord “appeared to James” (1 Corinthians 15:7). After that, James is found with the praying believers before Pentecost and later as the leader of the Jerusalem church, presiding at the great council where he said, “Brothers, listen to me” (Acts 15:13). The sceptic became a servant. When he writes that God “Of his own will he gave birth to us by the word of truth” (v. 18), he is writing his own testimony."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"James","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"No Favouritism, and Faith That Is Alive","themeTitle":"“Faith Apart from Works Is Dead”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"My brothers, don’t hold the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ of glory with partiality.","understanding":"μὴ ἐν προσωποληψίαις ἔχετε τὴν πίστιν, “don’t hold the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ of glory with partiality” — προσωποληψία, literally ‘face-receiving’: judging people by their face, their appearance or status. James calls Jesus τῆς δόξης, ‘the Glory’ — the very presence of God. Before Him every human ranking collapses; to bow to wealth in His presence is a contradiction.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G4382","hebrew":"προσωποληψία","translit":"prosōpolēpsía","english":"partiality"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For if a man with a gold ring, in fine clothing, comes into your synagogue, and a poor man in filthy clothing also comes in;","understanding":"ἀνὴρ χρυσοδακτύλιος ἐν ἐσθῆτι λαμπρᾷ, “a man with a gold ring, in fine clothing” — χρυσοδακτύλιος, ‘gold-fingered’, a man wearing rings to show his rank; λαμπρός, bright, shining clothes. Beside him, πτωχὸς ἐν ῥυπαρᾷ ἐσθῆτι, “a poor man in filthy clothing” — πτωχός, the destitute beggar, not merely someone with little. συναγωγή, “synagogue,” shows how Jewish these early congregations were: they still called their meeting a synagogue.","words":[{"strongs":"G5554","hebrew":"χρυσοδακτύλιος","translit":"chrysodaktýlios","english":"with a gold ring"},{"strongs":"G2986","hebrew":"λαμπρός","translit":"lamprós","english":"fine"},{"strongs":"G2066","hebrew":"ἐσθής","translit":"esthḗs","english":"clothing"},{"strongs":"G4864","hebrew":"συναγωγή","translit":"synagōgḗ","english":"synagogue"},{"strongs":"G4434","hebrew":"πτωχός","translit":"ptōchós","english":"poor"},{"strongs":"G4508","hebrew":"ῥυπαρός","translit":"rhyparós","english":"filthy"}]},{"v":3,"text":"and you pay special attention to him who wears the fine clothing, and say, “Sit here in a good place”; and you tell the poor man, “Stand there,” or “Sit by my footstool”;","understanding":"ἐπιβλέψητε, “you pay special attention” — to look upon with favour, to fix your eyes on someone because of what they can give you. “Sit here in a good place” for the rich; “Stand there,” or “Sit by my footstool” for the poor — ὑποπόδιον, the stool under someone's feet, the place of the lowest servant.","words":[{"strongs":"G1914","hebrew":"ἐπιβλέπω","translit":"epiblépō","english":"pay special attention"},{"strongs":"G2066","hebrew":"ἐσθής","translit":"esthḗs","english":"clothing"},{"strongs":"G4434","hebrew":"πτωχός","translit":"ptōchós","english":"poor"},{"strongs":"G5286","hebrew":"ὑποπόδιον","translit":"hypopódion","english":"footstool"}]},{"v":4,"text":"haven’t you shown partiality among yourselves, and become judges with evil thoughts?","understanding":"οὐ διεκρίθητε ἐν ἑαυτοῖς, “haven’t you shown partiality among yourselves” — διακρίνω, the same verb used for ‘doubting’ in 1:6: you have become divided, double-minded, splitting the church into first and second class. καὶ ἐγένεσθε κριταὶ διαλογισμῶν πονηρῶν, “and become judges with evil thoughts” — πονηρός, from the evil family: not κακός (badness in itself, 1:13) but evil that actively harms others. Their reasoning was harmful.","words":[{"strongs":"G1252","hebrew":"διακρίνω","translit":"diakrínō","english":"shown partiality"},{"strongs":"G2923","hebrew":"κριτής","translit":"kritḗs","english":"judges"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G1261","hebrew":"διαλογισμός","translit":"dialogismós","english":"thoughts"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Listen, my beloved brothers. Didn’t God choose those who are poor in this world to be rich in faith, and heirs of the Kingdom which he promised to those who love him?","understanding":"“Listen, my beloved brothers.” ἀγαπητοί, from the love family. “Didn’t God choose those who are poor in this world to be rich in faith” — ἐξελέξατο, God selected them. κόσμος, from the world family: poor in the world's eyes and by its standards, but πλουσίους ἐν πίστει, rich in faith, and κληρονόμους, heirs of the kingdom. “which he promised to those who love him” — ἀγαπάω, the same promise as 1:12.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G1586","hebrew":"ἐκλέγομαι","translit":"eklégomai","english":"choose"},{"strongs":"G4434","hebrew":"πτωχός","translit":"ptōchós","english":"poor"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G2818","hebrew":"κληρονόμος","translit":"klēronómos","english":"heirs"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But you have dishonored the poor man. Don’t the rich oppress you, and personally drag you before the courts?","understanding":"“But you have dishonored the poor man” — ἀτιμάζω, to strip of honour. The irony is sharp: “Don’t the rich oppress you” — καταδυναστεύω, to use power against someone, to tyrannise — “and personally drag you before the courts?” ἕλκω, to drag by force; κριτήρια, the law courts. They flatter the people who exploit them and despise the people God has chosen.","words":[{"strongs":"G818","hebrew":"ἀτιμάζω","translit":"atimázō","english":"dishonored"},{"strongs":"G4434","hebrew":"πτωχός","translit":"ptōchós","english":"poor"},{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G2616","hebrew":"καταδυναστεύω","translit":"katadynasteúō","english":"oppress"},{"strongs":"G1670","hebrew":"ἑλκύω","translit":"helkýō","english":"drag"},{"strongs":"G2922","hebrew":"κριτήριον","translit":"kritḗrion","english":"courts"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Don’t they blaspheme the honorable name by which you are called?","understanding":"“Don’t they blaspheme the honorable name by which you are called?” — βλασφημέω, to speak against, to slander God. τὸ καλὸν ὄνομα, the noble name — καλός, from the good family: good as fine and beautiful, rather than ἀγαθός, good in usefulness. τὸ ἐπικληθὲν ἐφ᾽ ὑμᾶς, ‘the name called over you’ — the name of Christ, perhaps spoken over them at baptism.","words":[{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blaspheme"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"honorable"},{"strongs":"G1941","hebrew":"ἐπικαλέομαι","translit":"epikaléomai","english":"called"}]},{"v":8,"text":"However, if you fulfill the royal law, according to the Scripture, “You shall love your neighbor as yourself,” you do well.","understanding":"εἰ μέντοι νόμον τελεῖτε βασιλικόν, “However, if you fulfill the royal law” — τελέω, from the perfect family: to carry out fully. βασιλικός, royal — the law of the King, the law that rules all others, as Jesus taught that all the law hangs on love. “You shall love your neighbor as yourself” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family — comes from Leviticus 19:18, and only a few verses earlier, Leviticus 19:15 forbids showing partiality to the poor or the great. καλῶς ποιεῖτε, “you do well” — καλῶς, the adverb of καλός, from the good family.","words":[{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"fulfill"},{"strongs":"G937","hebrew":"βασιλικός","translit":"basilikós","english":"royal"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4139","hebrew":"πλησίον","translit":"plēsíon","english":"neighbor"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But if you show partiality, you commit sin, being convicted by the law as transgressors.","understanding":"εἰ δὲ προσωποληπτεῖτε, ἁμαρτίαν ἐργάζεσθε, “But if you show partiality, you commit sin” — ἐργάζομαι, from the work family: literally ‘you work sin’. ἁμαρτία, from the sin family: missing the mark. ἐλεγχόμενοι ὑπὸ τοῦ νόμου ὡς παραβάται, “being convicted by the law as transgressors” — ἐλέγχω, from the discipline family: to expose and prove guilty; παραβάτης, from the sin family: one who steps across a known line. Favouritism is not a small social error; the law itself convicts it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4380","hebrew":"προσωποληπτέω","translit":"prosōpolēptéō","english":"show partiality"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"commit"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"convicted"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G3848","hebrew":"παραβάτης","translit":"parabátēs","english":"transgressors"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For whoever keeps the whole law, and yet stumbles in one point, he has become guilty of all.","understanding":"“For whoever keeps the whole law, and yet stumbles in one point, he has become guilty of all.” τηρέω, from the hold family: to keep carefully. πταίω, to trip. ἔνοχος, liable, answerable. The law is one, because the Lawgiver is one. Break one link and the whole chain is broken.","words":[{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G4417","hebrew":"πταίω","translit":"ptaíō","english":"stumbles"},{"strongs":"G1777","hebrew":"ἔνοχος","translit":"énochos","english":"guilty"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For he who said, “Do not commit adultery,” also said, “Do not commit murder.” Now if you do not commit adultery, but murder, you have become a transgressor of the law.","understanding":"“For he who said, ‘Do not commit adultery,’ also said, ‘Do not commit murder.’” The commandments come from one Person. Obeying one while breaking another still makes you παραβάτης, “a transgressor of the law” — from the sin family, the same word as verse 9.","words":[{"strongs":"G3431","hebrew":"μοιχεύω","translit":"moicheúō","english":"commit adultery"},{"strongs":"G5407","hebrew":"φονεύω","translit":"phoneúō","english":"commit murder"},{"strongs":"G3848","hebrew":"παραβάτης","translit":"parabátēs","english":"transgressor"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"}]},{"v":12,"text":"So speak, and so do, as men who are to be judged by a law of freedom.","understanding":"οὕτως λαλεῖτε καὶ οὕτως ποιεῖτε, “So speak, and so do” — words and deeds, the two subjects of the whole letter. “as men who are to be judged by a law of freedom” — κρίνω, to judge; ἐλευθερία, the same freedom as 1:25. Believers are not free from judgement; they are free people who will be judged by how they used their freedom. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G1657","hebrew":"ἐλευθερία","translit":"eleuthería","english":"freedom"}]},{"v":13,"text":"For judgment is without mercy to him who has shown no mercy. Mercy triumphs over judgment.","understanding":"ἡ γὰρ κρίσις ἀνέλεος τῷ μὴ ποιήσαντι ἔλεος, “For judgment is without mercy to him who has shown no mercy.” κρίσις, judgement; ἔλεος, from the mercy family: compassion that acts for those in need, rather than οἰκτιρμός, the feeling of pity. κατακαυχᾶται ἔλεος κρίσεως, “Mercy triumphs over judgment” — κατακαυχάομαι, to boast over, to exult in victory. Mercy shown to the poor man at the door is the proof of a heart that has received mercy, and on the last day mercy will stand up and boast. κρίσις is from the judge family — judgement as an act or process — the trial, the hour of judging.","words":[{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G448","hebrew":"ἀνίλεως","translit":"aníleōs","english":"without mercy"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"shown"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G2620","hebrew":"κατακαυχάομαι","translit":"katakaucháomai","english":"triumphs"}]},{"v":14,"text":"What good is it, my brothers, if a man says he has faith, but has no works? Can faith save him?","understanding":"τί τὸ ὄφελος, “What good is it” — ὄφελος, profit, use. ἐὰν πίστιν λέγῃ τις ἔχειν, “if a man says he has faith” — notice the word ‘says’. James is not attacking faith but a claim to faith. ἔργα δὲ μὴ ἔχῃ, “but has no works” — ἔργον, from the work family: deeds of love and obedience, not ceremonies. “Can faith save him?” — σῴζω, from the heal family, means to rescue and make whole; and in Greek it is ἡ πίστις, ‘that faith’, the kind just described. The question expects the answer ‘No.’","words":[{"strongs":"G3786","hebrew":"ὄφελος","translit":"óphelos","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"}]},{"v":15,"text":"And if a brother or sister is naked and in lack of daily food,","understanding":"“And if a brother or sister is naked and in lack of daily food” — γυμνός, poorly clothed, without a proper outer garment; λειπόμενοι τῆς ἐφημέρου τροφῆς, lacking food for today — not tomorrow's needs, today's. And it is a brother or sister, a fellow believer.","words":[{"strongs":"G1131","hebrew":"γυμνός","translit":"gymnós","english":"naked"},{"strongs":"G3007","hebrew":"λείπω","translit":"leípō","english":"lack"},{"strongs":"G2184","hebrew":"ἐφήμερος","translit":"ephḗmeros","english":"daily"},{"strongs":"G5160","hebrew":"τροφή","translit":"trophḗ","english":"food"}]},{"v":16,"text":"and one of you tells them, “Go in peace, be warmed and filled”; and yet you didn’t give them the things the body needs, what good is it?","understanding":"“Go in peace, be warmed and filled” — ὑπάγετε ἐν εἰρήνῃ, a pious blessing; θερμαίνεσθε καὶ χορτάζεσθε, perhaps ‘keep yourselves warm and fed’. Words without help. “and yet you didn’t give them the things the body needs” — τὰ ἐπιτήδεια τοῦ σώματος, the necessities. “what good is it?” — the same ὄφελος as verse 14: such a blessing helps no one, and such a faith saves no one.","words":[{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"Go"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G2328","hebrew":"θερμαίνω","translit":"thermaínō","english":"warmed"},{"strongs":"G5526","hebrew":"χορτάζω","translit":"chortázō","english":"filled"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G2006","hebrew":"ἐπιτήδειος","translit":"epitḗdeios","english":"things"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3786","hebrew":"ὄφελος","translit":"óphelos","english":"good"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Even so faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself.","understanding":"οὕτως καὶ ἡ πίστις, ἐὰν μὴ ἔχῃ ἔργα, νεκρά ἐστιν καθ᾽ ἑαυτήν, “Even so faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself” — ἔργα, from the work family, are missing, and the faith is νεκρός, a corpse. Not weak, not immature, but dead ‘in itself’ — inwardly lifeless, whatever it claims.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Yes, a man will say, “You have faith, and I have works.” Show me your faith without works, and I by my works will show you my faith.","understanding":"“Yes, a man will say, ‘You have faith, and I have works.’” An objector suggests that faith and works are separate gifts — some people have one, some the other. James refuses: “Show me your faith without works, and I by my works will show you my faith.” δείκνυμι, to display. Faith is invisible; works (ἔργα, from the work family) make it visible. A faith that cannot be shown cannot be proven to exist.","words":[{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"Show"}]},{"v":19,"text":"You believe that God is one. You do well. The demons also believe, and shudder.","understanding":"σὺ πιστεύεις ὅτι εἷς ἐστιν ὁ θεός, “You believe that God is one” — the Shema, the creed every Jew recited daily. καλῶς ποιεῖς, “You do well” — καλῶς from the good family, as in verse 8, but now with irony. καὶ τὰ δαιμόνια πιστεύουσιν καὶ φρίσσουσιν, “The demons also believe, and shudder” — φρίσσω, to bristle with terror. The demons have perfect theology; in the Gospels they named Jesus correctly. Correct belief without love and obedience is the faith of hell.","words":[{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G1520","hebrew":"εἷς","translit":"heîs","english":"one"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"},{"strongs":"G5425","hebrew":"φρίσσω","translit":"phríssō","english":"shudder"}]},{"v":20,"text":"But do you want to know, vain man, that faith apart from works is dead?","understanding":"“But do you want to know, vain man” — κενός, empty, hollow. “that faith apart from works is dead?” The WEB here follows manuscripts that read νεκρά, ‘dead’. Many early manuscripts read ἀργή instead — ‘idle, useless’ — from ἀ- (‘not’) and ἔργον (‘work’): ‘faith without works doesn't work.’ Either reading makes the same point about ἔργα, from the work family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2756","hebrew":"κενός","translit":"kenós","english":"vain"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"apart from"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Wasn’t Abraham our father justified by works, in that he offered up Isaac his son on the altar?","understanding":"Ἀβραὰμ ὁ πατὴρ ἡμῶν οὐκ ἐξ ἔργων ἐδικαιώθη, “Wasn’t Abraham our father justified by works” — ἔργα, from the work family; δικαιόω, to declare or show to be righteous. ἀνενέγκας Ἰσαὰκ … ἐπὶ τὸ θυσιαστήριον, “in that he offered up Isaac his son on the altar” — ἀναφέρω, from the offering family: to carry up onto the altar, the same verb Hebrews uses of Christ offering Himself (Hebrews 7:27). Genesis 22 came many years after Genesis 15:6.","words":[{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"father"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G399","hebrew":"ἀναφέρω","translit":"anaphérō","english":"offered up"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"}]},{"v":22,"text":"You see that faith worked with his works, and by works faith was perfected;","understanding":"βλέπεις ὅτι ἡ πίστις συνήργει τοῖς ἔργοις αὐτοῦ, “You see that faith worked with his works” — βλέπω, from the see family; συνεργέω, from the work family: to work together. Faith and works were partners, not rivals. καὶ ἐκ τῶν ἔργων ἡ πίστις ἐτελειώθη, “and by works faith was perfected” — τελειόω, from the perfect family: brought to its goal, matured. Faith was there first; works brought it to its full expression.","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4903","hebrew":"συνεργέω","translit":"synergéō","english":"worked"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfected"}]},{"v":23,"text":"and the Scripture was fulfilled which says, “Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him as righteousness”; and he was called the friend of God.","understanding":"καὶ ἐπληρώθη ἡ γραφή, “and the Scripture was fulfilled” — πληρόω, from the perfect family: filled full. Compare τελέω in verse 8. Genesis 15:6 — ‘Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him as righteousness’ — was spoken decades before Mount Moriah; the offering of Isaac filled that word with its full meaning. λογίζομαι, to credit to an account. “and he was called the friend of God” — φίλος, from the love family: warm personal affection, the language of 2 Chronicles 20:7 and Isaiah 41:8. Jesus said, “You are my friends, if you do whatever I command you” (John 15:14).","words":[{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"fulfilled"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"},{"strongs":"G3049","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","english":"accounted"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G5384","hebrew":"φίλος","translit":"phílos","english":"friend"}]},{"v":24,"text":"You see then that by works, a man is justified, and not only by faith.","understanding":"ὁρᾶτε, “You see then” — ὁράω, from the see family: here, to perceive the conclusion. “that by works, a man is justified, and not only by faith.” ἔργα, from the work family. Notice ‘not only’. James does not say faith is unnecessary; he denies that faith alone — the bare, workless faith of verses 14–19 — justifies. Here δικαιόω means shown and vindicated as righteous before God and people, the righteousness God credited now demonstrated.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":25,"text":"In the same way, wasn’t Rahab the prostitute also justified by works, in that she received the messengers, and sent them out another way?","understanding":"“In the same way, wasn’t Rahab the prostitute also justified by works” — Ῥαάβ ἡ πόρνη, a Canaanite woman with a shameful past, the opposite of Abraham in every way except one: her faith acted, in ἔργα, from the work family. ὑποδεξαμένη τοὺς ἀγγέλους, “in that she received the messengers” — ὑποδέχομαι, to welcome under one's roof. καὶ ἑτέρᾳ ὁδῷ ἐκβαλοῦσα, “and sent them out another way” — ἕτερος, from the another family: a different road, not the one the soldiers were watching.","words":[{"strongs":"G4460","hebrew":"Ῥαάβ","translit":"Rhaáb","english":"Rahab"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G1344","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","english":"justified"},{"strongs":"G5264","hebrew":"ὑποδέχομαι","translit":"hypodéchomai","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"messengers"},{"strongs":"G1544","hebrew":"ἐκβάλλω","translit":"ekbállō","english":"sent them out"},{"strongs":"G2087","hebrew":"ἕτερος","translit":"héteros","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"}]},{"v":26,"text":"For as the body apart from the spirit is dead, even so faith apart from works is dead.","understanding":"ὥσπερ γὰρ τὸ σῶμα χωρὶς πνεύματος νεκρόν ἐστιν, “For as the body apart from the spirit is dead” — πνεῦμα, here the breath of life. A body without breath may look complete, but it is a corpse. “even so faith apart from works is dead” — faith without ἔργα, from the work family, for the third time (vv. 17, 20, 26).","words":[{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G5565","hebrew":"χωρίς","translit":"chōrís","english":"apart from"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Two Visitors at the Door","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture a small house church on a Sunday morning. The door opens and a man walks in with gold rings flashing on his fingers and a robe so white it shines. Heads turn; an usher hurries forward and leads him to the best cushion near the front. Moments later the door opens again. A beggar in stained, smelly rags shuffles in. The same usher points to the back wall: ‘Stand there.’ Or he waves at the floor beside his own feet: ‘Sit down here.’","meaning":"“haven’t you shown partiality among yourselves, and become judges with evil thoughts?” (v. 4). Every time we rank people by appearance, wealth, status or usefulness to us, we take the judge's seat that belongs to God alone. In the presence of “our Lord Jesus Christ of glory” (v. 1), the gold ring and the rags are equally irrelevant. The church is the one place on earth where the poor man should be given the best seat."},{"title":"The Blessing That Left Her Hungry","verseRef":"v. 14-17","image":"Picture a woman shivering at a church gate on a winter evening, thin and hungry. A well-dressed believer recognises her, smiles warmly, lays a hand on her shoulder and says, ‘God bless you, sister. Go in peace. Stay warm and eat well.’ Then he walks to his car, turns on the heater and drives home to dinner. She stands at the gate exactly as she was — blessed, cold and hungry.","meaning":"“and one of you tells them, ‘Go in peace, be warmed and filled’; and yet you didn’t give them the things the body needs, what good is it?” (v. 16). Kind words that cost nothing do nothing. “Even so faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself” (v. 17). Faith that never reaches the hands is no more alive than a blessing that never reaches the stomach."},{"title":"The Shuddering Demons","verseRef":"v. 19","image":"Picture a dark place where unseen spirits gather. They are not atheists. They know there is one God; they know exactly who Jesus is; they have seen His power and heard His voice. And as they speak His name their whole being bristles with dread. They believe every word of the creed — and they tremble, and they hate.","meaning":"“You believe that God is one. You do well. The demons also believe, and shudder” (v. 19). James's point is devastating: you can have perfectly correct beliefs and still have the faith of a demon. Saving faith is not just agreeing that something is true; it is trusting God so that you love and obey Him. The demons' faith makes them shudder; Abraham's faith made him a friend of God."},{"title":"The Altar and the Scarlet Cord","verseRef":"v. 21-25","image":"Picture two scenes. On a mountain an old man lays wood on an altar, binds his only son and raises a knife, trusting that God can raise the dead. Far away and years later, in a house built into a city wall, a woman with a bad reputation hides two foreign spies under stalks of flax, then lowers them down the wall by a rope and points them to the hills.","meaning":"“Wasn’t Abraham our father justified by works, in that he offered up Isaac his son on the altar?” (v. 21). “In the same way, wasn’t Rahab the prostitute also justified by works, in that she received the messengers, and sent them out another way?” (v. 25). The patriarch and the prostitute stand side by side. Neither earned God's favour, but both showed that their faith was real by risking everything on God's word. Faith is the root; obedience is the fruit."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Sin of Favouritism (v. 1-7)","body":"Do not hold faith in the glorious Lord with partiality; honouring the rich man and humiliating the poor makes you judges with evil thoughts; God chose the poor of the world to be rich in faith and heirs of the kingdom, while the rich oppress believers and blaspheme Christ's name."},{"label":"The Royal Law and the Whole Law (v. 8-13)","body":"Loving your neighbour as yourself fulfils the royal law; partiality is sin and makes you a transgressor; breaking one commandment makes you guilty of all, because the same God gave them all; speak and act as those who will be judged by the law of freedom, for judgement is merciless to the merciless, and mercy triumphs over judgement."},{"label":"Dead Faith (v. 14-20)","body":"Faith that claims to exist but has no works cannot save; a blessing without bread helps no one; faith is shown by works, and even demons believe God is one and shudder."},{"label":"Living Faith: Abraham and Rahab (v. 21-26)","body":"Abraham was justified by works when he offered Isaac, his faith working with his works and being perfected by them; Rahab was justified by works when she received and sent away the messengers; as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Partiality Denies the Gospel","paragraphs":["James begins this chapter with a scene everyone in his first audience would recognise. A rich man is ushered to the best seat; a poor man is told to stand or sit on the floor. It looks like a small matter of seating. James calls it sin, because it contradicts everything the gospel says about God. God “doesn’t show favoritism” (Acts 10:34), and “there is no partiality with God” (Romans 2:11). When a church honours people for their wealth, it is preaching a different god.","It also gets God's choice backwards. “Didn’t God choose those who are poor in this world to be rich in faith, and heirs of the Kingdom which he promised to those who love him?” (v. 5). Jesus began His sermon with the same words: “Blessed are you who are poor, God’s Kingdom is yours” (Luke 6:20). Paul saw the same pattern in Corinth: “God chose the foolish things of the world that he might put to shame those who are wise” (1 Corinthians 1:27). This does not mean that every poor person is saved or every rich person lost. It means that God delights to choose people the world overlooks, and the church must see people as God sees them.","James adds an irony: the rich people they flatter are the very ones who oppress them, drag them to court and slander the name of Christ (vv. 6–7). Favouritism is not only unjust; it is foolish. It bows to power and despises the people God has honoured. For many readers today the ‘gold ring’ may be a title, a degree, a nationality, an accent, a skin colour, a social media following or a useful connection. The question is the same: do you see a person, or a price tag?"]},{"heading":"The Royal Law and Mercy's Triumph","paragraphs":["Someone might answer, ‘But I'm loving my neighbour — the rich one!’ James answers that the royal law cannot be obeyed selectively. “However, if you fulfill the royal law, according to the Scripture, ‘You shall love your neighbor as yourself,’ you do well” (v. 8). “But if you show partiality, you commit sin, being convicted by the law as transgressors” (v. 9). Jesus called this the second great commandment: “The whole law and the prophets depend on these two commandments” (Matthew 22:40). Paul agrees: “For the whole law is fulfilled in one word, in this: ‘You shall love your neighbor as yourself’” (Galatians 5:14).","James then makes a point that shocks moral people: “For whoever keeps the whole law, and yet stumbles in one point, he has become guilty of all” (v. 10). The law is not a list of separate rules where you score points. It is the expression of one Person's will. “For he who said, ‘Do not commit adultery,’ also said, ‘Do not commit murder’” (v. 11). Breaking any command is defying the same God. No one, therefore, can boast. Favouritism is as much a break in the law as adultery or murder.","This leads James to mercy. “So speak, and so do, as men who are to be judged by a law of freedom” (v. 12). “For judgment is without mercy to him who has shown no mercy. Mercy triumphs over judgment” (v. 13). Jesus said, “Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy” (Matthew 5:7). This is not salvation by being merciful; it is the evidence of salvation. A person who has truly received God's mercy becomes merciful. A heart that has no mercy for the poor shows that it has never understood its own need of mercy. And on the day of judgement, mercy wins."]},{"heading":"Faith That Works: James and Paul Together","paragraphs":["Verses 14–26 are some of the most discussed verses in the Bible. James asks, “What good is it, my brothers, if a man says he has faith, but has no works? Can faith save him?” (v. 14). The key word is ‘says’. James is attacking a claim to faith that never shows itself in action. His illustration is unforgettable: a brother or sister in need, a pious blessing, and nothing else (vv. 15–16). John says the same: “But whoever has the world’s goods, and sees his brother in need, and closes his heart of compassion against him, how does God’s love remain in him?” (1 John 3:17).","Then James appears to contradict Paul. Paul says, “a man is not justified by the works of the law but through faith in Jesus Christ” (Galatians 2:16), and “For what does the Scripture say? ‘Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness’” (Romans 4:3). James says, “You see then that by works, a man is justified, and not only by faith” (v. 24). Both even quote Genesis 15:6. But they are answering different questions. Paul asks: how can a guilty sinner be accepted by God? Answer: not by works of the law, but by faith in Christ. James asks: how can you tell whether a person's faith is real? Answer: by its works. Paul fights legalism; James fights empty profession. Paul's ‘works’ are works of the law done to earn favour; James's ‘works’ are the deeds of love that faith produces.","Notice how carefully James argues. Abraham was declared righteous when he believed, in Genesis 15. Many years later he offered Isaac, in Genesis 22. That act did not create his righteousness; it revealed and completed it: “You see that faith worked with his works, and by works faith was perfected; and the Scripture was fulfilled” (vv. 22–23). The word ‘fulfilled’ is the key. The faith God credited as righteousness was proved real on Mount Moriah. Paul himself says the same in his own language: we are told, “for by grace you have been saved through faith” (Ephesians 2:8), and “not of works, that no one would boast” (Ephesians 2:9) — and then, in the next breath, we are “created in Christ Jesus for good works” (Ephesians 2:10). Faith alone saves, but the faith that saves is never alone."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Abraham, Rahab, and the Faith That Obeys","paragraphs":["James's two examples are chosen with care, and both appear elsewhere in the New Testament. Hebrews celebrates the same act of Abraham: “By faith, Abraham, being tested, offered up Isaac” (Hebrews 11:17), “concluding that God is able to raise up even from the dead” (Hebrews 11:19). Jesus told the religious leaders who boasted of Abraham, “If you were Abraham’s children, you would do the works of Abraham” (John 8:39). Real children of Abraham share his obedient faith, not just his bloodline.","Rahab appears in Hebrews too: “By faith, Rahab the prostitute, didn’t perish with those who were disobedient, having received the spies in peace” (Hebrews 11:31). And she appears in the family tree of Jesus: “Salmon became the father of Boaz by Rahab” (Matthew 1:5). A Canaanite woman with a shameful past, whose faith acted when it was dangerous, became an ancestor of the Messiah. James places her beside Abraham to show that living faith is not for spiritual giants only. It is for anyone who trusts God enough to act.","Jesus Himself drew the line that James draws: “Not everyone who says to me, ‘Lord, Lord,’ will enter into the Kingdom of Heaven; but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven” (Matthew 7:21). His parable of the sheep and goats measures faith by the hungry fed and the naked clothed: “I was naked, and you clothed me. I was sick, and you visited me. I was in prison, and you came to me” (Matthew 25:36). And Paul, the apostle of grace, sums it up: “For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision amounts to anything, nor uncircumcision, but faith working through love” (Galatians 5:6). James and Paul are not opponents. They are two voices in one choir."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Partiality / favouritism","hebrew":"προσωποληψία","translit":"prosōpolēpsía","strongs":"G4382 (verb προσωποληπτέω, prosōpolēptéō, G4380)","verses":"v. 1, 9","insight":"Strong's: “partiality, i.e. favoritism.” The word is built from πρόσωπον (face) and λαμβάνω (receive) — ‘receiving a face’, a Hebrew idiom for judging by outward appearance. It appears to be a Christian word; it is not found in earlier Greek. The New Testament uses it of God only in the negative: God shows no partiality (Romans 2:11). James applies it to church seating, but the principle reaches everywhere — hiring, friendship, attention, respect. Every time you treat someone better or worse because of what they can do for you, you are receiving a face."},{"english":"Royal","hebrew":"βασιλικός","translit":"basilikós","strongs":"G937","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “regal (in relation).” The ‘royal law’ is the law of the King, the commandment of love for neighbour, which Jesus set alongside love for God as the summary of all the law. It may also mean the law fit for those who are themselves royal — heirs of the kingdom (v. 5). Either way, love is not one rule among many; it is the law that rules over all the others. Partiality breaks the king's own law."},{"english":"Mercy","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","strongs":"G1656 (without mercy ἀνίλεως, aníleōs, G448)","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “compassion (human or divine, especially active).” ἔλεος is mercy that does something — feeding, clothing, forgiving, helping. Its opposite in verse 13 is the dictionary's ἀνίλεως, ‘inexorable’, merciless. James says that the merciless will meet merciless judgement, but ‘mercy triumphs over judgment.’ The verb κατακαυχάομαι pictures mercy standing over judgement and boasting in victory. A merciful life is not the price of salvation but its sign."},{"english":"Dead","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","strongs":"G3498 (idle ἀργός, argós, G692)","verses":"v. 17, 20, 26","insight":"Strong's: “dead (literally or figuratively; also as noun).” James repeats it three times for weight. A dead faith may look like faith — it has the right words, as a corpse has the right shape — but there is no life in it. In verse 20 many manuscripts read ἀργή, ‘idle, unemployed’, from ἀ- and ἔργον: a faith that does no work. Both words say the same thing: faith is proved alive by what it does."},{"english":"Justified","hebrew":"δικαιόω","translit":"dikaióō","strongs":"G1344","verses":"v. 21, 24, 25","insight":"Strong's: “to render (i.e. show or regard as) just or innocent.” The dictionary's definition holds both senses that James and Paul use. In Paul, δικαιόω usually means God declaring a sinner righteous through faith in Christ. In James 2 it means being shown to be righteous — the reality of faith demonstrated and vindicated. Abraham was declared righteous in Genesis 15 and shown righteous in Genesis 22. Paul explains the root of salvation; James examines its fruit."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is exposing the gap between the faith you profess and the way you treat people.","items":[{"title":"Who Gets Your Best Seat?","body":"The Spirit wants to show you who you instinctively honour and who you instinctively overlook. Think of your church, your workplace, your social circle. Who gets your attention, your warmth, your time — and why? Who do you avoid because they are poor, awkward, unattractive or of no use to you? “Didn’t God choose those who are poor in this world to be rich in faith” (v. 5)? Ask Him to show you one person you have been seating by the footstool."},{"title":"Words That Feed No One","body":"It is easy to say ‘I'll pray for you’ and then do nothing. The Spirit is not against prayer; He is against religious words that replace love. “and yet you didn’t give them the things the body needs, what good is it?” (v. 16). Is there a need He has placed in front of you that you have answered only with words? What would it look like to answer it with your hands this week?"},{"title":"Is Your Faith Alive?","body":"The Spirit does not want you to live in fear, but He does want you to be honest. “Show me your faith without works, and I by my works will show you my faith” (v. 18). If someone watched your life for a week without hearing you speak, what would they conclude you believe? Where has your faith become a creed you recite rather than a trust you act on? Bring Him the answer, and ask Him to breathe life into it."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Gold Ring Check","body":"Whose opinion and approval matter most to you? Do you change your tone, attention or welcome depending on someone's wealth, position or usefulness (vv. 1–4)? Name the ‘gold ring’ that most impresses you."},{"title":"The ‘Go in Peace’ Check","body":"When did you last meet a practical need instead of offering only words (vv. 15–16)? Is there a brother or sister you know is in need right now?"},{"title":"The Mercy Check","body":"Are you quick to judge and slow to show mercy (v. 13)? Who do you find it hardest to be merciful to, and what does that reveal about how you see your own need of God's mercy?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Give the Best Seat Away","body":"This week, deliberately seek out the person others overlook — at church, at work, at school or in your neighbourhood. Sit with them, learn their name, listen to their story, and if you can, invite them to a meal (Luke 14:13). Do it without telling anyone. Afterwards, write down what you learned about them and about yourself."},{"title":"Turn One Blessing Into Bread","body":"Think of one person you know who is in practical need — food, rent, transport, clothing, a repair, a hospital visit. Within the next three days, meet some part of that need with your own money, time or hands. Then thank God that He has given you a faith that works, and ask Him to make this a habit rather than an event."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus Christ, Lord of glory, I confess that I have often held my faith in You with partiality. I have been impressed by gold rings and fine clothes, and I have overlooked the poor man at the door. Forgive me for becoming a judge with evil thoughts. Open my eyes to see people as You see them — to honour those You have chosen, the poor in this world who are rich in faith. Teach me to fulfil the royal law, to love my neighbour as myself, every neighbour, not only the useful ones. I know that if I stumble in one point I am guilty of all, so I come again for Your mercy. Make me merciful, because mercy triumphs over judgement. Deliver me from a faith that only talks. When my brother or sister is cold and hungry, do not let me say ‘Go in peace’ and walk away. Let my faith work with my works, as Abraham's did, and let my obedience show that I trust You, as Rahab's did. Breathe life into my faith, so that it is never a body without breath. Make me Your friend, Lord, by doing what You command. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Abraham on Mount Moriah","ref":"Genesis 22:1-19","relatedVerses":"v. 21-23","summary":"God told Abraham, ‘Take your son, your only son Isaac, whom you love,’ and offer him as a burnt offering on a mountain He would show him. Abraham rose early, saddled his donkey, split the wood and went. On the third day he told his servants, ‘The boy and I will go yonder. We will worship, and come back to you.’ He built the altar, bound Isaac and raised the knife — and the angel of the Lord stopped him, and God provided a ram. Decades earlier Abraham had believed God and been counted righteous. On Moriah that faith was put to the test and proved real. This is what James means: faith worked with his works, and by works faith was perfected."},{"title":"Rahab and the Spies","ref":"Joshua 2:1-24; 6:22-25","relatedVerses":"v. 25","summary":"Two Israelite spies entered Jericho and lodged at the house of Rahab, a prostitute whose house was built into the city wall. When the king's men came, she hid the spies under flax on her roof and sent the soldiers the wrong way. She told the spies, ‘I know that Yahweh has given you the land … for Yahweh your God, he is God in heaven above, and on earth beneath.’ Then she let them down by a rope through her window and told them to hide in the hills. She tied a scarlet cord in her window as a sign, and when Jericho fell, she and her family were saved. Her confession was faith; her risk was works. James says she was justified by works — her faith acted when acting could have cost her life."},{"title":"Peter Withdraws from the Table","ref":"Galatians 2:11-14","relatedVerses":"v. 1-4, 9","summary":"In Antioch, Peter used to eat freely with Gentile believers. Then “some people came from James” (Galatians 2:12), and Peter began to withdraw, “fearing those who were of the circumcision” (Galatians 2:12). Other Jewish believers followed him, “so that even Barnabas was carried away with their hypocrisy” (Galatians 2:13). Paul confronted Peter publicly because the gospel itself was at stake. The story is an honest warning: even great apostles, and even people connected with James, could fall into favouritism — honouring some at the table and shaming others. The sin James condemns in chapter 2 was not theoretical. It was happening in the church, and it had to be named."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"James","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"The Tongue and the Two Wisdoms","themeTitle":"“The Wisdom That Is from Above Is First Pure, Then Peaceful”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Let not many of you be teachers, my brothers, knowing that we will receive heavier judgment.","understanding":"μὴ πολλοὶ διδάσκαλοι γίνεσθε, “Let not many of you be teachers” — διδάσκαλος, a recognised teacher of the church, a rabbi. Teaching was honoured and many wanted it. James includes himself — “we will receive heavier judgment” — κρίμα, a verdict. Teachers work with words, and words are the hardest thing to control; the more you say about God, the more you will answer for. κρίμα is from the judge family — judgement as the verdict or sentence passed.","words":[{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teachers"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"heavier"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For in many things we all stumble. If anyone doesn’t stumble in word, the same is a perfect man, able to bridle the whole body also.","understanding":"πολλὰ γὰρ πταίομεν ἅπαντες, “For in many things we all stumble” — πταίω, the stumble of 2:10. James puts himself among the stumblers. “If anyone doesn’t stumble in word” — λόγος, from the word family: here simply what you say. “the same is a perfect man” — τέλειος, from the perfect family, as in 1:4: mature, complete. “able to bridle the whole body also” — χαλιναγωγέω, the bridling of 1:26. Control the tongue and you can control everything.","words":[{"strongs":"G4417","hebrew":"πταίω","translit":"ptaíō","english":"stumble"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G5468","hebrew":"χαλιναγωγέω","translit":"chalinagōgéō","english":"bridle"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Indeed, we put bits into the horses’ mouths so that they may obey us, and we guide their whole body.","understanding":"“we put bits into the horses’ mouths so that they may obey us” — χαλινός, the bit and bridle. A tiny piece of metal in the mouth turns a powerful animal. μετάγω, to steer, to turn: “and we guide their whole body.” What steers the mouth steers the life.","words":[{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G5469","hebrew":"χαλινός","translit":"chalinós","english":"bits"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouths"},{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"obey"},{"strongs":"G3329","hebrew":"μετάγω","translit":"metágō","english":"guide"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Behold, the ships also, though they are so big and are driven by fierce winds, are yet guided by a very small rudder, wherever the pilot desires.","understanding":"“Behold, the ships also, though they are so big and are driven by fierce winds” — σκληρός, harsh, rough; ἐλαύνω, pushed along. “are yet guided by a very small rudder” — ἐλάχιστος, the smallest; πηδάλιον, the steering oar. “wherever the pilot desires” — ὁ εὐθύνων, the one who steers straight, and βούλομαι, his will. The tongue is small but it sets the direction, and someone's will is always on the rudder.","words":[{"strongs":"G4143","hebrew":"πλοῖον","translit":"ploîon","english":"ships"},{"strongs":"G4642","hebrew":"σκληρός","translit":"sklērós","english":"fierce"},{"strongs":"G417","hebrew":"ἄνεμος","translit":"ánemos","english":"winds"},{"strongs":"G1643","hebrew":"ἐλαύνω","translit":"elaúnō","english":"driven"},{"strongs":"G3329","hebrew":"μετάγω","translit":"metágō","english":"guided"},{"strongs":"G1646","hebrew":"ἐλάχιστος","translit":"eláchistos","english":"very small"},{"strongs":"G4079","hebrew":"πηδάλιον","translit":"pēdálion","english":"rudder"},{"strongs":"G2116","hebrew":"εὐθύνω","translit":"euthýnō","english":"pilot"},{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"desires"}]},{"v":5,"text":"So the tongue is also a little member, and boasts great things. See how a small fire can spread to a large forest!","understanding":"“So the tongue is also a little member, and boasts great things” — μέλος, a part of the body; μεγαλαυχέω, to talk big. Then the image changes from steering to burning: “See how a small fire can spread to a large forest!” — ὕλη, woodland, brushwood; ἀνάπτω, to set alight. In the dry hills of Palestine one spark could consume a valley.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"member"},{"strongs":"G3166","hebrew":"μεγαλαυχέω","translit":"megalauchéō","english":"boasts great things"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G381","hebrew":"ἀνάπτω","translit":"anáptō","english":"spread"},{"strongs":"G5208","hebrew":"ὕλη","translit":"hýlē","english":"forest"}]},{"v":6,"text":"And the tongue is a fire. The world of iniquity among our members is the tongue, which defiles the whole body, and sets on fire the course of nature, and is set on fire by Gehenna.","understanding":"“And the tongue is a fire. The world of iniquity among our members is the tongue” — ὁ κόσμος τῆς ἀδικίας. κόσμος, from the world family: the whole system of a fallen world, concentrated in one small organ. ἀδικία, from the sin family: unrighteousness, wrong. “which defiles the whole body” — σπιλόω, to stain, the opposite of the ‘unstained’ life of 1:27. “and sets on fire the course of nature” — τὸν τροχὸν τῆς γενέσεως, ‘the wheel of birth’, the whole cycle of life from birth to death. “and is set on fire by Gehenna” — γέεννα, the valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem, a burning rubbish dump and the picture Jesus used for hell. The tongue's fire is lit from below.","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"iniquity"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"},{"strongs":"G4695","hebrew":"σπιλόω","translit":"spilóō","english":"defiles"},{"strongs":"G5394","hebrew":"φλογίζω","translit":"phlogízō","english":"sets on fire"},{"strongs":"G5164","hebrew":"τροχός","translit":"trochós","english":"course"},{"strongs":"G1078","hebrew":"γένεσις","translit":"génesis","english":"nature"},{"strongs":"G1067","hebrew":"γέεννα","translit":"géenna","english":"Gehenna"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For every kind of animal, bird, creeping thing, and sea creature, is tamed, and has been tamed by mankind;","understanding":"“For every kind of animal, bird, creeping thing, and sea creature, is tamed, and has been tamed by mankind” — the four groups of Genesis 1:26, over which God gave humans dominion. φύσις, nature or kind; δαμάζω, to tame, used twice for emphasis; τῇ φύσει τῇ ἀνθρωπίνῃ, ‘by human nature’. Humanity has tamed lions and elephants.","words":[{"strongs":"G5449","hebrew":"φύσις","translit":"phýsis","english":"kind"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"animal"},{"strongs":"G4071","hebrew":"πετεινόν","translit":"peteinón","english":"bird"},{"strongs":"G2062","hebrew":"ἑρπετόν","translit":"herpetón","english":"creeping thing"},{"strongs":"G1724","hebrew":"ἐνάλιος","translit":"enálios","english":"sea creature"},{"strongs":"G1150","hebrew":"δαμάζω","translit":"damázō","english":"tamed"},{"strongs":"G442","hebrew":"ἀνθρώπινος","translit":"anthrṓpinos","english":"mankind"}]},{"v":8,"text":"but nobody can tame the tongue. It is a restless evil, full of deadly poison.","understanding":"“but nobody can tame the tongue” — not ‘nobody does’ but ‘nobody can’: no human can do it alone. ἀκατάστατον κακόν, “It is a restless evil” — ἀκατάστατος, the same word as the ‘unstable’ double-minded man (1:8): never at rest. κακός, from the evil family: evil in its very nature. μεστὴ ἰοῦ θανατηφόρου, “full of deadly poison” — ἰός, venom, as of a snake (the same word is ‘rust’ in 5:3).","words":[{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G1150","hebrew":"δαμάζω","translit":"damázō","english":"tame"},{"strongs":"G182","hebrew":"ἀκατάστατος","translit":"akatástatos","english":"restless"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G3324","hebrew":"μεστός","translit":"mestós","english":"full"},{"strongs":"G2287","hebrew":"θανατήφορος","translit":"thanatḗphoros","english":"deadly"},{"strongs":"G2447","hebrew":"ἰός","translit":"iós","english":"poison"}]},{"v":9,"text":"With it we bless our God and Father, and with it we curse men, who are made in the image of God.","understanding":"ἐν αὐτῇ εὐλογοῦμεν τὸν κύριον καὶ πατέρα, “With it we bless our God and Father” — εὐλογέω, to speak well of. “and with it we curse men” — καταράομαι, to call down evil on someone. “who are made in the image of God” — καθ᾽ ὁμοίωσιν θεοῦ, ‘according to the likeness of God’. ὁμοίωσις, from the image family, is the word of Genesis 1:26 in the Greek Old Testament: ‘in our image (εἰκών), after our likeness (ὁμοίωσις)’. To curse a person is to curse the likeness of the God you have just blessed.","words":[{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"bless"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G2672","hebrew":"καταράομαι","translit":"kataráomai","english":"curse"},{"strongs":"G3669","hebrew":"ὁμοίωσις","translit":"homoíōsis","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Out of the same mouth comes blessing and cursing. My brothers, these things ought not to be so.","understanding":"“Out of the same mouth comes blessing and cursing.” εὐλογία and κατάρα, praise and imprecation. “My brothers, these things ought not to be so” — χρή, it is fitting, it must be; the only use of this word in the New Testament. James does not say ‘this is understandable’. He says it must not be.","words":[{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"},{"strongs":"G2671","hebrew":"κατάρα","translit":"katára","english":"cursing"},{"strongs":"G5534","hebrew":"χρή","translit":"chrḗ","english":"ought"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Does a spring send out from the same opening fresh and bitter water?","understanding":"“Does a spring send out from the same opening fresh and bitter water?” — πηγή, a spring; ὀπή, its opening in the rock; βρύω, to gush out; γλυκύς, sweet; πικρός, bitter, brackish. In a dry land everyone knew their springs, and no spring gave both.","words":[{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"spring"},{"strongs":"G3692","hebrew":"ὀπή","translit":"opḗ","english":"opening"},{"strongs":"G1032","hebrew":"βρύω","translit":"brýō","english":"send out"},{"strongs":"G1099","hebrew":"γλυκύς","translit":"glykýs","english":"fresh"},{"strongs":"G4089","hebrew":"πικρός","translit":"pikrós","english":"bitter"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Can a fig tree, my brothers, yield olives, or a vine figs? Thus no spring yields both salt water and fresh water.","understanding":"“Can a fig tree, my brothers, yield olives, or a vine figs?” — συκῆ, ἐλαία, ἄμπελος, the three great crops of Israel. What a tree is determines what it produces. “Thus no spring yields both salt water and fresh water” — ἁλυκός, salty. Speech reveals the source; Jesus said, ‘By their fruits you will know them.’","words":[{"strongs":"G4808","hebrew":"συκῆ","translit":"sykē","english":"fig tree"},{"strongs":"G1636","hebrew":"ἐλαία","translit":"elaía","english":"olives"},{"strongs":"G288","hebrew":"ἄμπελος","translit":"ámpelos","english":"vine"},{"strongs":"G4810","hebrew":"σῦκον","translit":"sŷkon","english":"figs"},{"strongs":"G252","hebrew":"ἁλυκός","translit":"halykós","english":"salt"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G1099","hebrew":"γλυκύς","translit":"glykýs","english":"fresh"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Who is wise and understanding among you? Let him show by his good conduct that his deeds are done in gentleness of wisdom.","understanding":"τίς σοφὸς καὶ ἐπιστήμων ἐν ὑμῖν, “Who is wise and understanding among you?” — two words from the wisdom family: σοφός, wise in God's ways, and ἐπιστήμων, the knowledgeable expert. This links back to verse 1: those who want to teach. “Let him show by his good conduct” — δείκνυμι, the ‘show me’ of 2:18; καλός, from the good family: fine, attractive conduct; ἀναστροφή, way of life. “that his deeds are done in gentleness of wisdom” — ἔργα, from the work family, and πραΰτης, from the humble family: meekness, the humility of 1:21. Wisdom, like faith, is proved by works.","words":[{"strongs":"G4680","hebrew":"σοφός","translit":"sophós","english":"wise"},{"strongs":"G1990","hebrew":"ἐπιστήμων","translit":"epistḗmōn","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"conduct"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"},{"strongs":"G4240","hebrew":"πραΰτης","translit":"praÿtēs","english":"gentleness"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"}]},{"v":14,"text":"But if you have bitter jealousy and selfish ambition in your heart, don’t boast and don’t lie against the truth.","understanding":"“But if you have bitter jealousy and selfish ambition in your heart” — ζῆλος πικρός, bitter zeal: the heat of rivalry, the same ‘bitter’ as the spring of verse 11. ἐριθεία, originally a hired worker's self-seeking, then party spirit and selfish ambition. “don’t boast and don’t lie against the truth” — κατακαυχάομαι, the word used in 2:13 of mercy triumphing, here of pride boasting; ψεύδομαι, to lie. Claiming wisdom while nursing rivalry is a lie against the truth.","words":[{"strongs":"G4089","hebrew":"πικρός","translit":"pikrós","english":"bitter"},{"strongs":"G2205","hebrew":"ζῆλος","translit":"zēlos","english":"jealousy"},{"strongs":"G2052","hebrew":"ἐριθεία","translit":"eritheía","english":"selfish ambition"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G2620","hebrew":"κατακαυχάομαι","translit":"katakaucháomai","english":"boast"},{"strongs":"G5574","hebrew":"ψεύδομαι","translit":"pseúdomai","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":15,"text":"This wisdom is not that which comes down from above, but is earthly, sensual, and demonic.","understanding":"“This wisdom is not that which comes down from above” — σοφία, from the wisdom family, but ἄνωθεν κατερχομένη, the language of 1:17, where every good gift comes down from above. This wisdom has another source. Three steps downward: ἐπίγειος, “earthly” — belonging to this world; ψυχικός, “sensual” — natural, unspiritual, the life of the soul without the Spirit (1 Corinthians 2:14); δαιμονιώδης, “demonic” — demon-like, and demon-inspired. Clever, but from below.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G509","hebrew":"ἄνωθεν","translit":"ánōthen","english":"from above"},{"strongs":"G2718","hebrew":"κατέρχομαι","translit":"katérchomai","english":"comes down"},{"strongs":"G1919","hebrew":"ἐπίγειος","translit":"epígeios","english":"earthly"},{"strongs":"G5591","hebrew":"ψυχικός","translit":"psychikós","english":"sensual"},{"strongs":"G1141","hebrew":"δαιμονιώδης","translit":"daimoniṓdēs","english":"demonic"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For where jealousy and selfish ambition are, there is confusion and every evil deed.","understanding":"“For where jealousy and selfish ambition are, there is confusion and every evil deed.” ἀκαταστασία, disorder, upheaval — the noun of the ‘unstable’ (1:8) and ‘restless’ (3:8). φαῦλος, from the evil family: worthless, base, vile — not κακός (evil in nature, v. 8) or πονηρός (actively harmful, 2:4), but cheap and shoddy evil. πρᾶγμα, a deed, a practice.","words":[{"strongs":"G2205","hebrew":"ζῆλος","translit":"zēlos","english":"jealousy"},{"strongs":"G2052","hebrew":"ἐριθεία","translit":"eritheía","english":"selfish ambition"},{"strongs":"G181","hebrew":"ἀκαταστασία","translit":"akatastasía","english":"confusion"},{"strongs":"G5337","hebrew":"φαῦλος","translit":"phaûlos","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G4229","hebrew":"πρᾶγμα","translit":"prâgma","english":"deed"}]},{"v":17,"text":"But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy.","understanding":"“But the wisdom that is from above is first pure” — σοφία, from the wisdom family; ἁγνός, clean, free from mixture. πρῶτον, first: purity before everything. Then seven qualities: εἰρηνική, “peaceful”; ἐπιεικής, “gentle” — reasonable, yielding, not insisting on one's rights; εὐπειθής, “reasonable” — easy to persuade, open to correction; μεστὴ ἐλέους καὶ καρπῶν ἀγαθῶν, “full of mercy and good fruits” — ἔλεος, from the mercy family; ἀγαθός, from the good family: good in its usefulness, compared with the καλός of verse 13; ἀδιάκριτος, “without partiality” — undivided, from the same root as the doubter (1:6) and the favouritism (2:4); ἀνυπόκριτος, “without hypocrisy” — sincere, not playing a part. It reads like a portrait of Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G509","hebrew":"ἄνωθεν","translit":"ánōthen","english":"from above"},{"strongs":"G53","hebrew":"ἁγνός","translit":"hagnós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G1516","hebrew":"εἰρηνικός","translit":"eirēnikós","english":"peaceful"},{"strongs":"G1933","hebrew":"ἐπιεικής","translit":"epieikḗs","english":"gentle"},{"strongs":"G2138","hebrew":"εὐπειθής","translit":"eupeithḗs","english":"reasonable"},{"strongs":"G3324","hebrew":"μεστός","translit":"mestós","english":"full"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruits"},{"strongs":"G87","hebrew":"ἀδιάκριτος","translit":"adiákritos","english":"without partiality"},{"strongs":"G505","hebrew":"ἀνυπόκριτος","translit":"anypókritos","english":"without hypocrisy"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Now the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those who make peace.","understanding":"“Now the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those who make peace.” καρπὸς δικαιοσύνης, the harvest that righteousness produces; σπείρω, to sow. Peacemakers sow in peace, and they reap righteous lives — their own and others'. Jesus called them blessed and children of God.","words":[{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruit"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G4687","hebrew":"σπείρω","translit":"speírō","english":"sown"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Bit and the Rudder","verseRef":"v. 3-4","image":"Picture a war horse, strong enough to kill a man with one kick, standing quietly while a rider turns it left and right with a small strip of metal in its mouth. Then picture a cargo ship in a storm, waves breaking over its bow, wind roaring in its sails — and at the back a single sailor leaning on a narrow wooden oar, keeping the whole vessel on course.","meaning":"“Indeed, we put bits into the horses’ mouths so that they may obey us, and we guide their whole body” (v. 3). “Behold, the ships also, though they are so big and are driven by fierce winds, are yet guided by a very small rudder, wherever the pilot desires” (v. 4). Your tongue is small, but it is steering your life — your relationships, your reputation, your witness, your future. The question is not whether your life is being steered, but whose hand is on the rudder."},{"title":"The Forest Fire","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture a dry hillside at the end of summer, the grass brown and brittle. A traveller drops a single glowing ember. A thread of smoke rises, then a small flame, then the wind catches it. Within an hour the whole valley is burning, villages are fleeing, and the sky is black with smoke. All of it from one spark.","meaning":"“See how a small fire can spread to a large forest! And the tongue is a fire” (vv. 5–6). One rumour can destroy a reputation; one careless sentence can split a church; one cruel word can scar a child for life. And James says the fire is “set on fire by Gehenna” (v. 6) — the enemy loves to light it. Before you speak, ask what you are about to set alight."},{"title":"The Two Springs","verseRef":"v. 9-12","image":"Picture a traveller in the desert reaching a spring. He kneels and drinks — and the water is sweet. The next day he comes back to the same spring and drinks again — and it is bitter, salty, undrinkable. He would never trust that spring again. Then picture a believer in church singing praise with tears, and in the car park afterwards cursing another driver.","meaning":"“With it we bless our God and Father, and with it we curse men, who are made in the image of God” (v. 9). “Out of the same mouth comes blessing and cursing. My brothers, these things ought not to be so” (v. 10). The mouth reveals the spring. If bitter water flows, there is bitterness at the source. The answer is not just to guard the mouth but to have the spring itself made new."},{"title":"Two Gardens of Wisdom","verseRef":"v. 13-18","image":"Picture two gardens side by side. In the first, everything is competition: every plant grows tall by choking its neighbours, thorns spread, and the ground is torn up by roots fighting for space. In the second, the plants are watered, pruned and spaced; fruit hangs heavy on the branches; and the gardener moves quietly among them, planting seeds in calm soil.","meaning":"“For where jealousy and selfish ambition are, there is confusion and every evil deed” (v. 16). “But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy” (v. 17). “Now the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those who make peace” (v. 18). You can tell which wisdom is ruling a person — or a church — by the garden it produces."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"A Warning to Teachers (v. 1-2)","body":"Not many should become teachers, since teachers will be judged more strictly; we all stumble in many ways, and whoever does not stumble in speech is mature and able to bridle the whole body."},{"label":"The Power of the Tongue (v. 3-6)","body":"Like a bit in a horse's mouth or a rudder on a ship, the small tongue directs the whole; like a spark it can set a forest alight; it is a fire, a world of unrighteousness, staining the body and inflamed by hell."},{"label":"The Untameable Tongue (v. 7-12)","body":"Every kind of creature has been tamed, but no human can tame the tongue, a restless evil full of deadly poison; with it we bless God and curse people made in His likeness; a spring cannot give both sweet and bitter water, nor a fig tree olives."},{"label":"Two Kinds of Wisdom (v. 13-18)","body":"The wise person shows wisdom by good conduct done in meekness; bitter jealousy and selfish ambition reveal a wisdom that is earthly, unspiritual and demonic, producing disorder and evil; the wisdom from above is pure, peaceful, gentle, open to reason, full of mercy and good fruits, impartial and sincere, and peacemakers sow a harvest of righteousness."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Small Member, Great Power","paragraphs":["James has already mentioned the tongue twice: “let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak” (James 1:19), and “If anyone among you thinks himself to be religious while he doesn’t bridle his tongue … this man’s religion is worthless” (James 1:26). Now he gives it a whole chapter. He begins with teachers, because their work is words: “Let not many of you be teachers, my brothers, knowing that we will receive heavier judgment” (v. 1). Jesus said, “I tell you that every idle word that men speak, they will give account of it in the day of judgment” (Matthew 12:36). The more you speak in God's name, the more seriously He takes your words.","Then James widens the warning to everyone: “For in many things we all stumble. If anyone doesn’t stumble in word, the same is a perfect man, able to bridle the whole body also” (v. 2). The tongue is the test of maturity. It is the last thing to be brought under control, and once it is controlled, everything else follows. That is the point of the bit and the rudder (vv. 3–4): small things steer great things. A few words at the right moment can turn a life towards God; a few words at the wrong moment can wreck it.","The tongue also destroys. “And the tongue is a fire. The world of iniquity among our members is the tongue, which defiles the whole body, and sets on fire the course of nature, and is set on fire by Gehenna” (v. 6). That is one of the strongest sentences in the New Testament. James says the tongue carries the whole fallen world-system inside it — all the pride, lust, greed and hatred of a world without God — and it can stain your whole life from birth to death. Its fire is lit from hell itself. Gossip, slander, lies, flattery, harshness and boasting are not small sins. They are fires."]},{"heading":"No One Can Tame It — but God Can Change the Spring","paragraphs":["James's honesty is startling: “but nobody can tame the tongue. It is a restless evil, full of deadly poison” (v. 8). Humanity has tamed every kind of creature — beasts, birds, reptiles and sea creatures (v. 7) — but not its own mouth. Paul, quoting the Psalms, describes the human condition in the same terms: “Their throat is an open tomb. With their tongues they have used deceit” (Romans 3:13), “whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness” (Romans 3:14). No self-help programme can fully master the tongue.","Why not? Because the tongue is not the source of the problem; it is the outlet. Jesus said, “For out of the abundance of the heart, the mouth speaks” (Matthew 12:34), and “But the things which proceed out of the mouth come out of the heart, and they defile the man” (Matthew 15:18). That is why James moves to the spring and the tree (vv. 11–12). A spring gives the water that is in it; a fig tree gives figs. If cursing flows from the same mouth that blesses God, the problem is a divided heart — the double-mindedness of 1:8 again.","So James is not driving us to despair but to God. No human can tame the tongue, but God can make a new heart, and a new heart produces new speech. At Pentecost the Holy Spirit came as “Tongues like fire” (Acts 2:3) — heaven's fire answering hell's fire — and the disciples' tongues were filled with the praises of God. Paul gives the positive rule: “Let no corrupt speech proceed out of your mouth, but only what is good for building others up as the need may be, that it may give grace to those who hear” (Ephesians 4:29). The cure for a poisoned tongue is a cleansed spring, a Spirit-filled heart, and the daily practice of being “swift to hear, slow to speak” (James 1:19)."]},{"heading":"Two Wisdoms, Two Harvests","paragraphs":["Verse 13 returns to those who want to teach: “Who is wise and understanding among you? Let him show by his good conduct that his deeds are done in gentleness of wisdom.” As with faith in chapter 2, James insists that wisdom must be shown. Wisdom is not how much you know, how well you argue or how impressively you speak. It is how you live — and especially whether you live with meekness.","There is a false wisdom, and James describes it without mercy. Its roots are “bitter jealousy and selfish ambition” (v. 14). It may be clever, persuasive and successful, but its source is “earthly, sensual, and demonic” (v. 15) — it belongs to this world, it is driven by natural desires rather than the Spirit, and it is inspired by the devil. Its fruit is visible: “confusion and every evil deed” (v. 16). Paul lists the same things among the works of the flesh: “hatred, strife, jealousies, outbursts of anger, rivalries, divisions” (Galatians 5:20). Whenever a church is torn by rivalry, someone's wisdom is from below.","The true wisdom comes down from above, like every good and perfect gift (1:17), and it is the answer to the prayer of 1:5. James describes it in eight qualities: pure, peaceful, gentle, reasonable, merciful, fruitful, impartial and sincere (v. 17). It is strikingly close to Paul's fruit of the Spirit: “love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith” (Galatians 5:22), “gentleness, and self-control” (Galatians 5:23). And it is a portrait of Christ Himself, who “was made to us wisdom from God” (1 Corinthians 1:30). Its harvest is righteousness, sown by peacemakers (v. 18). Jesus said, “Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called children of God” (Matthew 5:9)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Words, Fruit, and the Wisdom of Christ","paragraphs":["James's teaching on the tongue is Jesus' teaching. Jesus said, “For by your words you will be justified, and by your words you will be condemned” (Matthew 12:37). He taught that a tree is known by its fruit: “By their fruits you will know them. Do you gather grapes from thorns, or figs from thistles?” (Matthew 7:16), and “Even so, every good tree produces good fruit; but the corrupt tree produces evil fruit” (Matthew 7:17). James's fig tree that cannot produce olives (v. 12) is the same lesson. Peter, who knew the tongue's dangers from bitter experience, quotes Psalm 34: “He who would love life, and see good days, let him keep his tongue from evil, and his lips from speaking deceit” (1 Peter 3:10).","The curse on people made in God's likeness (v. 9) reaches back to Genesis 1:26 and forward to the new creation. Paul says the believer “put on the new man, who is being renewed in knowledge after the image of his Creator” (Colossians 3:10). To curse another person is to attack the image God is restoring. To bless them is to honour Him.","Above all, the wisdom from above has a name. Paul writes that “Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God” (1 Corinthians 1:24), the one “in whom are all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge hidden” (Colossians 2:3). Every quality of James 3:17 is fulfilled in Him: pure, peaceful, gentle, open, merciful, fruitful, impartial and sincere. To ask God for wisdom (James 1:5) is ultimately to ask for more of Christ to be formed in you. The fruit of righteousness sown in peace (v. 18) echoes Hebrews: discipline “yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been exercised thereby” (Hebrews 12:11)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Tongue","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","strongs":"G1100","verses":"v. 5, 6, 8; 1:26","insight":"Strong's: “the tongue; by implication, a language”. James uses it five times, more than any other New Testament letter except 1 Corinthians, where it means languages. In James it is the organ of speech, and it carries a whole world of good and evil. The same word is used at Pentecost for the tongues of fire and for the new languages the Spirit gave. The tongue set on fire by Gehenna (v. 6) can become a tongue set on fire by the Spirit (Acts 2:3–4). What lights your tongue determines what it burns."},{"english":"Gehenna","hebrew":"γέεννα","translit":"géenna","strongs":"G1067","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “valley of (the son of) Hinnom.” The valley south of Jerusalem where kings of Judah once burned their children in sacrifice to Molech, later a place of burning refuse. Jesus used it as the name of hell, the place of final judgement. Outside the Gospels this is the only time it appears in the New Testament. James's point is that the destructive fire of the tongue has its source in the realm of evil itself. Gossip and slander are not merely human failings; they serve the enemy's purposes."},{"english":"Image / likeness","hebrew":"ὁμοίωσις","translit":"homoíōsis","strongs":"G3669","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “assimilation, i.e. resemblance.” ὁμοίωσις is the word the Greek Old Testament uses in Genesis 1:26, ‘after our likeness’. The WEB translates it ‘image’ here. It differs from εἰκών, the image or representation itself, by stressing resemblance. This study adds it to the image family. Every human being still bears God's likeness, even after the fall. That is why cursing people is so serious: it is an attack on someone who resembles God."},{"english":"Selfish ambition","hebrew":"ἐριθεία","translit":"eritheía","strongs":"G2052 (jealousy ζῆλος, zēlos, G2205)","verses":"v. 14, 16","insight":"Strong's: “properly, intrigue, i.e. (by implication) faction.” The word once described people who worked for hire and then came to mean those who campaign for themselves — the politics of self-promotion, party spirit and rivalry. It is paired with ζῆλος, which can be good zeal or bad jealousy; here it is ‘bitter’. Together they describe the person who wants to be first, cannot bear another's success, and gathers followers. Paul uses the same word: ‘doing nothing through rivalry’ (Philippians 2:3)."},{"english":"Gentle","hebrew":"ἐπιεικής","translit":"epieikḗs","strongs":"G1933","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “appropriate, i.e. (by implication) mild.” ἐπιεικής is one of the hardest words in the New Testament to translate. It describes someone who does not insist on the letter of their rights, who yields when yielding is right, and who is fair-minded and gracious rather than rigid. Paul uses the noun of ‘the gentleness of Christ’ (2 Corinthians 10:1), and requires it of overseers (1 Timothy 3:3). It is the mark of wisdom that does not need to win every argument."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is putting His finger on your mouth — and tracing what comes out of it back to your heart.","items":[{"title":"What Is Steering Your Life?","body":"Your words are a rudder. The Spirit wants you to see where they have been steering you — towards peace or conflict, towards faith or fear, towards love or bitterness. Listen to yourself for a day. What are you saying about God, about yourself, about others, about your future? “If anyone doesn’t stumble in word, the same is a perfect man, able to bridle the whole body also” (v. 2). What would change if your words were in His hands?"},{"title":"Put Out the Fire","body":"Is there a fire burning because of something you said — a rumour you passed on, a harsh word, a careless criticism, a lie? “See how a small fire can spread to a large forest!” (v. 5). The Spirit is not condemning you; He is calling you to put it out. That may mean going back to someone and apologising, correcting what you said, or simply stopping a conversation. Which fire does He want you to deal with first?"},{"title":"Which Wisdom Are You Using?","body":"In the decisions you are making — at work, in ministry, in your family — are you driven by “bitter jealousy and selfish ambition” (v. 14), or by the wisdom that is “first pure, then peaceful” (v. 17)? Selfish ambition can wear religious clothes. Ask the Spirit to show you where you are competing instead of serving, and to give you the wisdom that comes down from above."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Spark Check","body":"Have your words started any fires this month — gossip, sarcasm, complaint, harsh criticism (vv. 5–6)? Name one conversation where you should have stayed silent."},{"title":"The Spring Check","body":"Does the same mouth that praises God on Sunday curse, mock or belittle people during the week (vv. 9–10)? Who do you speak about in ways you would never speak about God?"},{"title":"The Ambition Check","body":"Is there anyone whose success you find hard to celebrate (v. 14)? Where are you pushing for position, recognition or control? Measure your wisdom against the eight qualities of verse 17."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Day of Guarded Speech","body":"Choose one full day this week. Before you speak in any conversation, ask three silent questions: Is it true? Is it necessary? Is it kind and building up (Ephesians 4:29)? If the answer to any is no, stay silent or change your words. In the evening, write down what you noticed about your tongue and pray verse 2 over yourself."},{"title":"Bless Those You Have Cursed","body":"Think of someone you have criticised, mocked or spoken against. This week, deliberately speak a blessing over them: pray for them by name, thank God for His likeness in them, and if possible say something genuinely encouraging to them or about them to others. Replace the bitter water with sweet."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I bless You with my mouth, and I confess that with the same mouth I have spoken against people made in Your likeness. Forgive me. These things ought not to be so. I admit that I cannot tame my tongue by my own strength; it is restless and quick to spark fires. So I ask You to change the spring. Make my heart pure, so that what flows from my mouth is sweet and not bitter. Put Your bit in my mouth and take hold of the rudder of my life. Where my words have set fires, give me courage to put them out. Holy Spirit, set my tongue on fire with heaven's fire, as You did at Pentecost, so that I speak words of grace that build others up. Deliver me from bitter jealousy and selfish ambition, and from every wisdom that is earthly, unspiritual and demonic. Give me the wisdom that comes down from above — pure, peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality and without hypocrisy. Make me a peacemaker who sows in peace and reaps a harvest of righteousness. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Peter's Tongue in the Courtyard","ref":"Matthew 26:69-75","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 6, 10","summary":"Peter, who had boasted that he would never deny Jesus, warmed himself by a fire in the high priest's courtyard while his Lord was on trial. A servant girl said, ‘You were with Jesus.’ Peter denied it. Another said the same, and he denied it with an oath. Finally, when people recognised his accent, “Then he began to curse and to swear, ‘I don’t know the man!’ Immediately the rooster crowed” (Matthew 26:74). The same mouth that had confessed ‘You are the Christ’ now cursed. Peter went out and wept bitterly. Yet after the resurrection the same tongue preached at Pentecost and three thousand were saved. The tongue nobody can tame was transformed by the Spirit."},{"title":"Tongues Like Fire","ref":"Acts 2:1-41","relatedVerses":"v. 6, 17-18","summary":"On the day of Pentecost the disciples were gathered together when a sound like a rushing wind filled the house. “Tongues like fire appeared and were distributed to them, and one sat on each of them” (Acts 2:3). “They were all filled with the Holy Spirit, and began to speak with other languages, as the Spirit gave them the ability to speak” (Acts 2:4). James says the tongue is a fire set alight by hell; at Pentecost God set tongues alight with heaven's fire. The result was not destruction but praise, preaching and a harvest of three thousand. The same organ that spreads destruction can, under the Spirit, spread the gospel."},{"title":"The Sons of Thunder Want Fire","ref":"Luke 9:51-56","relatedVerses":"v. 13-18","summary":"When a Samaritan village refused to welcome Jesus, two disciples — James the son of Zebedee (not the writer of this letter) and his brother John — were furious. “Lord, do you want us to command fire to come down from the sky, and destroy them, just as Elijah did?” (Luke 9:54). Jesus nicknamed them Boanerges, ‘Sons of Thunder’. “But he turned and rebuked them” (Luke 9:55). Their zeal sounded biblical, but it was bitter and proud. Jesus simply went to another village. It is a picture of the two wisdoms: one that wants to call down fire, and one that is peaceable, gentle and full of mercy. John, the Son of Thunder, later became the apostle of love."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"James","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Friendship with the World, and the Grace Given to the Humble","themeTitle":"“Draw Near to God, and He Will Draw Near to You”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Where do wars and fightings among you come from? Don’t they come from your pleasures that war in your members?","understanding":"πόθεν πόλεμοι καὶ πόθεν μάχαι ἐν ὑμῖν, “Where do wars and fightings among you come from?” — πόλεμος, a war, a long campaign; μάχη, a battle, a single fight. Church conflict described in military language. James answers with a question: ἐκ τῶν ἡδονῶν ὑμῶν, “from your pleasures” — ἡδονή, pleasure, the root of ‘hedonism’ — τῶν στρατευομένων ἐν τοῖς μέλεσιν ὑμῶν, “that war in your members.” The war outside starts with a war inside.","words":[{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"wars"},{"strongs":"G3163","hebrew":"μάχη","translit":"máchē","english":"fightings"},{"strongs":"G2237","hebrew":"ἡδονή","translit":"hēdonḗ","english":"pleasures"},{"strongs":"G4754","hebrew":"στρατεύομαι","translit":"strateúomai","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G3196","hebrew":"μέλος","translit":"mélos","english":"members"}]},{"v":2,"text":"You lust, and don’t have. You murder and covet, and can’t obtain. You fight and make war. You don’t have, because you don’t ask.","understanding":"“You lust, and don’t have.” ἐπιθυμέω, to set the heart on. “You murder and covet” — φονεύω and ζηλόω: jealous desire that leads to destroying others, whether literally or, as Jesus taught, by hatred in the heart. ἐπιτυχεῖν, to obtain. “You fight and make war” — μάχομαι and πολεμέω, the verbs of verse 1. And the tragic irony: “You don’t have, because you don’t ask” — αἰτέω, from the pray family. They grab instead of asking.","words":[{"strongs":"G1937","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμέω","translit":"epithyméō","english":"lust"},{"strongs":"G5407","hebrew":"φονεύω","translit":"phoneúō","english":"murder"},{"strongs":"G2206","hebrew":"ζηλόω","translit":"zēlóō","english":"covet"},{"strongs":"G2013","hebrew":"ἐπιτυγχάνω","translit":"epitynchánō","english":"obtain"},{"strongs":"G3164","hebrew":"μάχομαι","translit":"máchomai","english":"fight"},{"strongs":"G4170","hebrew":"πολεμέω","translit":"poleméō","english":"make war"},{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"}]},{"v":3,"text":"You ask, and don’t receive, because you ask with wrong motives, so that you may spend it on your pleasures.","understanding":"“You ask, and don’t receive” — αἰτέω again, from the pray family. When they do pray, they pray κακῶς, “with wrong motives” — from the evil family: the adverb of κακός, badly. ἵνα ἐν ταῖς ἡδοναῖς ὑμῶν δαπανήσητε, “so that you may spend it on your pleasures” — δαπανάω, to spend, to squander, as the prodigal son spent everything. They treat God as a supplier for their desires.","words":[{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G2560","hebrew":"κακῶς","translit":"kakōs","english":"wrong motives"},{"strongs":"G1159","hebrew":"δαπανάω","translit":"dapanáō","english":"spend"},{"strongs":"G2237","hebrew":"ἡδονή","translit":"hēdonḗ","english":"pleasures"}]},{"v":4,"text":"You adulterers and adulteresses, don’t you know that friendship with the world is hostility toward God? Whoever therefore wants to be a friend of the world makes himself an enemy of God.","understanding":"μοιχαλίδες, “You adulterers and adulteresses” — the oldest manuscripts read only μοιχαλίδες, ‘adulteresses’, the language of the prophets for God's people, His bride, being unfaithful (Hosea 3:1). The WEB follows manuscripts that add the masculine. ἡ φιλία τοῦ κόσμου ἔχθρα τοῦ θεοῦ ἐστιν, “friendship with the world is hostility toward God” — φιλία, from the love family: warm affection and loyalty; κόσμος, from the world family: society organised without God, not the created earth (γῆ). ἔχθρα, hostility. “Whoever therefore wants to be a friend of the world” — φίλος, the word used of Abraham, the friend of God (2:23) — “makes himself an enemy of God.” You cannot be a friend of both.","words":[{"strongs":"G3428","hebrew":"μοιχαλίς","translit":"moichalís","english":"adulteresses"},{"strongs":"G5373","hebrew":"φιλία","translit":"philía","english":"friendship"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2189","hebrew":"ἔχθρα","translit":"échthra","english":"hostility"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"wants"},{"strongs":"G5384","hebrew":"φίλος","translit":"phílos","english":"friend"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemy"},{"strongs":"G2525","hebrew":"καθίστημι","translit":"kathístēmi","english":"makes himself"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Or do you think that the Scripture says in vain, “The Spirit who lives in us yearns jealously”?","understanding":"“Or do you think that the Scripture says in vain” — κενῶς, emptily. “The Spirit who lives in us yearns jealously” — no Old Testament verse says these exact words; James is summarising Scripture's teaching about God's jealous love. The Greek can be read two ways: ‘God jealously yearns for the spirit He made to live in us’ (the jealousy of a faithful husband for his bride, Exodus 34:14), or ‘the spirit that lives in us yearns enviously’ (the human spirit's sinful desires). The flow from verse 4, ‘adulteresses’, and verse 6, ‘he gives more grace’, favours the first: God loves His people with a jealous love and will not share them. ἐπιποθέω, to long for intensely; φθόνος, jealousy.","words":[{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G2761","hebrew":"κενῶς","translit":"kenōs","english":"in vain"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G1971","hebrew":"ἐπιποθέω","translit":"epipothéō","english":"yearns"},{"strongs":"G5355","hebrew":"φθόνος","translit":"phthónos","english":"jealously"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But he gives more grace. Therefore it says, “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble.”","understanding":"μείζονα δὲ δίδωσιν χάριν, “But he gives more grace” — greater grace: grace greater than the world's pull and God's jealousy combined. The proof text is Proverbs 3:34 in the Greek Old Testament, which Peter also quotes: “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble.” ἀντιτάσσεται, from the resist family: a military word, to draw up battle lines against. ὑπερήφανος, proud — literally ‘appearing above’ others. ταπεινός, from the humble family: low. God fights the proud and floods the humble with grace.","words":[{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"more"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gives"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G498","hebrew":"ἀντιτάσσομαι","translit":"antitássomai","english":"resists"},{"strongs":"G5244","hebrew":"ὑπερήφανος","translit":"hyperḗphanos","english":"proud"},{"strongs":"G5011","hebrew":"ταπεινός","translit":"tapeinós","english":"humble"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Be subject therefore to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.","understanding":"ὑποτάγητε οὖν τῷ θεῷ, “Be subject therefore to God” — ὑποτάσσω, to rank yourself under: surrender, the opposite of pride. Then, ἀντίστητε δὲ τῷ διαβόλῳ, “Resist the devil” — ἀνθίστημι, the second word of the resist family: stand your ground against an attack. God ἀντιτάσσεται the proud (v. 6); you ἀνθίστημι the devil. καὶ φεύξεται ἀφ᾽ ὑμῶν, “and he will flee from you” — φεύγω, to run away. The devil is not stronger than a submitted believer.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"Be subject"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G436","hebrew":"ἀνθίστημι","translit":"anthístēmi","english":"Resist"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"flee"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you. Cleanse your hands, you sinners; and purify your hearts, you double-minded.","understanding":"ἐγγίσατε τῷ θεῷ, καὶ ἐγγιεῖ ὑμῖν, “Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you” — ἐγγίζω, the priestly word for approaching God, which Hebrews uses for drawing near through Christ (Hebrews 7:19). “Cleanse your hands, you sinners” — καθαρίζω, the outward life; ἁμαρτωλός, from the sin family: those whose lives are marked by sin. “and purify your hearts, you double-minded” — ἁγνίζω, the inward life; δίψυχος, the two-souled person of 1:8. Hands and heart, deeds and desires, both made clean.","words":[{"strongs":"G1448","hebrew":"ἐγγίζω","translit":"engízō","english":"Draw near"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"Cleanse"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinners"},{"strongs":"G48","hebrew":"ἁγνίζω","translit":"hagnízō","english":"purify"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G1374","hebrew":"δίψυχος","translit":"dípsychos","english":"double-minded"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Lament, mourn, and weep. Let your laughter be turned to mourning, and your joy to gloom.","understanding":"ταλαιπωρήσατε καὶ πενθήσατε καὶ κλαύσατε, “Lament, mourn, and weep” — ταλαιπωρέω, to feel your wretchedness; πενθέω, to grieve as for the dead; κλαίω, to weep aloud. “Let your laughter be turned to mourning” — γέλως, the careless laughter of people who do not take sin seriously, as in Jesus' woe on those who laugh now. “and your joy to gloom” — κατήφεια, a downcast face, eyes lowered in shame. This is not a command to be miserable forever, but to grieve over sin until it is gone.","words":[{"strongs":"G5003","hebrew":"ταλαιπωρέω","translit":"talaipōréō","english":"Lament"},{"strongs":"G3996","hebrew":"πενθέω","translit":"penthéō","english":"mourn"},{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weep"},{"strongs":"G1071","hebrew":"γέλως","translit":"gélōs","english":"laughter"},{"strongs":"G3344","hebrew":"μεταστρέφω","translit":"metastréphō","english":"turned"},{"strongs":"G3997","hebrew":"πένθος","translit":"pénthos","english":"mourning"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G2726","hebrew":"κατήφεια","translit":"katḗpheia","english":"gloom"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he will exalt you.","understanding":"ταπεινώθητε ἐνώπιον κυρίου, “Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord” — ταπεινόω, from the humble family: take the low place, before His face. καὶ ὑψώσει ὑμᾶς, “and he will exalt you” — ὑψόω, to lift high. The humbled poor man of 1:9 boasts in his ὕψος. God lifts those who bow; Jesus said the same, and lived it (Philippians 2:8–9).","words":[{"strongs":"G5013","hebrew":"ταπεινόω","translit":"tapeinóō","english":"Humble yourselves"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in the sight of"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G5312","hebrew":"ὑψόω","translit":"hypsóō","english":"exalt"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Don’t speak against one another, brothers. He who speaks against a brother and judges his brother, speaks against the law and judges the law. But if you judge the law, you are not a doer of the law, but a judge.","understanding":"μὴ καταλαλεῖτε ἀλλήλων, “Don’t speak against one another, brothers” — καταλαλέω, to talk down, to slander. This is the tongue of chapter 3 again. “He who speaks against a brother and judges his brother, speaks against the law and judges the law” — the royal law says love your neighbour (2:8); to slander him is to say that law does not apply to you. “But if you judge the law, you are not a doer of the law, but a judge” — ποιητής, the ‘doer’ of 1:22; κριτής, the ‘judge’ of 2:4. You have climbed onto the bench. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G2635","hebrew":"καταλαλέω","translit":"katalaléō","english":"speak against"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judges"},{"strongs":"G3551","hebrew":"νόμος","translit":"nómos","english":"law"},{"strongs":"G4163","hebrew":"ποιητής","translit":"poiētḗs","english":"doer"},{"strongs":"G2923","hebrew":"κριτής","translit":"kritḗs","english":"judge"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Only one is the lawgiver, who is able to save and to destroy. But who are you to judge another?","understanding":"εἷς ἐστιν ὁ νομοθέτης καὶ κριτής, “Only one is the lawgiver” — νομοθέτης, legislator. ὁ δυνάμενος σῶσαι καὶ ἀπολέσαι, “who is able to save and to destroy” — σῴζω, from the heal family, to rescue and make whole; δύναμαι, from the power family: the capability to act, as distinct from ἐξουσία (the authority) or κράτος (sheer might). Only God has the power to decide a person's eternity. “But who are you to judge another?” — τὸν πλησίον, your neighbour: the one the royal law commands you to love. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G3550","hebrew":"νομοθέτης","translit":"nomothétēs","english":"lawgiver"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"destroy"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G4139","hebrew":"πλησίον","translit":"plēsíon","english":"another"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Come now, you who say, “Today or tomorrow let’s go into this city, and spend a year there, trade, and make a profit.”","understanding":"ἄγε νῦν, “Come now” — a sharp call to attention. “Today or tomorrow let’s go into this city, and spend a year there, trade, and make a profit.” Three words from the time family: σήμερον, today; αὔριον, tomorrow; ἐνιαυτός, a year. They plan the when, the where, the how long and the profit — everything except God. ἐμπορεύομαι, to travel as a merchant; κερδαίνω, to gain.","words":[{"strongs":"G4594","hebrew":"σήμερον","translit":"sḗmeron","english":"Today"},{"strongs":"G839","hebrew":"αὔριον","translit":"aúrion","english":"tomorrow"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"year"},{"strongs":"G1710","hebrew":"ἐμπορεύομαι","translit":"emporeúomai","english":"trade"},{"strongs":"G2770","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","english":"make a profit"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Whereas you don’t know what your life will be like tomorrow. For what is your life? For you are a vapor, that appears for a little time, and then vanishes away.","understanding":"οἵτινες οὐκ ἐπίστασθε τὸ τῆς αὔριον, “Whereas you don’t know what your life will be like tomorrow” — αὔριον, from the time family; ἐπίσταμαι, from the know family: to know as an expert knows, to understand; you are no expert on tomorrow. “For what is your life?” — ζωή, from the life family. ἀτμὶς γάρ ἐστε, “For you are a vapor” — ἀτμίς, a puff of steam or smoke, breath on a cold morning. Then a wordplay from the appear family: ἡ … φαινομένη, ἔπειτα καὶ ἀφανιζομένη, “that appears for a little time, and then vanishes away” — φαίνω, to appear, and ἀφανίζω, to disappear. ‘Time’ is added by the translators; the Greek simply says ‘for a little’.","words":[{"strongs":"G1987","hebrew":"ἐπίσταμαι","translit":"epístamai","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G839","hebrew":"αὔριον","translit":"aúrion","english":"tomorrow"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G822","hebrew":"ἀτμίς","translit":"atmís","english":"vapor"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"appears"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"little"},{"strongs":"G853","hebrew":"ἀφανίζω","translit":"aphanízō","english":"vanishes"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For you ought to say, “If the Lord wills, we will both live, and do this or that.”","understanding":"“For you ought to say, ‘If the Lord wills, we will both live, and do this or that.’” ἐὰν ὁ κύριος θελήσῃ — θέλω, to will, to decide. Not a magic phrase to add to plans, but a humble heart behind them. ζάω, from the life family: even staying alive is up to Him. Paul used the same words: “I will return again to you if God wills” (Acts 18:21).","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"wills"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"}]},{"v":16,"text":"But now you glory in your boasting. All such boasting is evil.","understanding":"“But now you glory in your boasting” — καυχάομαι, the ‘glory’ of 1:9, now misused; ἀλαζονεία, arrogant pretension, the swagger of someone claiming more than he has. “All such boasting is evil” — καύχησις, boasting; πονηρός, from the evil family: actively harmful, and in the singular the word used for ‘the evil one’. Boasting about tomorrow sides with him.","words":[{"strongs":"G2744","hebrew":"καυχάομαι","translit":"kaucháomai","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G212","hebrew":"ἀλαζονεία","translit":"alazoneía","english":"boasting"},{"strongs":"G2746","hebrew":"καύχησις","translit":"kaúchēsis","english":"boasting"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"}]},{"v":17,"text":"To him therefore who knows to do good, and doesn’t do it, to him it is sin.","understanding":"εἰδότι οὖν καλὸν ποιεῖν καὶ μὴ ποιοῦντι, ἁμαρτία αὐτῷ ἐστιν, “To him therefore who knows to do good, and doesn’t do it, to him it is sin.” εἴδω, from the know family (and the see family, since its root means ‘to see’): to know the facts; καλός, from the good family: the fine and fitting thing; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. Sin is not only doing wrong but failing to do right — the sin of omission. The whole letter in one verse: hearing and knowing without doing is sin.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The War Inside","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a church business meeting that has turned into a battlefield. Voices are raised; two factions glare across the room; people who once prayed together now refuse to speak. Then picture a camera zooming in — past the faces, past the words — into each person's chest, where another war is raging: wants fighting wants, pride fighting pride, desire grabbing for what it cannot have.","meaning":"“Where do wars and fightings among you come from? Don’t they come from your pleasures that war in your members?” (v. 1). The fights outside begin with the fights inside. James's answer is not to win the argument but to change the desire. And he adds a quiet rebuke: “You don’t have, because you don’t ask” (v. 2). What we grab for by fighting, God would often have given if we had asked."},{"title":"The Unfaithful Bride","verseRef":"v. 4-5","image":"Picture a wedding ring left on a bedside table in a stranger's house. A wife has slipped away from her husband, who has loved her faithfully, to spend the night with someone who promises excitement and flattery. At home the husband waits by the window, grieving — not with cold anger, but with a jealous, aching love.","meaning":"“You adulterers and adulteresses, don’t you know that friendship with the world is hostility toward God?” (v. 4). God's people are His bride. To love the world's values, pleasures and approval is spiritual adultery. And God's response is not indifference but jealousy: “The Spirit who lives in us yearns jealously” (v. 5). He wants all of you. And He offers more grace to bring you home."},{"title":"The Devil in Retreat","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture a soldier standing alone on a hill, his armour on, his sword drawn, his feet planted. Across the valley an enemy army approaches, roaring and confident. But the soldier does not move; behind him stands his King. The enemy reaches the foot of the hill, sees who stands with him — and turns and runs.","meaning":"“Be subject therefore to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you” (v. 7). The order matters: first submit, then resist. The devil does not flee from the proud, who stand alone, but from the humble, who stand under God. “Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you” (v. 8). Your strength in spiritual warfare is your nearness to God."},{"title":"The Morning Mist","verseRef":"v. 13-15","image":"Picture a cold morning in the hills. A merchant steps out of his house with his travel plans and his money bag, and his breath hangs in the air like a small white cloud. For a moment it is clearly visible. Then the sun rises over the hill, and the cloud is gone, and the valley mist with it. By noon no one could tell it was ever there.","meaning":"“For what is your life? For you are a vapor, that appears for a little time, and then vanishes away” (v. 14). Making plans is not wrong. Making them as if you controlled tomorrow is. “For you ought to say, ‘If the Lord wills, we will both live, and do this or that’” (v. 15). Every plan you make should be held in an open hand."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Source of Conflict (v. 1-3)","body":"Fights and quarrels come from desires at war within; people covet, murder and fight because they do not ask God, or they ask wrongly, to spend on their pleasures."},{"label":"Spiritual Adultery and Greater Grace (v. 4-6)","body":"Friendship with the world is hostility to God; God yearns jealously over His people and gives more grace, opposing the proud and giving grace to the humble."},{"label":"The Call to Return (v. 7-10)","body":"Submit to God, resist the devil and he will flee; draw near to God and He will draw near; cleanse hands and purify hearts; mourn over sin; humble yourselves before the Lord and He will lift you up."},{"label":"Do Not Judge (v. 11-12)","body":"Speaking against a brother is judging the law instead of doing it; there is only one Lawgiver and Judge, who can save and destroy."},{"label":"Do Not Boast About Tomorrow (v. 13-17)","body":"Those who plan trade and profit without God do not know what tomorrow holds, for life is a vapour; they should say, ‘If the Lord wills’; boasting is evil, and knowing the good and not doing it is sin."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Where Quarrels Come From","paragraphs":["James 3 ended with peace: “Now the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those who make peace” (James 3:18). James 4 begins with war. The believers were fighting — perhaps over position, money, leadership or doctrine — and James goes straight to the root: “Where do wars and fightings among you come from? Don’t they come from your pleasures that war in your members?” (v. 1). Paul describes the same inner battle: “For the flesh lusts against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh” (Galatians 5:17). When desires rule the heart, they will eventually fight other people's desires.","James traces the chain in verse 2: desire, frustration, violence. “You lust, and don’t have. You murder and covet, and can’t obtain.” The word ‘murder’ may be literal — some Jewish zealots of the time did kill for their cause — or it may be Jesus' sense, that hatred is murder in the heart. Either way, the result is war. And underneath it all is prayerlessness: “You don’t have, because you don’t ask” (v. 2). Or, when they do pray, their prayers are only another way of feeding the same desires: “You ask, and don’t receive, because you ask with wrong motives, so that you may spend it on your pleasures” (v. 3).","This is a searching diagnosis. Many church fights are not really about the issue on the table. They are about wanting, not getting, and refusing to take the want to God in honest prayer. The way out is not a better strategy but a changed heart — and that is where James goes next."]},{"heading":"Friendship with the World and the Jealous Love of God","paragraphs":["James's language now becomes shocking: “You adulterers and adulteresses” (v. 4). He is using the prophets' picture of Israel as God's unfaithful wife. To be a ‘friend of the world’ is not to love the world of people God loves (John 3:16), but to embrace the world's system of values — its pride, greed, lust and self-promotion. John says the same: “Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world. If anyone loves the world, the Father’s love isn’t in him” (1 John 2:15), “For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, isn’t the Father’s, but is the world’s” (1 John 2:16). James makes it starker: friendship with one is enmity with the other.","Verse 5 is one of the hardest verses in the letter. The words “The Spirit who lives in us yearns jealously” are not found word for word in the Old Testament, and the Greek can mean either that God jealously longs for the spirit He placed in us, or that the human spirit within us has envious longings. Both are true, but the context — spiritual adultery before it and greater grace after it — points to God's jealous love. God is not a casual partner. He loves His people with the burning, exclusive love of a faithful husband. That is not a threat but a comfort: He will not give you up.","And then comes the turning point of the chapter: “But he gives more grace” (v. 6). Whatever the world's pull, God's grace is greater. But that grace flows in only one direction: “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble” (v. 6). Pride does not just miss grace; it turns God into an opponent. Humility does not earn grace; it opens the door to it."]},{"heading":"Ten Commands for the Way Back","paragraphs":["Verses 7–10 contain ten commands in a row, all calling for urgent action: submit, resist, draw near, cleanse, purify, lament, mourn, weep, turn, humble yourselves. It is a call to repentance. It begins with surrender — “Be subject therefore to God” — and ends with surrender — “Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he will exalt you” (v. 10). Between them is the battle: “Resist the devil, and he will flee from you” (v. 7). Peter says the same: “Withstand him steadfast in your faith” (1 Peter 5:9).","The promise of verse 8 is one of the most precious in Scripture: “Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you.” God does not wait at a distance with folded arms. The moment you turn towards Him, He comes towards you, like the father running to the prodigal. But drawing near requires clean hands (what you do) and a pure heart (what you want). The double-minded person of 1:8 must become single-hearted. Hebrews gives the way: “let’s draw near with a true heart in fullness of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience” (Hebrews 10:22).","The call to mourn (v. 9) is not a call to permanent sadness. It is a call to take sin seriously, as Jesus said: “Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted” (Matthew 5:4). Grief over sin is the doorway to joy. And verse 10 sums up the whole gospel pattern. Jesus Himself lived it: “he humbled himself, becoming obedient to death, yes, the death of the cross” (Philippians 2:8), and “Therefore God also highly exalted him” (Philippians 2:9). The way up is down."]},{"heading":"Judging Others and Boasting About Tomorrow","paragraphs":["Two forms of pride close the chapter. The first is judging others: “Don’t speak against one another, brothers” (v. 11). To slander a brother is to set yourself above the law of love, as if it did not apply to you. “Only one is the lawgiver, who is able to save and to destroy. But who are you to judge another?” (v. 12). Jesus said, “Don’t judge, so that you won’t be judged” (Matthew 7:1), and Paul asks, “But you, why do you judge your brother?” (Romans 14:10). Discernment is needed in the church, but condemning, gossiping and tearing down are not discernment. They are taking God's seat.","The second is planning without God. The merchants of verse 13 are not condemned for making business plans, but for making them as if they were in charge of time, health and success. “Whereas you don’t know what your life will be like tomorrow. For what is your life? For you are a vapor, that appears for a little time, and then vanishes away” (v. 14). Jesus told the same story of the rich fool: “‘You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you’” (Luke 12:20). The remedy is not to stop planning but to plan humbly: “If the Lord wills, we will both live, and do this or that” (v. 15).","The chapter ends with a single verse that sums up the whole letter: “To him therefore who knows to do good, and doesn’t do it, to him it is sin” (v. 17). James will not let you hide behind knowledge. If you know what is right, the only question left is whether you will do it."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Grace to the Humble and the Devil in Flight","paragraphs":["Peter quotes the same Proverb as James, with the same application: “for ‘God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble’” (1 Peter 5:5), and then, “Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time” (1 Peter 5:6). Like James, he follows it immediately with spiritual warfare: “Your adversary, the devil, walks around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour” (1 Peter 5:8), “Withstand him steadfast in your faith” (1 Peter 5:9). Two apostles, the same order: humble yourself before God, then stand against the devil.","Jesus showed what that looks like in the wilderness. Every temptation was met with submission to God's word, and the final one with a command: “Get behind me, Satan! For it is written, ‘You shall worship the Lord your God, and you shall serve him only’” (Matthew 4:10). And the result was exactly what James promises: “Then the devil left him, and behold, angels came and served him” (Matthew 4:11). Paul gives the armour for the same fight: “Put on the whole armor of God, that you may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil” (Ephesians 6:11).","Jesus' teaching on humility runs through the chapter. In the parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector, the one who prayed “God, be merciful to me, a sinner!” went home justified, “for everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, but he who humbles himself will be exalted” (Luke 18:14). And in Gethsemane He prayed the perfect ‘If the Lord wills’: “Father, if you are willing, remove this cup from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but yours, be done” (Luke 22:42). The humble submission James calls for is the path Jesus walked first."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Pleasures","hebrew":"ἡδονή","translit":"hēdonḗ","strongs":"G2237","verses":"v. 1, 3","insight":"Strong's: “sensual delight; by implication, desire.” ἡδονή is pleasure as a ruling principle — the drive to have what feels good, the root of our word ‘hedonism’. Jesus used it of the ‘pleasures of life’ that choke the word in the parable of the sower (Luke 8:14). In James it is pictured as an army camped in your body, waging war. Pleasure in itself is God's gift; pleasure as master is a tyrant that will eventually turn you against God and other people."},{"english":"Friendship","hebrew":"φιλία","translit":"philía","strongs":"G5373 (friend φίλος, phílos, G5384)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “fondness.” φιλία is warm, personal affection — the love of friends, from the love family. It is the only time the noun appears in the New Testament. James uses it deliberately: in 2:23 Abraham was called φίλος θεοῦ, the friend of God; here people are φίλοι τοῦ κόσμου, friends of the world. You can only have one best friend. The question is not whether you are religious, but whose friendship you are cultivating — whose approval, values and company you most want."},{"english":"Resists","hebrew":"ἀντιτάσσομαι","translit":"antitássomai","strongs":"G498 (resist ἀνθίστημι, anthístēmi, G436)","verses":"v. 6, 7; 5:6","insight":"Strong's: “to range oneself against, i.e. oppose.” ἀντιτάσσομαι was used of an army drawn up in battle formation. James says God takes up that position against the proud. In the next verse he uses a different word, ἀνθίστημι, ‘to stand against’, for believers resisting the devil. This study gathers them into a new resist family. The two verses belong together: if you are proud, you are fighting God; if you are humble, God fights for you, and you can stand against the devil."},{"english":"Draw near","hebrew":"ἐγγίζω","translit":"engízō","strongs":"G1448","verses":"v. 8; 5:8","insight":"Strong's: “to make near, i.e. (reflexively) approach.” In the Greek Old Testament ἐγγίζω described priests approaching God in worship. Hebrews uses it of the better hope ‘through which we draw near to God’ (Hebrews 7:19). In James 4:8 every believer is invited to come as a priest — and God promises to come towards them. In 5:8 the same word announces that ‘the coming of the Lord is at hand.’ We draw near to Him now; He draws near to us at His return."},{"english":"Vapor","hebrew":"ἀτμίς","translit":"atmís","strongs":"G822","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “mist.” ἀτμίς is a puff of smoke or steam, the kind of vapour that rises from a cooking pot or a breath on a cold day — visible for a moment and then gone. The Hebrew idea behind it is הֶבֶל, the ‘vanity’ or ‘breath’ of Ecclesiastes. James does not use it to make life meaningless, but to make it precious and humble. Because your life is a vapour, every day is a gift to be placed in God's hands with the words ‘If the Lord wills.’"}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is calling you home — from fighting, from worldliness, from pride and from self-reliance — into His jealous love.","items":[{"title":"What Are You Fighting For?","body":"Think of the last conflict you were in — at home, at work, at church. The Spirit asks: what did you want that you were not getting? Recognition? Control? Comfort? Being right? “Where do wars and fightings among you come from?” (v. 1). And He asks a second question: did you pray about it, or only fight about it? “You don’t have, because you don’t ask” (v. 2). Take that desire to God honestly now."},{"title":"Whose Friend Are You?","body":"The Spirit does not accuse you to push you away; He yearns jealously to draw you back. Where has friendship with the world crept in — in your entertainment, your ambitions, your spending, your values, your need for approval? “Whoever therefore wants to be a friend of the world makes himself an enemy of God” (v. 4). He is not asking you to leave the world's people, but to stop loving the world's ways. And He promises, “But he gives more grace” (v. 6)."},{"title":"If the Lord Wills","body":"What plans are you making right now — for your career, your money, your family, your ministry? Have you included God in them, or only asked Him to bless what you have already decided? “For you are a vapor, that appears for a little time, and then vanishes away” (v. 14). The Spirit invites you to open your hands and say, honestly, “If the Lord wills” (v. 15). What would change in your plans if you meant it?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Desire Check","body":"What do you want so badly that it is causing conflict in your relationships (vv. 1–2)? When did you last bring that desire to God and ask Him about it — with right motives (v. 3)?"},{"title":"The Friendship Check","body":"Whose approval do you crave most? What would you be unwilling to give up if God asked? Are there areas where you are a ‘friend of the world’ (v. 4)?"},{"title":"The Knowing-Doing Check","body":"What good thing do you know you should do, but have not done (v. 17)? Name it specifically — a call, an apology, a gift, a change, an act of obedience."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Ten Steps Home","body":"Set aside thirty minutes this week. Read verses 7–10 slowly and turn each of the ten commands into a prayer: submit (name an area you are surrendering), resist (name a temptation and refuse it in Jesus' name), draw near (spend five minutes in silent worship), cleanse your hands (confess a specific action), purify your heart (confess a specific desire), lament and mourn (let yourself feel the weight of your sin), humble yourself (kneel if you can). End by thanking God that He draws near and lifts up the humble."},{"title":"Write ‘If the Lord Wills’ on Your Plans","body":"Take your diary, calendar or planning list for the coming month. Next to each major plan, write ‘If the Lord wills’ — and pray over each one, asking God to change, confirm or cancel it as He chooses. Notice which plans you feel anxious about surrendering; those are the places where you are trusting yourself more than God."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Holy God, I confess that the wars outside me often begin with the wars inside me. My desires fight for what I want, and I grab instead of asking You, or I ask with wrong motives. Forgive me. I confess the places where I have been a friend of the world and so made myself Your enemy. Thank You that You love me with a jealous love and that You give more grace. I humble myself before You now, because You oppose the proud and give grace to the humble. I submit to You. In Jesus' name I resist the devil, and I believe Your word that he will flee from me. I draw near to You, Lord, and I thank You that You draw near to me. Cleanse my hands and purify my heart; make me single-minded. Let me grieve over my sin until it is gone, and then lift me up in Your time. Keep me from speaking against my brothers and sisters and from sitting in Your judgement seat. My life is a vapour, so I place my plans in Your hands: if You will, I will live and do this or that. Whatever good I know to do, give me grace to do it today. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Jesus in the Wilderness","ref":"Matthew 4:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 7","summary":"After His baptism, Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness, where He fasted forty days. The devil came with three temptations: to turn stones into bread, to throw Himself from the temple, and to gain all the kingdoms of the world by worshipping him. Each time Jesus answered with Scripture, submitting Himself to His Father's word. At last He commanded, “Get behind me, Satan!” (Matthew 4:10), and “Then the devil left him, and behold, angels came and served him” (Matthew 4:11). This is James 4:7 lived out: submit to God, resist the devil, and he will flee."},{"title":"The Pharisee and the Tax Collector","ref":"Luke 18:9-14","relatedVerses":"v. 6, 9-10","summary":"Jesus told of two men who went to the temple to pray. The Pharisee stood and thanked God that he was not like other people, listing his fasting and tithing. The tax collector stood far off, would not even look up to heaven, beat his chest and prayed, “God, be merciful to me, a sinner!” (Luke 18:13). Jesus said the tax collector, not the Pharisee, went home justified, “for everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, but he who humbles himself will be exalted” (Luke 18:14). The tax collector is the one who lamented, mourned and humbled himself — and received the grace God gives to the humble."},{"title":"The Rich Fool","ref":"Luke 12:13-21","relatedVerses":"v. 13-16","summary":"A man's land produced a great harvest, and he planned to pull down his barns and build bigger ones. He said to himself, “Soul, you have many goods laid up for many years. Take your ease, eat, drink, be merry” (Luke 12:19). “But God said to him, ‘You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you. The things which you have prepared—whose will they be?’” (Luke 12:20). He made every plan except the one that mattered. James's merchants are the same man: they know where they will go and what they will earn, but they do not know whether they will be alive tomorrow."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"James","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Riches That Rot, Patience That Waits, and Prayer That Works","themeTitle":"“The Insistent Prayer of a Righteous Person Is Powerfully Effective”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Come now, you rich, weep and howl for your miseries that are coming on you.","understanding":"ἄγε νῦν οἱ πλούσιοι, “Come now, you rich” — the same sharp call as 4:13. These are not the rich believers of 1:10 but rich oppressors outside the church, addressed like the prophets addressed the nations. κλαύσατε ὀλολύζοντες, “weep and howl” — ὀλολύζω, to shriek, a sound word used in the prophets for people facing God's judgement. ταλαιπωρίαι, “miseries” — the noun of the ‘lament’ of 4:9. In 4:9 sinners were called to grieve now in repentance; here the rich are told that grief is coming upon them whether they repent or not.","words":[{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weep"},{"strongs":"G3649","hebrew":"ὀλολύζω","translit":"ololýzō","english":"howl"},{"strongs":"G5004","hebrew":"ταλαιπωρία","translit":"talaipōría","english":"miseries"},{"strongs":"G1904","hebrew":"ἐπέρχομαι","translit":"epérchomai","english":"coming"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Your riches are corrupted and your garments are moth-eaten.","understanding":"ὁ πλοῦτος ὑμῶν σέσηπεν, “Your riches are corrupted” — σήπω, to rot, as stored grain or food rots. καὶ τὰ ἱμάτια ὑμῶν σητόβρωτα γέγονεν, “and your garments are moth-eaten” — fine clothing was a form of wealth, hoarded in chests. The perfect tenses speak as if the judgement has already happened. Jesus said, “Don’t lay up treasures for yourselves on the earth, where moth and rust consume” (Matthew 6:19).","words":[{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"riches"},{"strongs":"G4595","hebrew":"σήπω","translit":"sḗpō","english":"corrupted"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garments"},{"strongs":"G4598","hebrew":"σητόβρωτος","translit":"sētóbrōtos","english":"moth-eaten"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Your gold and your silver are corroded, and their corrosion will be for a testimony against you, and will eat your flesh like fire. You have laid up your treasure in the last days.","understanding":"“Your gold and your silver are corroded” — κατιόω, to rust through. Gold does not rust, which shows James's point is moral, not chemical: hoarded wealth decays in God's sight. ὁ ἰὸς αὐτῶν, “their corrosion” — ἰός, the same word as the ‘poison’ of the tongue (3:8). It will be εἰς μαρτύριον, “for a testimony against you” — evidence in court — and it “will eat your flesh like fire” — σάρξ, the body. ἐθησαυρίσατε ἐν ἐσχάταις ἡμέραις, “You have laid up your treasure in the last days” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: the last days began with Christ's coming; to hoard in them is to stock a house on the eve of the flood. μαρτύριον is from the witness family — testimony as evidence or proof; used of the Law kept in the tabernacle.","words":[{"strongs":"G5557","hebrew":"χρυσός","translit":"chrysós","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G696","hebrew":"ἄργυρος","translit":"árgyros","english":"silver"},{"strongs":"G2728","hebrew":"κατιόω","translit":"katióō","english":"corroded"},{"strongs":"G2447","hebrew":"ἰός","translit":"iós","english":"corrosion"},{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2343","hebrew":"θησαυρίζω","translit":"thēsaurízō","english":"laid up your treasure"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Behold, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields, which you have kept back by fraud, cry out, and the cries of those who reaped have entered into the ears of the Lord of Armies.","understanding":"“Behold, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields” — μισθός, pay; ἐργάτης, from the work family: the labourer, the day-worker who lived on each day's pay (Leviticus 19:13; Deuteronomy 24:14–15). ὁ ἀπεστερημένος, “which you have kept back by fraud” — ἀποστερέω, to rob, to defraud. The withheld wages κράζει, “cry out,” like Abel's blood from the ground. “and the cries of those who reaped have entered into the ears of the Lord of Armies” — κυρίου σαβαώθ, the Hebrew title יְהוָה צְבָאוֹת, ‘Yahweh of hosts’, from the names of God: the Commander of heaven's armies hears the cry of the field hand.","words":[{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"wages"},{"strongs":"G2040","hebrew":"ἐργάτης","translit":"ergátēs","english":"laborers"},{"strongs":"G270","hebrew":"ἀμάω","translit":"amáō","english":"mowed"},{"strongs":"G5561","hebrew":"χώρα","translit":"chṓra","english":"fields"},{"strongs":"G650","hebrew":"ἀποστερέω","translit":"aposteréō","english":"kept back by fraud"},{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cry out"},{"strongs":"G995","hebrew":"βοή","translit":"boḗ","english":"cries"},{"strongs":"G2325","hebrew":"θερίζω","translit":"therízō","english":"reaped"},{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ears"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4519","hebrew":"σαβαώθ","translit":"sabaṓth","english":"Armies"}]},{"v":5,"text":"You have lived in luxury on the earth, and taken your pleasure. You have nourished your hearts as in a day of slaughter.","understanding":"ἐτρυφήσατε ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς καὶ ἐσπαταλήσατε, “You have lived in luxury on the earth, and taken your pleasure” — τρυφάω, soft living; σπαταλάω, self-indulgence, the word Paul uses of the widow “who gives herself to pleasure” (1 Timothy 5:6). γῆ, from the world family: the physical earth, as opposed to heaven. “You have nourished your hearts as in a day of slaughter” — fattening themselves like cattle on the very day they are to be butchered. ἡμέρα, from the time family: ἡμέρα σφαγῆς, the day of judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G5171","hebrew":"τρυφάω","translit":"trypháō","english":"lived in luxury"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4684","hebrew":"σπαταλάω","translit":"spataláō","english":"taken your pleasure"},{"strongs":"G5142","hebrew":"τρέφω","translit":"tréphō","english":"nourished"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G4967","hebrew":"σφαγή","translit":"sphagḗ","english":"slaughter"}]},{"v":6,"text":"You have condemned, you have murdered the righteous one. He doesn’t resist you.","understanding":"κατεδικάσατε, ἐφονεύσατε τὸν δίκαιον, “You have condemned, you have murdered the righteous one” — καταδικάζω, to pass sentence against, using the courts (2:6). τὸν δίκαιον is singular: most likely the righteous poor man as a type, whose life is taken by starving wages and unjust courts; it also echoes Christ, the Righteous One condemned and killed (Acts 7:52). οὐκ ἀντιτάσσεται ὑμῖν, “He doesn’t resist you” — ἀντιτάσσομαι, from the resist family, the word used of God resisting the proud (4:6). The righteous poor do not fight back; but God resists those who crush them. Tradition says James himself, called ‘the Just’, was later killed in Jerusalem.","words":[{"strongs":"G2613","hebrew":"καταδικάζω","translit":"katadikázō","english":"condemned"},{"strongs":"G5407","hebrew":"φονεύω","translit":"phoneúō","english":"murdered"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous one"},{"strongs":"G498","hebrew":"ἀντιτάσσομαι","translit":"antitássomai","english":"resist"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Be patient therefore, brothers, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, being patient over it, until it receives the early and late rain.","understanding":"μακροθυμήσατε οὖν, ἀδελφοί, “Be patient therefore, brothers” — μακροθυμέω, from the endurance family: to be long-tempered, slow to give up, as distinct from ὑπομονή, endurance under a load (v. 11). ἕως τῆς παρουσίας τοῦ κυρίου, “until the coming of the Lord” — παρουσία, the arrival, the royal visit: Christ's return. Then the farmer: ὁ γεωργὸς ἐκδέχεται τὸν τίμιον καρπὸν τῆς γῆς, “the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family, here is the soil. μακροθυμῶν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ, “being patient over it,” “until it receives the early and late rain” — πρόϊμος, the autumn rain at sowing, and ὄψιμος, the spring rain before harvest, the two rains God promised Israel.","words":[{"strongs":"G3114","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","english":"patient"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1092","hebrew":"γεωργός","translit":"geōrgós","english":"farmer"},{"strongs":"G1551","hebrew":"ἐκδέχομαι","translit":"ekdéchomai","english":"waits"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruit"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4406","hebrew":"πρώϊμος","translit":"prṓïmos","english":"early"},{"strongs":"G3797","hebrew":"ὄψιμος","translit":"ópsimos","english":"late"}]},{"v":8,"text":"You also be patient. Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord is at hand.","understanding":"μακροθυμήσατε καὶ ὑμεῖς, “You also be patient.” μακροθυμέω again, from the endurance family. στηρίξατε τὰς καρδίας ὑμῶν, “Establish your hearts” — στηρίζω, to fix firmly, to prop up, the opposite of the unstable, double-minded heart (1:8). “for the coming of the Lord is at hand” — ἤγγικεν, from ἐγγίζω, the ‘draw near’ of 4:8: we draw near to Him now; He is drawing near to us.","words":[{"strongs":"G3114","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","english":"be patient"},{"strongs":"G4741","hebrew":"στηρίζω","translit":"stērízō","english":"Establish"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G1448","hebrew":"ἐγγίζω","translit":"engízō","english":"at hand"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Don’t grumble, brothers, against one another, so that you won’t be judged. Behold, the judge stands at the door.","understanding":"μὴ στενάζετε, ἀδελφοί, κατ᾽ ἀλλήλων, “Don’t grumble, brothers, against one another” — στενάζω, to groan, to sigh with complaint. Pressure from outside often turns into irritation inside. “so that you won’t be judged” — κρίνω, as in 4:11–12. “Behold, the judge stands at the door” — κριτής, the Judge; θύρα, the door. He is close enough to hear the grumbling. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G4727","hebrew":"στενάζω","translit":"stenázō","english":"grumble"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G2923","hebrew":"κριτής","translit":"kritḗs","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G2374","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","english":"door"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Take, brothers, for an example of suffering and of perseverance, the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord.","understanding":"ὑπόδειγμα λάβετε, “Take, brothers, for an example” — ὑπόδειγμα, from the pattern family: a model to copy, the word Jesus used when He washed the disciples' feet (John 13:15). τῆς κακοπαθείας καὶ τῆς μακροθυμίας, “of suffering and of perseverance” — κακοπάθεια, from the suffer family: enduring hardship; μακροθυμία, from the endurance family: long-tempered patience. The WEB translates it ‘perseverance’, the same English it uses for ὑπομονή in the next verse. “the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord” — speaking for God brought them suffering, not comfort.","words":[{"strongs":"G5262","hebrew":"ὑπόδειγμα","translit":"hypódeigma","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G2552","hebrew":"κακοπάθεια","translit":"kakopátheia","english":"suffering"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Behold, we call them blessed who endured. You have heard of the perseverance of Job, and have seen the Lord in the outcome, and how the Lord is full of compassion and mercy.","understanding":"ἰδοὺ μακαρίζομεν τοὺς ὑπομείναντας, “Behold, we call them blessed who endured” — μακαρίζω, to pronounce blessed, as in 1:12; ὑπομένω, from the endurance family. “You have heard of the perseverance of Job” — ὑπομονή, endurance under a load, not μακροθυμία: Job was not always patient in his words, but he never let go of God. “and have seen the Lord in the outcome” — τὸ τέλος κυρίου, the end the Lord brought about; τέλος, from the perfect family: the goal, the ‘perfect work’ of 1:4. “and how the Lord is full of compassion and mercy” — two words from the mercy family: πολύσπλαγχνος, literally ‘many-bowelled’, deeply moved within, a word James may have coined; and οἰκτίρμων, tender pity.","words":[{"strongs":"G3106","hebrew":"μακαρίζω","translit":"makarízō","english":"call them blessed"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G2492","hebrew":"Ἰώβ","translit":"Iṓb","english":"Job"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"outcome"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4184","hebrew":"πολύσπλαγχνος","translit":"polýsplanchnos","english":"compassion"},{"strongs":"G3629","hebrew":"οἰκτίρμων","translit":"oiktírmōn","english":"mercy"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But above all things, my brothers, don’t swear— not by heaven, or by the earth, or by any other oath; but let your “yes” be “yes”, and your “no”, “no”; so that you don’t fall into hypocrisy.","understanding":"πρὸ πάντων δέ, “But above all things, my brothers, don’t swear” — ὀμνύω, to take an oath. James is repeating Jesus (Matthew 5:34–37) almost word for word. The issue is not formal oaths in court but everyday swearing ‘by heaven’ or ‘by the earth’ (γῆ, from the world family) to make your words sound more reliable — as if your ordinary words were not. ὅρκος, oath. “but let your ‘yes’ be ‘yes’, and your ‘no’, ‘no’” — be the kind of person whose word is enough. “so that you don’t fall into hypocrisy” — εἰς ὑπόκρισιν, the reading the WEB follows; many early manuscripts read ὑπὸ κρίσιν, ‘under judgement’. Both say that careless swearing carries a cost.","words":[{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swear"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3727","hebrew":"ὅρκος","translit":"hórkos","english":"oath"},{"strongs":"G5272","hebrew":"ὑπόκρισις","translit":"hypókrisis","english":"hypocrisy"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Is any among you suffering? Let him pray. Is any cheerful? Let him sing praises.","understanding":"κακοπαθεῖ τις ἐν ὑμῖν; προσευχέσθω, “Is any among you suffering? Let him pray.” κακοπαθέω, from the suffer family: to endure hardship, the verb of the ‘suffering’ of verse 10; προσεύχομαι, from the pray family: the general word for prayer as worship and communion with God. εὐθυμεῖ τις; ψαλλέτω, “Is any cheerful? Let him sing praises” — ψάλλω, originally to pluck strings, then to sing praise. In every state of life, turn to God.","words":[{"strongs":"G2553","hebrew":"κακοπαθέω","translit":"kakopathéō","english":"suffering"},{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G2114","hebrew":"εὐθυμέω","translit":"euthyméō","english":"cheerful"},{"strongs":"G5567","hebrew":"ψάλλω","translit":"psállō","english":"sing praises"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the assembly, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord,","understanding":"ἀσθενεῖ τις ἐν ὑμῖν, “Is any among you sick?” — ἀσθενέω, from the heal family: to be weak, without strength. “Let him call for the elders of the assembly” — πρεσβύτεροι, the recognised leaders of the ἐκκλησία. The sick person takes the initiative. “and let them pray over him” — προσεύχομαι, from the pray family. ἀλείψαντες ἐλαίῳ, “anointing him with oil” — ἀλείφω, the ordinary word for rubbing with oil, used for medicine (Luke 10:34) and for care; here it also marks the person as set apart to God. The disciples anointed the sick and healed them (Mark 6:13). “in the name of the Lord” — the power is in the Name, not the oil.","words":[{"strongs":"G770","hebrew":"ἀσθενέω","translit":"asthenéō","english":"sick"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G218","hebrew":"ἀλείφω","translit":"aleíphō","english":"anointing"},{"strongs":"G1637","hebrew":"ἔλαιον","translit":"élaion","english":"oil"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":15,"text":"and the prayer of faith will heal him who is sick, and the Lord will raise him up. If he has committed sins, he will be forgiven.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ εὐχὴ τῆς πίστεως σώσει τὸν κάμνοντα, “and the prayer of faith will heal him who is sick” — εὐχή, a second word from the pray family: a prayer, a vow, a wish offered to God. σῴζω, from the heal family, means to save or make whole; the WEB rightly translates it ‘heal’ here, but the word reaches beyond the body. κάμνω, a second word for ‘sick’: worn out, exhausted. καὶ ἐγερεῖ αὐτὸν ὁ κύριος, “and the Lord will raise him up” — ἐγείρω, from the rise family: raise from the sickbed, and ultimately from the grave. It is the Lord who raises, not the elders. “If he has committed sins, he will be forgiven” — ἁμαρτία, from the sin family; ἀφίημι, to release. Not all sickness is caused by sin, but some is, and God deals with both.","words":[{"strongs":"G2171","hebrew":"εὐχή","translit":"euchḗ","english":"prayer"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"heal"},{"strongs":"G2577","hebrew":"κάμνω","translit":"kámnō","english":"sick"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raise"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"forgiven"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Confess your offenses to one another, and pray for one another, that you may be healed. The insistent prayer of a righteous person is powerfully effective.","understanding":"ἐξομολογεῖσθε οὖν ἀλλήλοις τὰ παραπτώματα, “Confess your offenses to one another” — ἐξομολογέω, to agree openly, to say the same thing about your sin that God says. παράπτωμα, from the sin family: a false step, a falling aside — the WEB follows manuscripts that read this word; others read ἁμαρτίας, ‘sins’. καὶ εὔχεσθε ὑπὲρ ἀλλήλων, “and pray for one another” — εὔχομαι, the verb of εὐχή, from the pray family. “that you may be healed” — ἰάομαι, from the heal family: the physician's word for cure, of body and soul. Then the great sentence, with three family words: πολὺ ἰσχύει δέησις δικαίου ἐνεργουμένη, “The insistent prayer of a righteous person is powerfully effective.” δέησις, from the pray family: a specific petition born of need. ἐνεργέω, from the power family: to be at work, energetic — the WEB renders it ‘insistent’. ἰσχύω, also from the power family: to have strength, to prevail — ‘is powerfully effective’. Prayer is at work, and it is strong.","words":[{"strongs":"G1843","hebrew":"ἐξομολογέω","translit":"exomologéō","english":"Confess"},{"strongs":"G3900","hebrew":"παράπτωμα","translit":"paráptōma","english":"offenses"},{"strongs":"G2172","hebrew":"εὔχομαι","translit":"eúchomai","english":"pray"},{"strongs":"G2390","hebrew":"ἰάομαι","translit":"iáomai","english":"healed"},{"strongs":"G1754","hebrew":"ἐνεργέω","translit":"energéō","english":"insistent"},{"strongs":"G1162","hebrew":"δέησις","translit":"déēsis","english":"prayer"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous person"},{"strongs":"G2480","hebrew":"ἰσχύω","translit":"ischýō","english":"powerfully effective"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Elijah was a man with a nature like ours, and he prayed earnestly that it might not rain, and it didn’t rain on the earth for three years and six months.","understanding":"Ἠλίας ἄνθρωπος ἦν ὁμοιοπαθὴς ἡμῖν, “Elijah was a man with a nature like ours” — ὁμοιοπαθής, of like feelings and weaknesses; Paul used it of himself and Barnabas at Lystra (Acts 14:15). Elijah, the great prophet, was also the man who ran from Jezebel and asked to die. καὶ προσευχῇ προσηύξατο, “and he prayed earnestly” — literally ‘he prayed with prayer’, a Hebrew way of saying wholeheartedly; προσευχή and προσεύχομαι, from the pray family. “that it might not rain, and it didn’t rain on the earth for three years and six months” — γῆ, from the world family; ἐνιαυτός and μήν, from the time family; Jesus gives the same length of time (Luke 4:25).","words":[{"strongs":"G2243","hebrew":"Ἡλίας","translit":"Hēlías","english":"Elijah"},{"strongs":"G3663","hebrew":"ὁμοιοπαθής","translit":"homoiopathḗs","english":"with a nature like ours"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"earnestly"},{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"prayed"},{"strongs":"G1026","hebrew":"βρέχω","translit":"bréchō","english":"rain"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"years"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"months"}]},{"v":18,"text":"He prayed again, and the sky gave rain, and the earth produced its fruit.","understanding":"“He prayed again, and the sky gave rain, and the earth produced its fruit.” προσεύχομαι again, from the pray family; οὐρανός, the sky; ὑετός, rain; γῆ, from the world family: the earth; βλαστάνω, to sprout; καρπός, fruit — the harvest the farmer of verse 7 was waiting for. One man's prayer opened the heavens over a nation.","words":[{"strongs":"G4336","hebrew":"προσεύχομαι","translit":"proseúchomai","english":"prayed"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G5205","hebrew":"ὑετός","translit":"hyetós","english":"rain"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G985","hebrew":"βλαστάνω","translit":"blastánō","english":"produced"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruit"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Brothers, if any among you wanders from the truth and someone turns him back,","understanding":"“Brothers, if any among you wanders from the truth” — πλανάω, to wander off like a sheep, the ‘deceived’ of 1:16. The person is ‘among you’, a member of the church. ἀλήθεια, the word of truth of 1:18. “and someone turns him back” — ἐπιστρέφω, to turn around, the word for conversion and repentance. Anyone can do it; James says ‘someone’.","words":[{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"wanders"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G1994","hebrew":"ἐπιστρέφω","translit":"epistréphō","english":"turns him back"}]},{"v":20,"text":"let him know that he who turns a sinner from the error of his way will save a soul from death and will cover a multitude of sins.","understanding":"γινωσκέτω, “let him know” — γινώσκω, from the know family: to know by experience. ὁ ἐπιστρέψας ἁμαρτωλὸν ἐκ πλάνης ὁδοῦ αὐτοῦ, “that he who turns a sinner from the error of his way” — ἁμαρτωλός and ἁμαρτία, from the sin family; πλάνη, straying. σώσει ψυχὴν αὐτοῦ ἐκ θανάτου, “will save a soul from death” — σῴζω, from the heal family, as in verse 15; most likely the wanderer's soul, rescued from the death sin produces (1:15). ψυχή, from the life family. καὶ καλύψει πλῆθος ἁμαρτιῶν, “and will cover a multitude of sins” — from Proverbs 10:12, ‘love covers all wrongs’, which Peter also quotes. Sins are covered because they are forgiven when the sinner returns. The letter ends not with a farewell but with a mission.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1994","hebrew":"ἐπιστρέφω","translit":"epistréphō","english":"turns"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinner"},{"strongs":"G4106","hebrew":"πλάνη","translit":"plánē","english":"error"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"save"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2572","hebrew":"καλύπτω","translit":"kalýptō","english":"cover"},{"strongs":"G4128","hebrew":"πλῆθος","translit":"plēthos","english":"multitude"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Rotting Storehouse","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a rich man's storeroom. Sacks of grain are stacked to the ceiling, chests overflow with silk robes, and a locked box holds gold and silver coins. Years pass. The grain turns black and smells of rot. Moths have eaten holes through every robe. The coins are crusted and green. And outside the door, poor families have gone hungry the whole time.","meaning":"“Your riches are corrupted and your garments are moth-eaten” (v. 2). “Your gold and your silver are corroded, and their corrosion will be for a testimony against you” (v. 3). Wealth hoarded while others suffer does not simply sit idle; it becomes evidence. Jesus' alternative is clear: “but lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust consume” (Matthew 6:20)."},{"title":"The Cry from the Field","verseRef":"v. 4","image":"Picture a long line of labourers at the end of a hot day in the harvest field, hands blistered and backs aching, waiting for their pay. The landowner's steward tells them to come back next week. They go home with nothing for their children. Their voices rise in the evening air — and James says those voices do not stop at the sky.","meaning":"“Behold, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields, which you have kept back by fraud, cry out, and the cries of those who reaped have entered into the ears of the Lord of Armies” (v. 4). The Commander of heaven's armies listens to the cry of underpaid workers. How you pay, treat and honour those who work for you is a spiritual matter."},{"title":"The Farmer and the Rain","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture a farmer in ancient Israel. In the autumn he ploughs and sows, then watches the sky for the early rain that makes the seed sprout. All winter he waits. In the spring he watches again for the late rain that fills the grain. He cannot make the rain fall; he cannot hurry the harvest. He works, he waits, he watches, and he trusts.","meaning":"“Be patient therefore, brothers, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, being patient over it, until it receives the early and late rain” (v. 7). Patience is not passivity. The farmer works hard; he simply knows that the harvest is in God's hands and God's timing. “You also be patient. Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord is at hand” (v. 8)."},{"title":"The Man on Mount Carmel","verseRef":"v. 16-18","image":"Picture a prophet on the top of a mountain after three and a half years of drought, crouched on the ground with his face between his knees. Seven times he sends his servant to look towards the sea. Six times the servant returns: ‘There is nothing.’ The seventh time he says, ‘A small cloud, like a man's hand, is rising out of the sea.’ Within hours the sky is black and the rain is pouring down.","meaning":"“Elijah was a man with a nature like ours” (v. 17). “He prayed again, and the sky gave rain, and the earth produced its fruit” (v. 18). Elijah was not a superhuman. He had the same fears and weaknesses you have. What made the difference was that he prayed — earnestly, persistently, in faith. “The insistent prayer of a righteous person is powerfully effective” (v. 16)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Warning to the Oppressive Rich (v. 1-6)","body":"Weep, you rich, for coming miseries; your hoarded wealth has rotted and will testify against you; the withheld wages of your workers cry out to the Lord of Armies; you lived in luxury, fattened yourselves for the day of slaughter, and condemned and killed the righteous, who did not resist you."},{"label":"Patience Until the Lord Comes (v. 7-11)","body":"Be patient until the Lord's coming, like a farmer waiting for the early and late rains; strengthen your hearts, for His coming is near; do not grumble against each other, for the Judge stands at the door; take the prophets and Job as examples of endurance, and see the compassion and mercy of the Lord."},{"label":"Let Your Yes Be Yes (v. 12)","body":"Above all, do not swear by heaven, earth or any oath, but let your yes be yes and your no be no."},{"label":"The Praying Community (v. 13-18)","body":"The suffering should pray and the cheerful sing; the sick should call the elders to pray and anoint them in the Lord's name, and the prayer of faith will save and the Lord will raise them up, with sins forgiven; confess sins and pray for one another, for the prayer of a righteous person is powerful, as Elijah's prayers for drought and rain show."},{"label":"Turning Back the Wanderer (v. 19-20)","body":"Whoever turns a wandering brother back from error saves a soul from death and covers a multitude of sins."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Wealth, Justice and the Lord of Armies","paragraphs":["The rich men of verses 1–6 are probably not believers. James addresses them as the Old Testament prophets addressed foreign nations, so that God's people overhear the warning and take comfort. Their sins are specific: hoarding wealth (vv. 2–3), withholding wages (v. 4), living in self-indulgence (v. 5), and using their power to condemn and destroy the innocent (v. 6). These are the same people who “oppress you, and personally drag you before the courts” (James 2:6).","James does not say that wealth itself is evil. He condemns wealth gained by fraud, kept from the poor and spent on luxury while others suffer. Jesus said, “But woe to you who are rich! For you have received your consolation” (Luke 6:24). The Law commanded that a day-labourer be paid before sunset, because he depended on that day's wage to feed his family (Deuteronomy 24:14–15). To withhold it was to steal his family's dinner. James says those withheld wages are crying out, and the cry has reached “the ears of the Lord of Armies” (v. 4).","That title is deliberate. ‘Lord of Armies’ (in Hebrew, יְהוָה צְבָאוֹת) is the name of God as the Commander of heaven's hosts, the warrior King. The poor labourer may have no lawyer, no union and no voice in court, but he has the Lord of Armies listening. For believers who are oppressed, this is comfort: God sees, and He will judge. For believers who employ, buy, sell and invest, it is a warning: how we treat the people who work for us is a matter of faith."]},{"heading":"Patience Like a Farmer","paragraphs":["To the oppressed believers James says, “Be patient therefore, brothers, until the coming of the Lord” (v. 7). The answer to injustice is not revenge or despair but patient endurance, grounded in the certainty that Christ is coming. The word used for His coming, παρουσία, was used for the arrival of a king. Paul uses it the same way: “we who are alive, who are left until the coming of the Lord” (1 Thessalonians 4:15).","The farmer is James's picture of patience. He cannot control the rain, but he does not sit idle; he ploughs, sows and tends, and he waits with confidence. James adds a practical warning: “Don’t grumble, brothers, against one another, so that you won’t be judged. Behold, the judge stands at the door” (v. 9). Suffering often makes people irritable with each other. James says the Judge is so close He can hear it. Jesus said the same of His coming: “Even so you also, when you see all these things, know that it is near, even at the doors” (Matthew 24:33).","Then come the examples. The prophets suffered for speaking God's word (v. 10). And “You have heard of the perseverance of Job, and have seen the Lord in the outcome, and how the Lord is full of compassion and mercy” (v. 11). Job is an honest example. He questioned, protested and wished he had never been born. But he never let go of God, and in the end God restored him. That is ὑπομονή — not silent calm, but refusal to give up on God. And the end of Job's story reveals God's heart: “full of compassion and mercy.” Whatever you are suffering, the final chapter will show the same."]},{"heading":"A Community That Prays","paragraphs":["The letter ends with prayer — fitting for a man remembered for his praying knees. James covers every situation: “Is any among you suffering? Let him pray. Is any cheerful? Let him sing praises” (v. 13). Then sickness: “Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the assembly, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord” (v. 14). The oil was both a common medicine (Luke 10:34) and a sign of setting someone apart for God's care; the power was in “the name of the Lord.” The promise is strong: “and the prayer of faith will heal him who is sick, and the Lord will raise him up” (v. 15). Honesty requires one sentence: Scripture shows that God does not always heal in this life (Paul left Trophimus sick at Miletus, 2 Timothy 4:20), but James tells the church to pray in faith, expecting God to act, and every believer will finally be raised up in the resurrection.","Sickness and sin are sometimes linked: “If he has committed sins, he will be forgiven” (v. 15). So James calls for honesty in the community: “Confess your offenses to one another, and pray for one another, that you may be healed” (v. 16). This is not a priest hearing confession but believers being honest with one another. John promises, “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness” (1 John 1:9). Confession to God brings forgiveness; confession to one another brings healing, accountability and support.","Then the great encouragement: “The insistent prayer of a righteous person is powerfully effective” (v. 16). Two power-family words make the point — prayer is at work (ἐνεργέω), and it has strength (ἰσχύω). And James's example is Elijah, “a man with a nature like ours” (v. 17). His prayers shut the sky for three and a half years and opened it again (vv. 17–18). The ‘righteous person’ is not a spiritual celebrity but an ordinary believer who is right with God and prays in faith. Jesus told His disciples “that they must always pray, and not give up” (Luke 18:1)."]},{"heading":"The Last Word: Go After the Wanderer","paragraphs":["James closes without greetings or blessing. His last words are a commission: “Brothers, if any among you wanders from the truth and someone turns him back, let him know that he who turns a sinner from the error of his way will save a soul from death and will cover a multitude of sins” (vv. 19–20). After five chapters on what real faith looks like, he leaves us with what real love does: it goes after the one who has drifted.","The word for ‘wanders’ is the same as ‘Don’t be deceived’ (James 1:16). The whole letter has warned against wandering — into doubt, temptation, favouritism, dead faith, an untamed tongue, worldly wisdom, pride and greed. Now James asks every believer to become a shepherd. Jesus said, “If your brother sins against you, go, show him his fault between you and him alone. If he listens to you, you have gained back your brother” (Matthew 18:15). Paul says, “Brothers, even if a man is caught in some fault, you who are spiritual must restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness” (Galatians 6:1).","The result is twofold: a soul saved from death, and a multitude of sins covered. Peter quotes the same Proverb: “And above all things be earnest in your love among yourselves, for love covers a multitude of sins” (1 Peter 4:8). The letter that began with trials and endurance ends with love that will not let a brother go. That is faith that works."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Coming Lord, Rust and Moth, and Elijah's Prayer","paragraphs":["James 5 is full of Jesus' words. The rotting riches echo the Sermon on the Mount: “Don’t lay up treasures for yourselves on the earth, where moth and rust consume, and where thieves break through and steal” (Matthew 6:19). The command about oaths repeats it almost exactly: “but I tell you, don’t swear at all: neither by heaven, for it is the throne of God” (Matthew 5:34), “nor by the earth, for it is the footstool of his feet” (Matthew 5:35), and “But let your ‘Yes’ be ‘Yes’ and your ‘No’ be ‘No.’ Whatever is more than these is of the evil one” (Matthew 5:37). The Judge at the door recalls the risen Christ's words: “Behold, I stand at the door and knock” (Revelation 3:20).","Elijah's drought is mentioned by Jesus too: “there were many widows in Israel in the days of Elijah, when the sky was shut up three years and six months, when a great famine came over all the land” (Luke 4:25). And in Revelation the two witnesses carry Elijah's authority: “These have the power to shut up the sky, that it may not rain during the days of their prophecy” (Revelation 11:6). James's point is that the same God who answered Elijah answers the prayers of His people today.","The patience of verses 7–8 looks forward to the final promise of the Bible: “He who testifies these things says, ‘Yes, I come quickly.’ Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (Revelation 22:20). Until then, believers wait like the farmer, pray like Elijah, endure like Job, and go after the wanderer — faith that works while it waits."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Coming","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","strongs":"G3952","verses":"v. 7, 8","insight":"Strong's: “a being near, i.e. advent (often, return; specially, of Christ to punish Jerusalem, or finally the wicked).” In ordinary Greek παρουσία meant ‘presence’ or ‘arrival’, and it was used for the official visit of a king or emperor to a city — with preparations, celebration and judgement. The New Testament uses it for Christ's return (1 Corinthians 15:23; 1 Thessalonians 4:15). James uses it to strengthen suffering believers: the King is coming, He will put things right, and He is near. Patience is not waiting for nothing; it is waiting for Someone."},{"english":"Be patient","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","strongs":"G3114 (endurance ὑπομονή, hypomonḗ, G5281)","verses":"v. 7, 8, 10; 1:3, 4; 5:11","insight":"Strong's: “to be long-spirited, i.e. (objectively) forbearing or (subjectively) patient.” μακροθυμία is ‘long temper’ — the opposite of a short fuse. It is usually patience with people and with God's timing, while ὑπομονή (v. 11) is endurance under hard circumstances. James uses both. The farmer needs μακροθυμία, waiting for the rain without despair; Job showed ὑπομονή, holding on through suffering. The WEB translates both nouns as ‘perseverance’ in verses 10–11, so the endurance family in this study shows which is which."},{"english":"Full of compassion","hebrew":"πολύσπλαγχνος","translit":"polýsplanchnos","strongs":"G4184 (merciful οἰκτίρμων, oiktírmōn, G3629)","verses":"v. 11","insight":"Strong's: “extremely compassionate.” The word is built from πολύς (much) and σπλάγχνα (the inward parts, where the ancients felt deep emotion) — literally ‘many-bowelled’. It is found nowhere earlier in Greek literature, and James may have coined it to describe how deeply the Lord feels for His suffering people. Beside it is οἰκτίρμων, ‘compassionate, merciful’, the tender pity of a father. This study gathers both into a new mercy family. The end of Job's story is not a lesson about suffering but a revelation of God's heart."},{"english":"Prayer","hebrew":"εὐχή","translit":"euchḗ","strongs":"G2171 (petition δέησις, déēsis, G1162; prayer προσευχή, proseuchḗ, G4335)","verses":"v. 15, 16, 17","insight":"Strong's: “properly, a wish, expressed as a petition to God, or in votive obligation.” James uses three different nouns for prayer in four verses. εὐχή (v. 15) is a prayer or vow — ‘the prayer of faith’. δέησις (v. 16) is a petition, a specific request born out of need. προσευχή (v. 17) is the general word for prayer as communion with God. All three are in the pray family. Together they show that prayer is many things — faith, need and fellowship — and that every kind is effective in the mouth of a righteous person."},{"english":"Turns back","hebrew":"ἐπιστρέφω","translit":"epistréphō","strongs":"G1994","verses":"v. 19, 20","insight":"Strong's: “to revert (literally, figuratively or morally).” ἐπιστρέφω is the New Testament word for conversion: turning around, turning back to God. Jesus told Peter, “You, when once you have turned again, establish your brothers” (Luke 22:32). James gives every believer a share in that ministry. To turn someone back is not to condemn them, but to walk after them, as a shepherd goes after a lost sheep, until they are home."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this final chapter the Holy Spirit gathers the whole letter into three callings: be just, be patient, and pray.","items":[{"title":"Is Your Money Testifying for You or Against You?","body":"The Spirit asks you to look honestly at your money — what you earn, what you keep, what you spend, and how you treat those who work for you. Are you paying fairly and promptly? Are you storing up while others go without? “Your gold and your silver are corroded, and their corrosion will be for a testimony against you” (v. 3). What would your bank statement say about your faith? Ask Him where He wants you to be generous or just this week."},{"title":"The Judge Is at the Door","body":"Waiting for God can make you bitter with God or with others. “Don’t grumble, brothers, against one another, so that you won’t be judged. Behold, the judge stands at the door” (v. 9). The Spirit wants to replace grumbling with patient trust. Where have you been complaining about people or circumstances while you wait for God to act? Hand that situation back to Him, and establish your heart in His coming."},{"title":"Pray Like Elijah","body":"Elijah was “a man with a nature like ours” (v. 17). The Spirit reminds you that the power of prayer does not depend on being a spiritual hero but on being right with God and praying in faith. Who is sick, suffering or wandering in your life? Who could you confess to and pray with? Is there something you have stopped praying about because nothing seemed to happen? Like Elijah sending his servant seven times, pray again."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Wages Check","body":"Do you pay people fairly and on time — employees, contractors, cleaners, tradespeople, market sellers (v. 4)? Have you ever held back what you owed? Put it right this week."},{"title":"The Grumbling Check","body":"Who have you been grumbling about or against while you wait for something to change (v. 9)? Would you say those words if you saw the Judge standing at the door?"},{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"When you are suffering, is prayer your first response or your last resort (v. 13)? Do you have anyone you can confess to and pray with honestly (v. 16)? Is there someone wandering from the truth you have stopped reaching out to (v. 19)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Find a Confession and Prayer Partner","body":"This month, ask one mature believer of the same sex whom you trust to meet with you once a week, or call you, for honest confession and prayer (v. 16). Begin with a short time: share one struggle, one sin and one need each; pray for each other by name. Keep it confidential. Watch what God does when you stop hiding."},{"title":"Go After One Wanderer","body":"Think of one person who used to walk with God and has drifted away (vv. 19–20). This week, contact them — not to lecture, but to love. Invite them for coffee or a meal, ask how they really are, listen, and if the door opens, gently point them back to Christ. Pray for them daily, like Elijah, until God answers."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord of Armies, You hear the cry of the poor labourer and the suffering widow. Search my heart and my money. Where I have hoarded, make me generous; where I have been unjust, make me put it right; where I have lived in luxury while others lacked, forgive me. Teach me to lay up treasure in heaven, where moth and rust do not destroy. Give me the patience of the farmer, who works and waits for the early and late rain. Establish my heart, because the coming of the Lord is near. Keep me from grumbling against my brothers and sisters, for the Judge stands at the door. When I suffer, let me pray; when I am cheerful, let me sing praise; when I am sick, let me call for prayer and trust You to raise me up. Give me the courage to confess my sins to others and to pray for them, so that we may be healed. Make me a righteous person whose prayers are powerful and effective, like Elijah, a man with a nature like mine. Thank You that You are full of compassion and mercy, as You were to Job. And send me after those who have wandered from the truth, to turn them back and see them saved. Let my faith work until the day I see You face to face. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Elijah Shuts and Opens the Sky","ref":"1 Kings 17:1; 18:41-45","relatedVerses":"v. 17-18","summary":"Elijah stood before the wicked King Ahab and declared, ‘As Yahweh, the God of Israel, lives, before whom I stand, there shall not be dew nor rain these years, but according to my word.’ The rain stopped. After three and a half years and the great contest on Mount Carmel, Elijah climbed to the top of the mountain, bowed to the ground and put his face between his knees. Seven times he sent his servant to look towards the sea, until a cloud ‘as small as a man's hand’ appeared, and soon the sky grew black with wind and heavy rain. James says Elijah had a nature like ours — the same man later ran from Jezebel in fear — yet his persistent prayers moved heaven."},{"title":"Job's Endurance and the Lord's Mercy","ref":"Job 1:1-2:10; 42:1-17","relatedVerses":"v. 10-11","summary":"Job was a righteous man who lost his children, his wealth and his health in a few days. His wife told him to curse God and die; his friends blamed him for his suffering. Job wept, argued, questioned and longed for an answer, but he did not let go of God: ‘Though he slay me, yet will I wait for him.’ At the end God spoke to him out of the whirlwind, Job humbled himself, and the Lord restored his fortunes and blessed the latter part of his life more than the beginning. James draws the lesson: we have “seen the Lord in the outcome, and how the Lord is full of compassion and mercy” (v. 11)."},{"title":"Peter Turned Back","ref":"Luke 22:31-34, 54-62; John 21:15-19","relatedVerses":"v. 19-20","summary":"On the night before the cross, Jesus told Peter, “Simon, Simon, behold, Satan asked to have all of you, that he might sift you as wheat” (Luke 22:31), “but I prayed for you, that your faith wouldn’t fail. You, when once you have turned again, establish your brothers” (Luke 22:32). Peter denied Jesus three times that night. But after the resurrection Jesus came to him by the Sea of Galilee and asked him three times, ‘Do you love me?’, restoring him and giving him a mission. Peter was the wanderer whom Jesus turned back. Then Peter became the one who turned others back. James asks every believer to do for others what Jesus did for Peter."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"James","titleEmojis":"🌾🔥","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Faith That Works Is the Only Faith That Lives","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of James in one overarching thought, it is this: true faith is never alone — it endures trials, obeys the word, loves without favouritism, tames the tongue, chooses heavenly wisdom, humbles itself before God, waits patiently and prays, and anything less is a body without breath.","paragraphs":["James wrote to believers under pressure — scattered, poor, oppressed by the rich, tempted, quarrelling and tired. He did not give them a new doctrine. He held up a mirror. Five chapters of short, sharp sermons asked one question again and again: does your faith actually work? “But be doers of the word, and not only hearers, deluding your own selves” (James 1:22). “For as the body apart from the spirit is dead, even so faith apart from works is dead” (James 2:26).","The writer's name is a key to the letter. Ἰάκωβος is the Greek form of the Hebrew name Jacob (יַעֲקֹב), the father of the twelve tribes — and this James writes “to the twelve tribes which are in the Dispersion” (James 1:1). Jacob was the double-minded schemer who wrestled with God and came away limping and renamed. His namesake writes against double-mindedness and calls God's scattered people to a single heart: “Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you” (James 4:8).","The man himself is part of the message. He grew up with Jesus and did not believe in Him (John 7:5). Then the risen Christ appeared to him (1 Corinthians 15:7), and the sceptic became “a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ” (James 1:1), the praying leader of the Jerusalem church. When he writes, “Of his own will he gave birth to us by the word of truth” (James 1:18), he writes his own story. His faith was tested and proved — and tradition says he died for it, as the righteous one who did not resist.","James is not against grace. He opens with “Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above” (James 1:17), and at the centre of the letter is “But he gives more grace” (James 4:6). What James is against is a grace that never arrives — a faith that talks, sings and believes the right things but never feeds the hungry, controls the tongue or humbles itself. He shows what grace looks like when it lands on an ordinary life."]},"map":{"intro":"James is the twentieth book of the New Testament, the first of the letters written to the whole scattered church rather than to one congregation or person. It stands right after Hebrews and before the letters of Peter, John and Jude.","sections":[{"title":"Hebrews Taught Who Christ Is; James Teaches How to Walk","body":"Hebrews soared through the priesthood of Christ, the new covenant and the cloud of witnesses, and ended with practical commands. James begins where Hebrews ended. Hebrews told the readers, “For you need endurance” (Hebrews 10:36); James tells them how endurance grows. Hebrews 11 praised Abraham and Rahab for their faith; James 2 shows that the same faith was proved by works. Hebrews ended at the altar outside the camp; James ends at the sickbed, the confession and the wanderer's door. Doctrine and practice belong together, and the Holy Spirit placed them side by side."},{"title":"The Sermon on the Mount Written Down","body":"No New Testament letter is closer to the teaching of Jesus. Joy in trials (1:2; Matthew 5:12), asking and receiving (1:5; Matthew 7:7), hearing and doing (1:22; Matthew 7:24), the poor who are rich (2:5; Luke 6:20), mercy (2:13; Matthew 5:7), the tree known by its fruit (3:12; Matthew 7:16), peacemakers (3:18; Matthew 5:9), not judging (4:11; Matthew 7:1), treasure that rusts (5:2–3; Matthew 6:19) and the simple ‘yes’ and ‘no’ (5:12; Matthew 5:37). James rarely quotes his Brother directly, but almost every paragraph echoes Him."},{"title":"James and Paul: Two Voices, One Gospel","body":"James 2:24 and Romans 3:28 have been set against each other for centuries, and Luther once called James ‘an epistle of straw’. But the two apostles fight different errors. Paul shows that a sinner is declared righteous by faith, not by works of the law; James shows that the faith that justifies is a living faith that works. Paul's own summary is “faith working through love” (Galatians 5:6). The honest conclusion is the one the church has long held: we are saved by faith alone, but the faith that saves is never alone."},{"title":"A Feast of Word Families","body":"James added more word families to this study than any book except Hebrews. New families: test (πειρασμός the trial or temptation, δοκίμιον the proving, δόκιμος approved), endurance (ὑπομονή remaining under, μακροθυμία long temper — both called ‘perseverance’ in 5:10–11), gift (δόσις the giving, δώρημα the gift, 1:17), wisdom (σοφία, σοφός, ἐπιστήμων, φρόνησις, σύνεσις), humble (ταπεινός lowly, πραΰτης meek), resist (God ἀντιτάσσεται the proud, you ἀνθίστημι the devil, 4:6–7), heal (ἀσθενέω, κάμνω, σῴζω, ἰάομαι, 5:14–16) and mercy (ἔλεος, οἰκτίρμων, πολύσπλαγχνος). And James 5:16 holds three prayer words and two power words in one sentence."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: James ended with patient waiting for the Lord's coming, endurance like Job's and love that turns back the wanderer. The next two books are 1 Peter, a letter of hope for believers suffering as exiles, and 2 Peter, a call to grow in faith and stand firm against false teachers while waiting for the day of the Lord.","books":[{"book":"1 Peter","connection":"Peter writes to the same scattered people as James: “to the chosen ones who are living as foreigners in the Dispersion” (1 Peter 1:1). He picks up James's opening theme at once — trials that prove faith like gold in fire: “that the proof of your faith, which is more precious than gold that perishes even though it is tested by fire, may be found to result in praise, glory, and honor at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (1 Peter 1:7). He quotes the same Proverb James quoted (1 Peter 5:5) and calls believers to good works that the world can see: “having good behavior among the nations, so in that of which they speak against you as evildoers, they may by your good works, which they see, glorify God in the day of visitation” (1 Peter 2:12). Watch for how Peter roots suffering and good works in Christ's own sufferings and in “a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (1 Peter 1:3)."},{"book":"2 Peter","connection":"Peter's second letter is James's faith that works in a ladder: “in your faith supply moral excellence; and in moral excellence, knowledge” (2 Peter 1:5), “and in knowledge, self-control; and in self-control perseverance; and in perseverance godliness” (2 Peter 1:6), “and in godliness brotherly affection; and in brotherly affection, love” (2 Peter 1:7). The goal is the same as James's: “they make you to be not idle or unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 1:8) — ‘idle’ is ἀργός, the very word many manuscripts read in James 2:20. And Peter explains the patience of James 5: “The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (2 Peter 3:9). Watch for how Peter guards the church against false teachers while it waits."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"Trials Produce Endurance and Approval","anchorRef":"James 1:2-4, 12","body":"“Count it all joy, my brothers, when you fall into various temptations” (James 1:2), because “the testing of your faith produces endurance” (James 1:3). The same test can lead upward or downward: met with faith, it makes you approved and wins “the crown of life” (James 1:12); met with your own desire, it conceives sin and death (James 1:15). God never tempts anyone to sin, and He gives wisdom generously to all who ask in faith (James 1:5)."},{"title":"Faith Is Shown by Works","anchorRef":"James 1:22; 2:14-26","body":"Hearing without doing is self-deception (James 1:22). Faith that says ‘Go in peace’ to the hungry is dead (James 2:16–17). Even demons believe correctly (James 2:19). Abraham's faith “worked with his works, and by works faith was perfected” (James 2:22), and Rahab proved her faith by risking her life. Real faith loves without favouritism, fulfils the royal law of love and shows mercy, because “Mercy triumphs over judgment” (James 2:13)."},{"title":"The Tongue and the Two Wisdoms","anchorRef":"James 3:2-18","body":"The small tongue steers the whole life like a bit or a rudder and can burn like a forest fire (James 3:3–6). No human can tame it, but God can change the spring (James 3:8–12). True wisdom is shown by meek, good conduct, not by clever words: “But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy” (James 3:17)."},{"title":"Humility Receives Grace","anchorRef":"James 4:1-17","body":"Quarrels come from desires at war within (James 4:1). Friendship with the world is spiritual adultery, but God “gives more grace” (James 4:6). “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble” (James 4:6). The way back is surrender, resistance and nearness: “Draw near to God, and he will draw near to you” (James 4:8). Humility also means not judging others and not boasting about tomorrow, but saying, ‘If the Lord wills.’"},{"title":"Patient, Praying, Restoring Faith","anchorRef":"James 5:7-20","body":"Believers wait for the Lord's coming like a farmer for the rain (James 5:7), endure like Job and trust a Lord “full of compassion and mercy” (James 5:11). They pray in every situation, call for prayer in sickness, confess to one another and pray for one another, because “The insistent prayer of a righteous person is powerfully effective” (James 5:16). And they go after the wanderer, to save a soul from death (James 5:20)."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of James practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":"1","title":"Turn Every Trial Into a Prayer for Wisdom","lesson":"The Lesson: “But if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach; and it will be given to him” (James 1:5).","practice":"The Practice: For the next month, whenever a difficulty comes — large or small — stop and pray three sentences before you react: ‘Father, I count this as joy because You are producing endurance in me. I lack wisdom; please give it to me. I ask in faith, with a single heart.’ Keep a short journal of the trials and the wisdom God gives."},{"step":"2","title":"Make Your Faith Visible This Week","lesson":"The Lesson: “Show me your faith without works, and I by my works will show you my faith” (James 2:18).","practice":"The Practice: Choose three concrete acts this week: one for someone poor or overlooked (a meal, a gift, a visit), one for a ‘fatherless or widow’ (James 1:27), and one act of obedience you have been putting off. Do them quietly. At the end of the week, thank God that your faith has hands."},{"step":"3","title":"Guard Your Tongue and Pray for One Another","lesson":"The Lesson: “If anyone doesn’t stumble in word, the same is a perfect man, able to bridle the whole body also” (James 3:2), and “pray for one another, that you may be healed” (James 5:16).","practice":"The Practice: Every morning for thirty days, pray over your mouth (compare Psalm 141:3): ‘Lord, set a guard over my tongue today.’ Refuse gossip, grumbling and boasting. Then find one believer to meet or call weekly for honest confession and prayer, and together pray by name for one person who has wandered from the truth until they return."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father of lights, I close the letter of James, and I declare that every good gift and every perfect gift in my life comes from You, and You do not change. I will count my trials as joy, for You are using the testing of my faith to produce endurance, and You will make me complete, lacking nothing. When I lack wisdom, I will ask You, and You will give it generously. I will not blame You for my temptations; I will refuse the bait and receive the crown of life You have promised to those who love You. I will be a doer of Your word and not a hearer only. I will not hold my faith with partiality; I will honour the poor You have chosen and fulfil the royal law of love. My faith will not be a body without breath. Like Abraham, my faith will work with my works; like Rahab, I will act on what I believe. I give You my tongue — bridle it and make my words a spring of sweet water. I choose the wisdom from above: pure, peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits. I reject friendship with the world. I submit to You, I resist the devil and he must flee, I draw near to You and You draw near to me. I humble myself, and You will lift me up. My life is a vapour, so I say, ‘If the Lord wills.’ I will be just with my money and patient like the farmer, for the coming of the Lord is near. I will pray when I suffer, sing when I am glad, confess my sins and pray for others, for the prayer of a righteous person is powerful and effective. And I will go after the wanderer and turn them back. Let my faith work until I see You face to face. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
